2 TYPE Diary entry
2 DATE 1676
2 PLAC Lancaster Castle, Lancaster, Lancashire, Kingdom of England
2 NOTE During one of his imprisonments in Lancaster Castle, John Moon writes in his diary, “the Lord was with me in prison and made me more to rejoice than those that have abundance of riches of corn and wine and oil”.
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Distrainment
2 DATE 1683
2 NOTE John Moon of Carhouse near Garstang was prosecuted at the suit of Thomas Butler for Tithes, had "a verdict him for on the Statute given against 4 pounds 7 shillings  for treble damages amounting to 13 pounds 1 shilling.
1 OCCU John Moon accompanied George Fox in his journeys through Norfolk, as well as in Wales. He had a celebrated disputation with John Toombes the erudite vicar of Leominster in 1656. The latter was the author of a number of controversial works.
1 OCCU On this occasion Moon was accompanied by Alexander Parker who was arrested by the Leominster authorities and imprisoned a few days prior to the controversy with the vicar. The latter also had a lively debate with Fox on the ‘Inner Light’.
1 DEAT
2 DATE Nov 1689
