2 TITL The Will of Richard Moon of Jamaica
1 _OID 023bafa6-0773-407c-9634-aa747f92abf8
1 DATE 12 Mar 1799
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;He died possessed of properties and personal effects in the i
2 CONC sland of Jamaica, which he left to his two children, Henry and Sarah Moon, both of Kingston, Jamaica, equally. Richard is des&amp;shy; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;cribed as yeoman of Catforth quarter within Wood&amp;shy; pl
2 CONC umpton. He desired his estate in Catforth, including&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;the house in which he lived and its appurtenances, to be sold, out of which his debts were to be paid. To his son, Robert, 215.; so
2 CONC n Henry, &amp;ldquo;now an inhabitant of Iamaica, &amp;pound;2 10s.&amp;rdquo;; daughter Sarah, also an inhabitant of Jamaica, &amp;pound;5. All the residue he gave to his son Edward Moon, Ellen, wife of Thomas Waterworth,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Elizabeth Moon, and Mary Moon, his daughters, all living in England, share and share alike. To his son &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Edward and his three daughters he gave the yearly &lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;profits from his estate held by lease from Robert&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Gwilliam, Esq., in the township of Atherton (his grandfather&amp;rsquo;s property) in eq
2 CONC ual shares. Also to his son &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Edward and his three &amp;lsquo;daughters all the residue of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;cattle, chattels and other effects equally, excep
2 CONC t a writing desk, &amp;ldquo;which is the property of my son, Henry Moon of Iamaica&amp;rdquo; given him by his grandfather (Robert&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Moon, who died in 1778, of Liverpool and VVhinney&amp;
2 CONC shy; field). He appointed Robert Moon of Catterall (his son), William, son of Thomas Houghton of Lewth, Wood&amp;shy; plumpton, and Elizabeth Bell, gentlewoman, of Wood&amp;shy; plumpton, executors. The witnesses were Richard &lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: times;"&gt;Hardman, Dorothy Sturzaker, and the mark of Frances&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Smith, the effects were sworn under &amp;pound;100.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&
2 CONC lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
