1 _OID 05ad3089-5b9b-4342-a513-70b735d2682c
1 _DSCR Life story Written by: Maureen Moon LaPray December 1999. It can be found in the book The Family of Henry Moon: Mormon Pioneer 1819-1894.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;http://adventuresinfamilyhistory.blogspot.com/2010_10_01_archive.html&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Henry Moon&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="line-height: 1.6; ma
2 CONC rgin: 0px 0px 1em; color: #a99e87; font-family: arial, tahoma, helvetica, freesans, sans-serif; font-size: 12.88px; background-color: #3e1d00;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="width: 738px; font-size: 13.524px; line-height: 1.5
2 CONC ; color: #fff3dc; font-family: arial, tahoma, helvetica, freesans, sans-serif; background-color: #3e1d00;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul style="padding: 0px 2.5em; marg
2 CONC in: 0.5em 0px; line-height: 1.4;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="padding: 0px; margin: 0px 0px 0.25em; border: none;"&gt;Name: Henry Moon&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="padding: 0px; margin: 0px 0px 0.25em; border: none;"&gt;Born: March 29, 18
2 CONC 19 Eccleston, Lancashire, England&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="padding: 0px; margin: 0px 0px 0.25em; border: none;"&gt;Died: November 14, 1894 Farmington, Utah&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="padding: 0px; margin: 0px 0px 0.25em; borde
2 CONC r: none;"&gt;Related through: Erin's grandmother Margaret Udy Hanni&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;br&gt;On a spring day, March 29, 1819, Robert and Ann Walton Moon welcomed their second child and first born son, Henry Moon, int
2 CONC o their family at Eccleston, Lancashire, England. The Robert Moon family lived in the Horse Stone estate. Henry&amp;rsquo;s father, Robert, had inherited it from his father, Henry, and the estate had passed down through the Moon family for years. I
2 CONC t was later sold a few years after Henry left England.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The names on Henry Moon&amp;rsquo;s genealogical pedigree alternate between Henry Moon and Robert Moon from father to son for the eleven generations preceding him. These elev
2 CONC en generations were all born in Lancashire, England from the mid-1500s down to Henry Moon, born in 1819. (Henry had a son named Robert, but this Robert Henry Moon had no sons, so the chain was broken.) The small English village of Eccleston, Lancas
2 CONC hire, England where Henry was born lies about seven miles south of Preston and about five miles west of Chorley. Lancashire shares common boundaries with other English counties on the north, east and south, but on the west the Irish Sea defines he
2 CONC r boundary.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&amp;nbsp;Eccleston ChurchHenry would have had no memories of his mother, Ann, because she died just thirty-six day
2 CONC s after Henry was born, May 5, 1819 in nearby Chorley. Ann Walton Moon&amp;rsquo;s family buried her at Eccleston in the family&amp;rsquo;s burial place near the pulpit of the north aisle in St. Mary&amp;rsquo;s, an English church which dates fro
2 CONC m the fourteenth century. Henry had only one sibling, an older sister named Hannah, born sixteen months before him on November 20, 1817. Nothing is recorded of Henry&amp;rsquo;s younger years in England.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;After Ann&amp;rsquo;s dea
2 CONC th at the age of thirty-two, Henry&amp;rsquo;s father, Robert, age thirty-four, married a second time to a lady named Hannah. It is not known when the marriage took place nor if there were any children born to them. The family lived in Horse Ston
2 CONC e House.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;Horse Stone House&amp;ldquo;On Sunday, 4 June, 1837 an event occurred in Kirtland, Ohio, which determined the fate of many English families. Latter-day Apostle Heber C. Kimball says in his autobiography, &amp;l
2 CONC dquo;. . . the Prophet Joseph came to me. . . in the Kirtland Temple and whispering to me said, Brother Heber, the Spirit of the Lord has whispered to me, &amp;lsquo;Let my servant Heber go to England and proclaim my Gospel and open the door of Sal
2 CONC vation to that Nation&amp;rsquo;. The Prophet Joseph sent Heber C. Kimball, Orson Hyde, and other missionaries to England. Not quite two months later, the group landed in Liverpool, July 20, 1837.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Heber C. Kimball stayed at the h
2 CONC ouse of Matthias Moon (first cousin to Henry&amp;rsquo;s grandfather and father of Lydia) One evening while in England, Heber C. Kimball and Amos Fielding, by request, stopped at the home of Matthias and Alice Plumb Moon in Penwortham, a few mile
2 CONC s north of Eccleston. Matthias belonged to the Methodist religion, and, prior to that, the Church of England. They had nine adult children, five boys and four girls. The two Elders were kindly received by the Moon family and they had a considerabl
2 CONC e conversation on the subject of the great work of the Lord in the last days.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;From the Autobiography of Heber C. Kimball, we gain the following insight into the conversion of the Moons who lived in that area: &amp;ldquo
2 CONC ;Having an appointment to preach in the village of Wrightington, while on the way I stopped at the houses of Brothers Francis Moon and Amos Fielding, when I was informed that the family of Matthias Moon had sent a request for me to visit them, tha
2 CONC t they might have the privilege of conversing with me on the subject of the Gospel. Accordingly, Brother Amos Fielding and I paid them a visit that evening. We were kindly received by the family, and had considerable conversation on the subject o
2 CONC f my mission to England, and the great work of the Lord in the Last Days. They listened with attention to my statements, but at the same time they appeared to be prejudiced against them. We remained in conversation until a late hour, and then retur
2 CONC ned home. On our way Brother Fielding observed that he thought our visit had been in vain, as the family seemed to have considerable prejudice. I answered, &amp;lsquo;be not faithless but believing; we shall yet see great effects from this visit, f
2 CONC or I know that some of the family have received the testimony, and will shortly manifest the same; at which remark he seemed surprised&amp;hellip;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;ldquo;I returned by the way of Brother Fielding&amp;rsquo;s, with whom I agai
2 CONC n tarried for the night. The next morning I started for Preston, but when I got opposite the lane leading to Mr. Moon&amp;rsquo;s, I was forcibly led by the Spirit of the Lord to call and see them again. I therefore directed my steps to the house
2 CONC . On my arrival I knocked at the door. Mrs. Moon exclaimed, &amp;lsquo;Come in! You are welcome here! I and the lassies (meaning her daughters) have just been calling on the Lord, and praying that he would send you this way.&amp;rsquo; She then inf
2 CONC ormed me of her state of mind since I was there, and said she at first rejected my testimony, and endeavored to think lightly on the things I had advanced, but on trying to pray, the heavens seemed to be like brass over her head, and it was like ir
2 CONC on under her feet. She did not know what was the matter, saying, &amp;lsquo;Certainly the man has not bewitched me has he?&amp;rsquo; and upon inquiring she found it was the same with the lassies. They then began to reflect on the things I told the
2 CONC m, and thinking it possible that I had told them the truth, they resolved to lay the case before the Lord, and beseech Him to give them a testimony concerning the things I had testified of. She then observed that as soon as they did so, light brok
