2 CONC ry&amp;rsquo;s oldest son, and William R. Smith were sustained as his counselors.While Henry served as Bishop in Woodland, he organized the Sunday School, YMMIA, and Primary. The Relief Society was already functioning.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;</line><li
2 CONC ne>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&amp;nbsp;Moon Circle, Farmington, UtahAt one time Henry wanted to move Temperance and her family to Woodland so he could have the two families together, bu
2 CONC t Temperance was comfortable in her four room adobe home in Farmington. She loved her roses, trees and garden and couldn&amp;rsquo;t bear to leave. Even though Temperance chose to stay in Farmington, it is likely that Henry took the children back a
2 CONC nd forth from Woodland to Farmington on his various trips. Temperance&amp;rsquo;s son Phillip said in his later years, he would rather go to Woodland than Disneyland. He had very fond memories of his early adventures in Woodland.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt
2 CONC ;Henry owned big orchards and lots of land in Farmington and Woodland so his children were all taught to work in their youth. There were no idle hands around the homes. Hours were never set for the children, but they worked early and late. In Woodl
2 CONC and, the children attended school in one room log building with one teacher. Amanda said, &amp;ldquo;I think it is a wonder one did secure any education at all under the circumstances.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The Edmunds Act was passed in the U.S. Congr
2 CONC ess on March 14, 1882 aimed at trying to stop the Mormons from practicing polygamy. Polygamy was now officially against the law and U.S. Marshals were sent to arrest those breaking it. The fact that Temperance and Mary Ann lived in distant towns a
2 CONC t that time was probably good.&lt;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear
2 CONC : both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;br&gt;Henry suffered a stroke in 1888, a year after his last baby, Nephi, was born and a y
2 CONC ear before the Manifesto was issued. The stroke rendered Henry an invalid for the last six years of his life. All Nephi remembered about his father was &amp;ldquo;he was quite feeble.&amp;rdquo; What a hardship this must have been for the two wive
2 CONC s who still had young children in their care. No government aid such as Social Security was available to them. After Henry had the stroke, he remained in Farmington with his wife, Temperance, who nursed him. Henry Moon&amp;rsquo;s undaunted spiri
2 CONC t finally gave up and passed from this world to the next on November 14, 1894 at the age of seventy-five. Henry was buried in the Farmington City Cemetery next to his son Franklin.&lt;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: cente
2 CONC r;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>Henry tol
2 CONC d his daughter Amanda, &amp;ldquo;All my life has been spent in service for others and I am not sorry for it, for after all we get the most pleasure in doing good.&amp;rdquo; In Henry&amp;rsquo;s words, he says, &amp;ldquo;I am happy and I want yo
2 CONC u to be happy. I am for peace, rightness, happiness and every other principle that is for the happifying [sic] of the human family and to increase and grow in the knowledge of the truth and to rise until I attain to the perfect stature of a man i
2 CONC n Christ. How can that be done? By being faithful obedient to those holding the holy priesthood, keeping the commandments of God in all things. We are only sent here on a mission. We ought to fulfill that mission aright. The old ship Zion will lan
2 CONC d us safe if we will keep on board, but a great many jump off and that is the reason they get lost. If I had jumped off when I came to New York, the captain could not have landed me safe. It is just so with those that obey the gospel.&amp;rdquo;&lt
2 CONC ;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;em&gt;Written by: Maureen Moon LaPray December 1999. It can be found in the book&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style="color: #936307;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Family of Henry Moon: Mormon Pioneer 1819-1894.&lt;/em&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
