2 TITL T Rawcliffe - Account of visit to Nauvoo
1 _OID 05dceb4f-1f49-48a1-8bd6-41d58794547f
1 DATE 23 March 1844
1 PLAC Preston, Lancashire, England
1 _DSCR Preston Chronicle - Saturday 23 March 1844
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;NAUVOO, THE CITY OF THE MORMONITES.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;MORMON EMIGRANTS : JOE SMITH, THE PROPHET :&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;HIS SAYINGS AND DOING
2 CONC S :&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;MORMONISM, A PIECE OF FRAUD AND IMPOSTURE, ETC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;sp
2 CONC an style="font-size: large;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;To the Editor of the Preston Chronicle.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mar
2 CONC gin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;SIR,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-
2 CONC bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;Having lately returned from the United States of America, after an absence of nearly three years, and as people are daily inquiring of me concerning that country, and particularly respectin
2 CONC g Nauvoo, the Mormonite city, where I have principally resided, I have considered it best to answer a few of their queries through the medium of your paper. I have travelled through various parts of the United States, and have resided for some tim
2 CONC e in Upper Canada; but, as you have heard the sentiments of other men, of greater abilities and deeper research, on the subject of emigration, I shall reserve my opinions to myself, and proceed to give a sketch of Nauvoo, or the Mormons&amp;rsquo
2 CONC ; Zion, as it was when I left it in December last. I will first state that I am not a Mormon, and never was; and, being fully persuaded that Mormonism was a Yankee speculation, a deeply-laid conspiracy in the guise of religion; knowing well, too, t
2 CONC hat John Bull is so easily caught by a bait of this kind; and, by their craft and oily tongues, in spinning out long yarns about gold plates, angels, and revelations, they caught several of my connexions in their nets, I was induced to visit the ho
2 CONC ly city, to examine for myself, and see if the signs were following the believer, according to the doctrine they preached, - for the sick were to be healed, the blind to receive their sight, the lame to walk, and the dead to rise. But it is no les
2 CONC s true than strange, that I have never witnessed one such instance, of the simplest kind, the whole of the two years I have been among them. I went to see if the poor Mormons, from England, were, as they expected, living under their own vines and f
2 CONC ig-trees; but, alas, instead of the shades of vines and fig-trees, there were two or three families commonly stowed in some small hut, dejected and gloomy, through distress and sickness, scarcely one English family escaping at the fall of the year
2 CONC , and the deaths, in Nauvoo, from July to October, averaging from about 15 to 21 weekly. There they were, living on the coarsest kind of food, and scores could not get a pinch of salt to make their hog-mess more palatable; for salt is an imported a
2 CONC rticle to that place, and cannot be had without cash, while cash for work, at Nauvoo, is looked on as a phenomenon in nature. Joe, and his twelve apostles, generally manage to pocket it all, in the name of the Lord, as soon as anybody brings any in
2 CONC . The poor are frequently obliged to wear their shirts for three or four weeks, for want of soap to wash them with; and whole families have to go barefooted, for want of shoes, in cold weather, although they have plenty of money owing them for wor
2 CONC k on the Temple, Nauvoo House, and from the heads of the church. No tongue can tell, or pen describe, the poverty, misery, and sickness, the poor surviving Mormons, from England, have had to wade through; and God only knows the number that have gon
2 CONC e to an untimely grave, unpitied and unmourned, broken-hearted through disappointment, and from the want of suitable food, proper treatment, and comfortable houses in time of sickness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom
2 CONC : .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;It may be said by the Mormons, in England, that they should live by faith; and, if any be sick, let them call for an elder, and let him pray, &amp;amp;c., according to St. James. But I can tell t
2 CONC hem that those unblushing scoundrels, who came over to England to preach that doctrine, dare not live by it; for, if one of them feel a little sick, his house is sure to be crowded with doctors, and a world of labour is made for his recovery. Inste
2 CONC ad of calling for an elder, they call for a doctor, both for themselves, their wives, and children. Joseph&amp;rsquo;s wife (that is, Sister Emma, or the elect lady, alias the Grand Master of the Ladies&amp;rsquo; Freemasons&amp;rsquo; Society, i
2 CONC n Nauvoo) was taken sick, a while ago, and no less than four doctors were attending on her. When I left, Joe had a doctor in his own house. Joe keeps a chest of medicine, and sells and deals out drugs himself, which is quite inconsistent with the d
2 CONC octrine they preach. These leaders of the poor dupes are now living in splendor, and at their ease, and laughing in their sleeves at the miserable fanatics who have enabled them to do so; for they will scarcely look at the simpletons from England
2 CONC , after they have stripped them of their little all. One of Joe&amp;rsquo;s apostles was preaching one day, and said these English will have to be stripped of their gold, or their yellow god, or they will be lost; for John Bull always worships th
2 CONC e Mammon of unrighteousness, but we must get it from them by some means, for the Lord says they cannot worship both God and Mammon; yet, says he, you will often see when John Bull&amp;rsquo;s money is gone, his God is gone at the same time. And thi
2 CONC s is a plan that the Lord has laid to try these English by; for how could they come up through much tribulation, and have their robes washed white, &amp;amp;c, while they had plenty of money to procure the comforts of this life. At the same time th
2 CONC ese leaders are rolling in luxury, adding house to house and land to land, contrary to the scripture injunction. Nauvoo is something like a barber&amp;rsquo;s shop, where the managers shave their customers of everything that is superfluous or of a
2 CONC n earthly nature, so that they may have no burdens or impediments to hinder them from slipping easily into the kingdom of Heaven; and, with the true spirit of rapacity which has always distinguished these Mormon leaders, they are faring sumptuousl
2 CONC y every day out of what they have got from their followers, and can coolly look on the work of destruction and wreck of families they have caused, and exclaim &amp;ldquo;this is the Lord&amp;rsquo;s sifting time, and he is sending his angels to gat
2 CONC her his elect from the four winds; and you must humble yourselves before God, for his destroying angel is about to go through the nations; and you must live by and according to every word that proceeds from the mouth of his holy prophet Joseph.&amp
2 CONC ;rdquo; Joe, in the meantime, will often say, that he will provide for himself and his own family, if this generation and the Mormon church go to hell; which prophecy he is fulfilling to the very letter, for he is now the chief magistrate or Mayo
2 CONC r of the city, and, through being surrounded by satellites of his own choosing, for aldermen, &amp;amp;c., he and they can pass their own laws, and make it a profitable concern, by taxing the inhabitants to a greater amount, to support him and hi
2 CONC s crew in splendor, than both the state tax, the county tax, the government revenue, and all the other taxes put together. Joe&amp;rsquo;s city tax was fifty cents on every 100 dollars worth of property, while all other taxes only amounted to forty
2 CONC -five cents. On the same, the last year. He is now the head tavern keeper in Nauvoo, commander-in-chief of the Nauvoo legion, a military body which are mostly Mormons, of which Joe says the prophet was partially speaking, when he talks of the littl
2 CONC e stone coming out of the mountain, and rolling forth until it had subdued the whole earth. The Nauvoo legion is to do the same, and they are infusing the same spirit into the minds of the children, so that on the days of parade, which they go thro
2 CONC ugh eight or ten times in the year, you may see old men and children, in companies, going through military evolutions, apparently as eager after martial glory, as Buonaparte was when he crossed the frozen Alps.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&l
2 CONC t;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;I will just mention, before I leave this subject, that the training, or parading, is causing a deal of enmity and jealousy in the minds of the surrounding inhabitants, a
2 CONC s to what they ultimately intend to do, and the world may easily perceive the cause of the Missourians driving them from their state; for they interfere at elections, and all unite and vote on one side, according to the ipse dirit of Joe, or Hyru
2 CONC m (his brother), as they generally are favoured with a revelation from God, at the polling time, to let them know what side to vote on. The Mormons generally vote on the democratic ticket; and, when Hyrum Smith declared, at the last August election
2 CONC , that he had inquired of the Lord what side the Mormons should take, and that the Lord had decided in favour of the democrats (generally the Irishmen&amp;rsquo;s party), a Paddy exclaimed, &amp;ldquo;Share, and don&amp;rsquo;t you hear, no, when G
2 CONC od spakes, it&amp;rsquo;s always democratic.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;I need not say any more to let you see Joe&amp;rsquo;s influence, and how thi
2 CONC s modern Mahomet can lead them by the nose; and, providing the Mormons were sufficiently strong, I leave you to judge whether they would be peaceable inhabitants of any state, unless their man God was the King or governor.&lt;span style="mso-spacer
2 CONC un: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;(To be concluded next week)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line
2 CONC ><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;Longton, March 12&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1844.&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC T Rawcliffe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-ansi-language: en-us;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
