2 TITL The Eccleston Moon Estate By The End of The 19th Century
1 _OID 073cc918-5532-4141-8028-1713eaa65eb6
1 DATE 1880
1 PLAC Eccleston, Lancashire, England
1 _DSCR Extract From The Moons Of Amounderness
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;The wills of the Eccleston Moons and the Tithe&amp;nbsp;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Award show that the family owned in freehold and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;leasehold, a large portion of the centre of the township&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: times;"&gt;and many outlying areas such as Sherbourn house, the windmill lands, and those near Ulneswalton. A note&amp;shy;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;worthy feature of their rural economy we
2 CONC re the exten&amp;shy; sive orchards in the old village centre around Parr lane. Partition of their inheritance played havoc with &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;their financial stability, especially in the years of rural &lt;/s
2 CONC pan&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;depression at the end of the nineteenth century when even ancillary trades failed to return a decent living, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;and resulted in the dispersion of the E
2 CONC ccleston estate.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
