2 TITL Hursley &amp; Anfield
1 _OID 0a7a7e46-e516-4c9c-a34c-61e85603ea42
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;Anfield is known today as Ampfield, it is a hamlet very close to Hursley.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hursley is refered to in the late 12th Century when the Bishop of Winchester Henry de Bloi
2 CONC s built a manor house called Merdon castle within the parish in 1138. It continued in the ownership of the Bishop of Winchester until&amp;nbsp;1552 when it was surrendered to King Edward VI.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;The buildings were ruined b
2 CONC y the 16th Century when Edwrad VI granted the manor to Sir Philip Hoby.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;The hoby family sold the manor to Thomas Clerke in 1600.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;The estate passed to the Cromwell family in 1643 when Oliver Cr
2 CONC omwellis son Richard married Dorothy major daughter of the then owner Richard Major.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Richard Cromwell lived in Hursley from 1649 to 1658 when he was made lord Protector of the Realm following the death of his father. This made Hur
2 CONC sley briefly the counry seat of the ruler of England. However he was forced from offfice and fled the county within months. Richard&amp;#39;s son Oliver Cromwell II took over Hursley. Richard returned to Hursley after Oliver died in 1705 until hi
2 CONC s death in 1712 where he was buried in All Saints Curch Hursley. Richard&amp;#39;s daugther sold the estates to Sir William Heathcote, baronet for &amp;pound;35,100 in 1718.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;William Heathcote was a successful merchant. He built Hu
2 CONC rsley House (owner by IBM). William died in 1751 and passed the estates to his son Sir Thomas Heathcote.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;Poet John Keble was Vicar of Hursely in 1835 and rebuilt the church in 1848.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
2 CONC &lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
