2 CONC 1,4018-1-39665,00.html"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thayne&lt;/strong&gt;,  Martha Jane&lt;/a&gt; (12)&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td&gt;&lt;img src="ch/images/spacer.gif" alt="" width="10" height="1"&gt;&lt;/td&gt; &lt;td class="bodyText"&gt;&lt;a h
2 CONC ref="churchhistory/library/pioneerdetails/1,15791,4018-1-39669,00.html"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thayne&lt;/strong&gt;,  Miriam Catherine&lt;/a&gt; (1)&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td&gt;&lt;img src="ch/images/spacer.gif" alt="" width="10" height="
2 CONC 1"&gt;&lt;/td&gt; &lt;td class="bodyText"&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="churchhistory/library/pioneerdetails/1,15791,4018-1-39664,00.html"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thayne&lt;/strong&gt;,  Nancy Cunningham&lt;/a&gt; (34)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="header"&
2 CONC gt;Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel, 1847&amp;ndash;1868&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;table border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td width="100%" bgcolor="#d5d6d6"&gt;&lt;img src="ch/images/s
2 CONC pacer.gif" alt="" width="50" height="1"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;table border="0" cellspacing="8" cellpadding="0" width="95%" class="bodytext" align="center"&gt;  &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td class="bodytext"&gt;&l
2 CONC t;strong&gt;Narrative:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td class="bodytext"&gt;Job Pingree immigrated to Utah in 1857. In the autumn of  1859, he returned to his native England to dispose of family property. While  there he serve
2 CONC d as a part-time missionary. In the spring of 1861, he returned to  America, escorting some of his recent converts. &lt;br&gt; &lt;p&gt;At Florence, Nebraska Territory, 24-year-old Pingree was appointed captain  over a company of 33 wagons and 3 o
2 CONC r 4 carriages. This was an independent  company, the emigrants being able to purchase their supplies and outfits without  Church assistance. Most in the company came from St. Louis, but a few came  directly from England. There were about 150 peopl
2 CONC e in the company. Pingree did  not submit a roster of his company, drawn from his memory, to the Church  Historian�s Office until 1916. It is probably incomplete. Oxen pulled most of  the wagons except for four or five outfits drawn by horses. Ther
2 CONC e was also at  least one span of mules pulling a wagon, and a few cows tagged along. The  company left Florence on June 7. &lt;br&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A few in the company suffered accidents even before leaving the outfitting  place. One man ha
2 CONC d to ride in a wagon all the way because his foot was crushed  when a horse threw him. Before leaving, a woman with a painful inflammation in  the joint of a finger had the sore opened and the bone scraped. The wound was  slow to mend so someone i
2 CONC n the company suggested she wrap her finger in a  gunpowder poultice to speed healing. One evening as she was lighting a campfire,  her poultice caught fire and the gunpowder exploded. She lost the fleshy tip of  her finger. &lt;br&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&l
2 CONC t;p&gt;In his book &lt;em&gt;Roughing It&lt;/em&gt;, Mark Twain wrote that just east of  Independence Rock, his westward-bound stagecoach passed a Mormon train  consisting of 33 wagons. Almost certainly this would have been Pingree&amp;#39;s  compa
2 CONC ny. Twain observed that the emigrants had no head coverings, were covered  with dust and looked very tired. &lt;br&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Most of the company arrived in Salt Lake on September 2 except for some left  behind who straggled in at inter
2 CONC vals during the next few days. Pingree reported  that there were no accidents &amp;quot;to speak of,&amp;quot; no deaths, and little loss of  cattle. Pingree was asked by Brigham Young how his company had fared. &amp;quot;After I  told him,&amp;quo
2 CONC t; Pingree recorded, &amp;quot;he said I had done just right.&amp;quot; &lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt; &lt;/table&gt;&lt;table border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" width="100%" class="bodytext"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt; &lt;
2 CONC tr&gt; &lt;td colspan="3" valign="top"&gt;&lt;!-- Included: CH Pioneer Procs Lib //--&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td colspan="3" valign="top"&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td valign="top"&gt; &lt;table border="0" cellspacin
2 CONC g="0" cellpadding="0"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td class="header"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Job &lt;strong&gt;Pingree&lt;/strong&gt; Company  (1861)&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td class="bodytext" align="center"&gt;  &lt;/td&
2 CONC gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;table border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" width="98%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt; &lt;tr&gt; &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Departure: &lt;/strong&gt;7 June 1861 &lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Arrival: &lt;/strong&gt;2
2 CONC 4 August, 5 September  1861&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Company Information: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;33 wagons were in the company when  it began its journey from the outfitting post at Florence, Nebraska (now Omaha).  &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&
2 CONC lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
