1 _META <metadataxml><content><line /><line>&lt;tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;td valign="top"&gt;Birth:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;td align="left" valign="top"&gt;Oct. 5, 1844&lt;br&gt;Blackburn, England&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>
2 CONC &lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;td valign="top"&gt;Death:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;td align="left" valign="top"&gt;Sep. 22, 1932&lt;br&gt;Heber City&lt;br&gt;Wasatch County&lt;br&gt;Utah, USA&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&l
2 CONC t;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;td colspan="2" valign="top"&gt;&lt;br&gt;Roger Horrocks was the son of John and Ann Horrocks. Mr. Horrocks was a pioneer choir leader and weaver. He died on Thursday of last week. He was born in Lanc
2 CONC ashire, England October 5, 1844 and come to Utah when a boy. He lived in Heber, Utah for 65 years. He married Sarah Ann Taylor in 1867. She died in 1931. Eleven sons and daughters survive: Mrs. Sarah Richardson and Joseph Horrocks of Vernal, Utah
2 CONC , Roger and John J. Horrocks of Hayden, David and William Horrocks of Roosevelt, Utah, Franklin Horrocks of Duchesne, Utah, Brigham Horrocks of Idaho, Mrs. Joseph M. Jones of California, Mrs. Frank Epperson and Thomas Horrocks of Heber, Utah, one s
2 CONC ister Rachel Duke of Heber, Utah.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tbody&gt;</line><line /></content></metadataxml>
