2 TITL Will of Edward Harrill
1 _OID 12adc6cf-6c05-46e0-8231-1d7d288d6eb2
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Edward Harrill was a yeoman, a man owning a small amount of land and ranking as a commoner; a freeholder; especially a man of respectable standing in the community. His g
2 CONC randdaughter and grandson Jane and Thomas would have grown up in a rural environment helping their grandparents with household and farm chores. They were the only grandchildren of the Harrills.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Edward Harrill made his will, &amp;q
2 CONC uot;this twenty third day of February in the year of our Lord one thousand seven hundred and eighty eight&amp;quot; and signed it with his mark. On 28 October 1797, John Harrill and William Bradley, the executors of this will named, appeared and fo
2 CONC resworn in common form.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The will reads in part as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I, Edward Harrill of Bromsgrove in the County of Worcester, yeoman, being of sound and disposing mind and memory....do make this my last will and testeme
2 CONC ntin manner following....I give and divie unto my loving wife Jane the rents, insures and profits of all that my dwellinghouse, shop and garden now in the occupation of Thomas Ward....and all that close of pasture land thereto adjoining and belongi
2 CONC n now i my own possession with their appurtenances and situated at Yearnold Lane in the Parish of Bromsgrove....for and during the term of her natural life....and from and after her decease, I give and devise all the said premises unto my cousin, J
2 CONC ohn Harril of the parish of Leobury North in the County of Salop, woodcollier for and during the term of his natural life and from and after his decease, I give and devise all the said premises....unto my two grandchildren, Thomas Dany and Jane Dan
2 CONC by, children of my deceased daughter Sarah Danby to hold to them, their heirs and assigns as tenants in common and not as joint tenants. Also I give and bequeath unto my said cousin John Harrill, my respected friends, John Murray, Skinner, and Will
2 CONC iam Bradley, Malster, both of Bromsgrove, whom I appoint join executors of this my will and guardians to my said two grandchildren during their minoites in respect to the property hereby given to them. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nb
2 CONC sp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Sarah Danby died after the last birth of their daughter Jane, almost six months after the birth of Jane. She was buried 04 October 1780 in Belbroughton, Worcestershire, England. Sarah was the daughter of Edward and Jane Gree
2 CONC n Danby. After this Thomas and Jane Edward&amp;#39;s two grandchildren lived with them and because of Thomas Danby and his remarriage the two children stayed with Edward and Jane Green Harrill.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp; Jane, Edwards youngest granddaughter was married at 17 years old to Richard Webley, also of Bromsgrove. On 01 July 1797, just two and one half months after she was married, Edward Harrill died and his wife Jane followed him in death on the 0
2 CONC 6 July 1797, five days later. They were both buried in Belbroughton.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
