2 TITL History of John Johnson Thayn as told by his son Ebenezer Thayn
1 _OID 13e22460-e193-4cf0-b6b7-9465c5a5eb49
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;John Johnson Thayn was born in Glasgow, Scotland, November 11, 1825. When he was about two years old he came to C
2 CONC anada with his family. Soon after he arrrived his father died. His mother was unable to support all her children, so the oldest boy, Ebenezer, was let out to work for his board. There were only two boys but there were four girls. Following are th
2 CONC e names of the children: Ellen, Jane, Ebenezer, Janet, John Johnson and Fanny.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; John Johnson m
2 CONC arried Sydney Boyer in Canada. While living there four children were born to them, Mary Ann, Nephi, John Lehi and Ellen Jane. He then came to the United States and settled in the state of Iowa. I think four more children were born to them there: Em
2 CONC ma, Fanny, Eliza Catherine and Janet. In 1861 they came to Utah. They had believed the gospel, but had not been baptised. When they were ready to leave Council Bluffs as an independent company there were two girls from England wanting to come. He w
2 CONC as asked if he could take one of them with his company and was told to take his pick, so he picked one. Her name was Elizabeth Hunt. When they arrived in Salt Lake, Elizabeth lived with the family for about one year and then John Johnson took her t
2 CONC o be his second wife. They were married on the 25th of October 1862. To them were born five boys: Joseph, who died as a baby, William Alvin, Brigham, Edgar and Ebenezer. Elizabeth's son and Sidney's son, Hyrum were born on the same day, Nov. 8th 18
2 CONC 63. Sidney gave birth to two more children while living in Salt Lake.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; They lived in Salt Lake
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;City and vicinity,having a saw mill up Mill Creek Canyon, also one in Thayn Canyon. Elizabeth&amp;nbsp;lived at the sawmill part of the time while Sydney lived in Salt Lake. At times they both lived in the same house.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Elizabeth died and was buried in Salt Lake City on June 21, 1875 when Ebenezer was just 10 months old. Sidney too
2 CONC k all the children and reared them as her own. Shortly after, enemies or someone who was jealous burned one sawmill down and he lost most of his property. They moved to Woodland, Utah, taking the other sawmill&amp;nbsp;where they operated it in Ril
2 CONC ey's Canyon. Here they also farmed using oxen for teams for logging and plowing. The land was so rocky they had to haul the rocks off before planting. When they planted their grain they had to take long willows and tie together and tied the oxen t
2 CONC o them going up and down to cover the wheat. When they harvested it they had to use a cradle and bind it by hand.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; They built a water power mill and put in a picket and lathe mill which was run by a turbine wheel. They sawed and sold some of their lumber, but most of it was made into pickets and lather. The timber came from the head water
2 CONC s of the Provo River, cutting it early and floating the logs down the river 12 to 25 miles. &lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp; In October of 1886 part of the family moved to Wellington, Utah. The rest came in November of that same&amp;nbsp;year. The first group stayed with Sydney and her husband, Ephriam Green. After the others came they lived in a one room log c
2 CONC abin belonging to Ben Buchanin on the south side&amp;nbsp;of the track close to the river on the old Jessen farm. Eliza and her husband, Elisha Jones came to Wellington with the last group, all living in the same house. The boys slept out in the wa
2 CONC gonbox as there was not enough room in the house.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; In the spring they went up to Pin
2 CONC e Canyon and got logs to build a new house, which was John Johnson's home from then on. &lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp; In May 1901 Sydney died and was buried in Wellington, Utah. John Johnson's son, Ebenezer bought his father's home and from this time on John Johnson made his home with Ebenezer and his family. He died 21 May 1910 and was buried beside his wif
2 CONC e, Sidney. &lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; John Johnson served a mission to Canada, England and Scotland (1878-188
2 CONC 0). For more on this read history by Alvin Thayn.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadat
