2 TITL Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel, Trail Excerpt by Joseph Holbrook
1 _OID 19c8ded8-bdd2-4878-b6f7-fe69633a631d
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel, 1847&amp;ndash;1868&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;Source of Trail Excerpt: &lt;br&gt;Holbrook, Joseph, Reminiscences [ca. 1860
2 CONC -1871], fd. 1, 145-47. &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;Read Trail Excerpt:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;Along about the first of June we made a move west over the Horn River where when the company of Brigham Young came up were organized into his company[
2 CONC .] Daniel Carns [Garn] Captain of fifty[,] Anson Call Captain &amp;lt;of&amp;gt; the ten I belonged to. I continued to be afflicted with chills &amp;amp; Fever and being unable to sit up most of my time I employed Benjamin Tolman to Drive one of m
2 CONC y teams[,] a brother of Judson Tolman&amp;mdash;while Judson drove the other[.] our team which now consisted of some unbroke[n] stears &amp;amp; cows made it very difficult for 8 or 10 days to get along but as our loads hade been gro[w]ing hifher [
2 CONC heavier.] by our living and trading our flour to for our oxen we managed to keep up with the camp. &lt;br&gt;one day on the Platte River Sister Elisha Groves broke her leg[.] the camp stoped a few minutes when brother Brigham Young came up. Drs too
2 CONC k and set her leg[.] in a short time we were again on the march[.] there were about 200 waggons in this company until we passed Fort Bridger Larimie [Laramie] when the company seperated into fifties for the better conven[i]ence of traveling among t
2 CONC he Black-hills &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;on the Boise River one of my twins cows died which I had worked on the [&amp;hellip;..] hade from winter Quarters[.] I was now forced to hire another cow from Benjamin Tolman to [..] with she soon broke in[.] it be
2 CONC ing our off cow that died[.] my health had so improved that I could walk a part of the time &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;at I[n]dependence Rock both Hyran [Hiram] Clawson loste some of his team so that I took on three hundred of his flour[.] some of the res
2 CONC t of the brethren took some more so they could continue on thus to the valley. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;on the sweet water River we killed some Buffalo and we diveded the meat and carried with us[.] we also gathered sometime bushels of Saleratus from th
2 CONC e Sareratus Lakes which was about 6 inches thick[.] it answers well for making bread. We hade a plenty of feed for our stock and our sheep was much trouble to us as they would not stay with other sheep in the night but would ramble off[.] one nigh
2 CONC t they rambled off and we hunted all the next day and we could not find them So we had to Start without them. But in a few days after brother George D. Grant found two of them. one &amp;lt;of&amp;gt; ours[,] one of brother Calls. the rest were neve
2 CONC r found[.] we took every caution to save our breadstuff as we were very short from being forced to sell it off to lighten our load and to buy oxen&amp;mdash;&amp;amp; in the fore part of our journ[ey] we had a plenty of Milk &amp;amp; butter but th
2 CONC e last of our journey we had to feed our pigs as they had grown so that it took more for them then it did when we started &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;towards the last of the journey Sister Daniel [Margaret] Carns [Garn] cut her knee with a common Jim knif
2 CONC e blade so that in a few days it so inflamed that she died in about two days travel of the vally[.] She was brought too the vally to be buried at the head of Echo Canyon[.] we stoped a few days and went about 6 miles to the South and picked servic
2 CONC e berries[.] I got about 6 Bus[hels] which we dried for our fruit[.] as we were in the advance of the main company we staid on Weber River until Brother Brighams Company came up[.] we came into the valley about the 20 day of Sept. 1848. and campe
2 CONC d north west of the old fort &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
