1 _DSCR Excerpt from The Moons of Amounderness
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;He lived in Catforth as a leaseholder of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Warrens and devised all his messuages and tenements in Catforth &amp;ldquo;held by lease of esquire Warren&amp;rdquo; to his wife whilst she lived and did not marry again. If she did not live on the est
2 CONC ate his son Robert Moon was to take it into his hands and allow his mother &amp;pound;10 yearly. Whereas he was bound to pay his son Robert &amp;pound;8 yearly he allowed him &amp;pound;10. To his son Robert he gave the leasehold in Catforth for li
2 CONC fe, if the lives in the lease so long lived and then to his grandson Robert Moon, provided he paid his mother &amp;pound;4 yearly. He also desired that Robert (testator&amp;rsquo;s son) should pay &amp;pound;20 to each of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span style="font-family: times;"&gt;his own children except the eldest, Richard of Jamaica, and if his son Robert failed to do so, Richard his grand&amp;shy; son, must pay the same to each of his brothers and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="f
2 CONC ont-family: times;"&gt;sisters when they attained the age of 21 years. He gave to his wife Ann all credits, goods and personal &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;estate during her life, except the cupboard and the &lt;/span&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;table in the &amp;lsquo;house&amp;rsquo;. She to dispose of the same as she &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;thought fit to her children and grandchildren. The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="fo
2 CONC nt-family: times;"&gt;executors were Thomas Benson of Wharles and Richard&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Hudson of Woodplumpton. A brief inventory of the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;goods of R
2 CONC ichard Moon of Catforth was prized by Henry Moon and James Sanderson, 4 February, 1744/5 and amounted to &amp;pound;64 45. 6s. Goods in the &amp;lsquo;house&amp;rsquo; &amp;pound;5; buttery &amp;pound;1 55. 0d.; chamber &amp;pound;3 10s.; kitchen &
2 CONC amp;pound;2 25. 0d.; upstairs &amp;pound;5; carts and husbandry gear, &amp;pound;5 16s. 0d.; a mare &amp;pound;2 155. 0d.; six cows and a heifer &amp;pound;24 10s. 0d.; corn and hay &amp;pound;10; a pig 7s. 6d.; apparel, bridle &amp;pound;4. The wi
2 CONC ll is signed Richard Moon and witnessed by Edmund Charnley, Richard Clarkson, and VVilliam Critchley.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadat
2 CONC axml>
