2 TITL Biography of Jane Danby Webley
1 _OID 22c9b94d-cf4b-4b10-950a-e7ac06a7f087
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Jane Danby was christened at Belbroughton, Worcestershire, England 13 Apr 1780. She was the daughter of Thomas Danby and Sarah (Harrill or Orrell) Danby. Her mother die
2 CONC d when Jane was almost 6 months old and was buried 04 Oct 1780 in Belbroughton. Her father married again, 25 Jan 1781, at Bromsgrove, Worcestershire, England. Judith Houghton. Jane and her older brother, Thomas, appear to have been raised by thei
2 CONC r maternal grandparents, Edward and Jane&amp;nbsp;Green Harrill.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Edward Harrill was a yoeman, a man owning a small amount of land and ranking as a commoner; a freeholder, especially a man of respecta
2 CONC ble standing in the community. Jane and Thomas would have grown up in a rural environment helpng their grandparents with household and farm chores. They were the only grandchildren of the Harrills.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; E
2 CONC dward Harrill made his will, &amp;quot;this twenty third day of February in the year of our Lord one thousand seven hundred and eighty eight&amp;quot; and signed it with his mark. On 28 Oct 1797, John Harrill and William Bradley, the executors of t
2 CONC his will named, appeared and foresworn in common form.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The will reads in part as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I. Edward Harrill of Bromsgrove in the County of Worcester, yeoman, being of sound and disposing mind and memory.....do ma
2 CONC ke this my last will and testement in manner following.....I give and divise unto&amp;nbsp;my loving wife Jane the rents, issues and profits of all that my dwellinghouse, shop and graden now in the occupation of Thomas Ward.....and all that close o
2 CONC f pasture land thereto adjoining and belonging now in my own possession with their appurtenances and situate[d] at Yearnold Lane in the Parish of Bromsgrove....for and during the term of her natural life....and from and after her decease, I give an
2 CONC d devise all the said premises unto my cousin, John Harrill of the parish of Leobury North in the County of Salop, woodcollier for and during the term of his natural life and from and after his decease, I give and devise all the said premises....un
2 CONC to my two grandchildren, Thomas Danby and Jane Danby, children of my deceased daughter Sarah Danby to hold to them, their heirs and assigns as tenants in common and not as joint tenants. Also I give and bequeth unto my said cousin John Harrill, m
2 CONC y respected friends, John Murray, Skinner, and William Bradley, Malster, both of Bromsgrove, whom I appoint joint executors of this my will and guardians to my said two grandchildren during their minorities in respect to the property hereby given t
2 CONC o them....&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Jane Danby was 17 years old when she married, 17 Apr 1797 at Bromsgrove, Richard Webley, also of Bromsgrove. On 01 Jul 1797, just two and one half months after she was married, h
2 CONC er grandfather Harrill died and his wife, Jane, followed him in death on the 06 Jul 1797, five days later. They were buried in Belbroughton. Following their marriage, Richard and Jane remained in where they christened their first child, a daughte
2 CONC r named Elizabeth Webley, on 28 May 1798. Their second daughter, Sarah appears to have also been born in Belbroughton, but the record of her birth and christening was not found. In the 1871 Census she (Sarah) stated that she was born in Belbroughto
2 CONC n. In the 1851 Census she listed her birthplace as Farfeld, Worcestershire County-which would be Fairfield. Before the birth of their next child, Mary, 28 Dec 1802, they had moved to Bromsgrove Church. Here also 7 year old Elizabeth died and was bu
2 CONC ried. During this time they had two more children, their only son, Phillip, born in 1810 and Susannah, born 30 Nov 1812. Later they joined the Bromsgrove Baptist Church and then when the Little Catskill Baptist Church was formed 04 Jul 1830 by Mose
2 CONC s Nokes, Jane Danby Webley was the third person to be baptized into the church on the same day it opened.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Jane was left a widow when Richard Webley died 16 Apr 1841. Jane&amp;#39;s good Chr
2 CONC istian upbringing throughout her life must have strengthened her at this time. Following his death, she joined the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints (Mormon). She was one of the earliest converts to the Church in England. Many members o
2 CONC f her family also joined the LDS church. England especially had been a fertile field for Mormon missionaries during the 1830&amp;#39;s and 1840&amp;#39;s. The first missionaries of England arrived in the late 1830&amp;#39;s and found good success a
2 CONC mong the poor and humble of the industrial districts. The promise of a better life in a America was appealing to many and the message of the gospel was eagerly accepted by thousands during those early years. A desire to gather to Zion in&amp;nbsp;A
2 CONC merica was prevalent and many made plans to immigrate to America. The LDS set up fund was later established to help the people come to America it was called the Perpetual Emigration Fund which provided the ship passage, usually ten English pounds
2 CONC , for those Saints who could not afford to leave England on there own resources.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Shortly after the establishment of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints, Joseph Smith the prophe
2 CONC t and the people moved from New York to Kirtland, Ohio. A beautiful temple was built, extensive missionary work was instituted, and the Church made substantial gains in membership. Among the missions opened was one in Missouri, and the body of th
2 CONC e Church later moved to Missouri and then to Illinois. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Although Jane was 60 years old, she agreed to accompany the other Saints and left England to join Joseph Smith whom was the prophet o
2 CONC f the church at the settlement of the church in Nauvoo, Illinois in 1841. Jane felt very strong to leave England. Jane left Liverpool, England 21 Mar 1843, aboard the ship &amp;quot;Claiborne&amp;quot; bound for New Orleans USA. Joseph S. Burgess w
2 CONC as ship master and they arrived at New Orleans 13 May 1843. The passenger list contains the following information: Jane Wibly, age 64, Female; no occupation; from England; destination, New Orleans; number of packs of luggage 4.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; This was a time of religious intolerance. Antagonism was soon stirred up against the Latter Day Saints. Mobs dispossessed them. They were compelled to move, leaving behind their homes, well cultivated farms, a
2 CONC nd their house of worship. The base of the church had to move to Nauvoo, Illinois. It was a desolate spot on the Mississippi River. They purchased a large tract of swamp land. They drained this swampy area and built what was at that time the larges
2 CONC t city in Illinois. &amp;quot;the beautiful.&amp;quot; Missionary efforts, both in America and England, greatly increased their numbers, and they prospered for a season. Nauvoo soon became the largest and most prosperous city in Illinois.&lt;/p&gt;
2 CONC &lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Jane traveled up the Mississippi river by boat to Nauvoo, Illinois. Jane wrote many letters home to her family while in Nauvoo. She continued to encourage them to join her at Nauvoo. She became wel
2 CONC l acquainted with the elders and wrote of the Prophet Joseph Smith that he had any expressions of joy and pleasure and she felt he was truly a prophet of the Lord. She said he had a &amp;quot;wonderful smile and bright countenance,&amp;quot; as sh
2 CONC e related life among the LDS Saints.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; However, the peace of Nauvoo was short-lived. Again bitter religious intolerance led to persecution. In 1844, Joseph Smith and his brother Hyrum were im
2 CONC prisoned under false charges and they went willingly to Carthage Jail, Illinois. While in jail awating trial under the guaranteed protection of the state, they were shot and killed 27 Jun 1844. by armed mob with painted faces.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; After the murder of Joseph Smith and his brother, the Mormons were forced to flee mob violence once more. The persecutions of the Saints continued several more years. It finally reached a point where the Govern
2 CONC or could not assure their safety and he issued an edict to them. &amp;quot;They should be gone before the first blade of grass broke through in the Spring.&amp;quot; With the militia eage to torch homes and oust them with musket and bayonet, ther
2 CONC e was little choice but to go.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Brigham Young succeeded to the leadership of the Church after the death of Joseph Smith. It was under his direction that the Latter Day Saints left Illinois a
2 CONC nd made their historic and tragic trek to the Rocky Mountains. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Beginning 04 Feb 1846, the Mormon pioneers worked day and night to cross the broad expanse of the Mississippi River on flatbo
2 CONC ats, skiffs, and some old&amp;nbsp;lighters (flat bottomed barges). They sold all of their possessions for mere pennies on the dollar. They left behind a progressive and thriving community and a beautiful Temple. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; When the temperature dropped, the river froze, thus allowing for a steady stream of wagons and pioneers to cross the ice. The first families began crossing the frozen Mississippi on that same frigid February day, with tempe
2 CONC ratures barely above zero. They began gathering for a camp at Sugar Creek in Iowa territory, about nine miles from Nauvoo. Nine babies were born that first freezing night at Sugar Creek, some in tents, others in wagons, in rain, sleet, and snow. &l
2 CONC t;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The Saints gathered at Sugar Creek, about six miles from the Mississippi River and on 01 Mar 1846. Brigham Young led 500 wagons westward from the camp through the snow and mud.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;
2 CONC p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The main body of the group reached Council Bluffs on the banks of the Missouri River on 14 Jun 1846 marking the first stage of the larger journey to the Great Basin. Because of their hasty departure an
2 CONC d skimpy provisions, lack of vegetables, many of the party fell ill from scurvy, malaria, pneumonia, fatigue, and cholera. The struggle through Iowa over soft and marshy ground, up and down constant hills wore at the wagons and strength of the peop
2 CONC le and their sprint.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; They were exhausted, nearly out of supplies, short on cash and trying to face the fact that they could not cross the mountains in a year. They established Sugar Creek a
2 CONC nd Mount Pisgah as way stations for those to ill, weak or poor to continue with the journey. In their weakened state, many of the Saints fell and they died. Burials were conducted everyday. It was there that our brave ancestor, Jane Danby Webley di
2 CONC ed in the coldness of 1846. She was laid to rest at the Garden Grove Cemetery in Mount Pisgah, Iowa. Due to the hardship and the poor health of more members, it became apparent they would not reach the Rocky Mountains that year.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Sarah (Hurst) Jones, a great granddaughter wrote in 1964: &amp;quot;My great grandmother accepted the Gospel in the early days of the Church when the first missionaries came to England. She left her home, fam
2 CONC ily, loved ones, and friends to emigrate to the new world. She settled in Nauvoo, Illinois and became acquanted with the Prophet Joseph Smith and other church leaders. She wrote many letters to her children back home telling them about the prophet
2 CONC , the church and beautiful city of Nauvoo and the many advantages in the new world. She urged them to come to Zion and join with the saints. She suffered the persecutions and trials when their beloved prophet and his brother Hyrum, were martyred. S
2 CONC he did not live to see her children when they came to America. She started to go to&amp;nbsp;Utah in the Job Smith Company, contracted cholera and died. She was buried at Mount Pisgah in April 1846. She left a strong testimony of the divinity of th
2 CONC e Church of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints.&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; A second great granddaughter, Florence Wade Neil wrote the following. &amp;quot;William and Susannah Webley Hurst learned about th
2 CONC e gospel of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints through the efforts and testimony of Jane Danby Webley. She was the mother of Susannah and was converted to Mormonism in 1841.... She bore strong testimony of the truthfulness of the Church and tried t
2 CONC o help her family to Illinois, where she became acquainted with the Prophet Joseph Smith.&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; A copy of a letter in the possession of Mrs. William W. Wade, RFD #3, Ogden, Weber, Uta
2 CONC h from Jane Danby Webley reads as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Nauvoo, April 29th 1844&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dear Friend,&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I received a letter in which it was stated that you thought I was dead, but from this you will learn that I am i
2 CONC n the land of the living, a Pilgrim travelling toward the Celestial City and in a land of liberty enjoying health and strength and among a people who are blessed of the Lord. Nauvoo is a place that is increasing very fast fulfilling the words of th
2 CONC e prophet Isaiah. Bring sons from afar among the ends of the earth. In fact, they are gathering very much more than anyone could imagine. Times are very dull here just now, but in the course of another month there will be plenty of work; yet no on
2 CONC e will starve here, money or no money. Everyone has a little. Money is a thing that is not to be had here for they are generally poor and pay in trade which answers for the present.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I bear testimony that Joseph Smith is a prophe
2 CONC t of the Lord and we intend to make him president of the United States, that the word of the Lord may be fulfilling which to say &amp;quot;The law shall go forth of Zion and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem.&amp;quot; I don&amp;#39;t know wher
2 CONC e sister Foster is, she has left Nauvoo, but her father and mother are well. And brother Moss is well. Also sister Bundy and the family are well and Job Smith is well. I was glad to hear from&amp;nbsp;you all and hope I shall have the pleasure of s
2 CONC eeing you all here in a short time. Thanks for the letter to daughter Mary. Will write to me to inform how all the family is and little Joseph. I am sorry to hear of Ann&amp;#39;s thigh being broken.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I remain your affectionate mot
2 CONC her. Give my love to all inquiring friends and more especially the house hold of faith. I was glad to hear of Susannah and Wm. being baptized and Abraham Webley and Wife.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; To consider, Jane&
2 CONC amp;#39;s remarkable experience brings tears to the eyes of descendants who reflect upon the faith, hardships, and deprivations which Jane experienced-all for the gospel&amp;#39;s sake. It is a story that needs to be remembered and retold to all fu
2 CONC ture generations of Danby descendants. They have an ancestor who at an advanced age, tried to find a new beginning, weathered the worst and remained true and faithful to the end of her life.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
