2 TITL The Will of Robert Moon
1 _OID 2de03a94-d555-428d-97f2-26855cd904b3
1 DATE 28 Jan 1691
1 PLAC Whinneyfield House, Woodplumpton, Lancashire, Kingdom of England
1 _DSCR Extract from The Moons of Amounderness
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Robert Moon the elder brother, died 1691/92 at &lt;/span&gt;&
2 CONC lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Whinneyfield house. In his will dated 28 January, 1691/92 he is described as yeoman, of Whinneyfield in Woodplumpton; &amp;ldquo;First and most principally I give and commend my soule into ye handes of all mig
2 CONC hty God my maker, hopeing in him to have free pardon of all my sines and I comitt my body to ye earth to be buried at the discretion of my executors . . . &amp;rdquo; His messuage with appurtenances &amp;ldquo;commonly called by ye name of ye Whinn
2 CONC eyfield house tenement, situate in Wood&amp;shy; plumpton,&amp;rdquo; was held on lease from the Warrens of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Poynton and the lease had been renewed to Robert&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="
2 CONC font-family: times;"&gt;Moon, 23 March, 1687/88. This he conveyed to trustees Iohn Miller of Cottam, yeoman, and Henry Threlfall of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Plumpton quarter in Woodplumpton, yeoman. One &lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span style="font-family: times;"&gt;half of the tenement was for the use of Ellen Moon, his now wife for life, but if she remarried or died the issues were to go to his son John and daughters Isabel and Elizabeth Moon until ]ohn came of age (21), a
2 CONC nd then limited to his issue, with priority of male over &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;female children and the elder before the younger. The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;other half of the Whinneyfiel
2 CONC d was for the use of John, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Isabel, and Elizabeth, for their maintenance until ]ohn &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;was 21 years old and then to the issue of John, male befor
2 CONC e female, elder before younger. When John was 21 he to pay &amp;pound;20 to Isabel his sister, and when Elizabeth&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;was 21 she also received &amp;pound;20; if either died, &amp;pound;10 wa
2 CONC s to be paid to the surviving sister in lieu of &amp;pound;20. If&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;John died without issue then the trustees were to pay &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;the profits t
2 CONC o Ann Rossall, daughter of Robert Moon, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;the testator, and to Isabel and Elizabeth his daughters. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Ellen his wife was sole executor. The residu
2 CONC e was given equally &amp;lsquo;to Iohn&amp;lsquo;, &amp;lsquo;Isabel and Elizabeth. Ann, who married Rossall, was his eldest child, perhaps she had already received her portion on marriage. She was baptized 25 ]uly, 1669, Isabel 12 March, 1671/2; J
2 CONC ohn, 11 May, 1677; Elizabeth, 8 February 1684/85.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;
2 CONC </line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;The inventory was made 19 October, 1697, by William Whalley of Broughton, Thomas Grimbaldeston&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;of Bryning, Robert Charnley of Myerscough, and john &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Miller of Cottam. Total &amp;pound;210 95. 6d. The stock con&amp;shy; sisted of 4 cows
2 CONC , 2 bullocks, 2 lesser bullocks, 1 heifer,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;3 calves, 1 gelding, a two year old colt, 1 mare, a colt and a pig; husbandry gear included 3 carts, a pair of shod wheels, an old pair of sho
2 CONC d wheels, 2 harrows, a plough and irons, 2 ladders, 2 pitchforks, an ark and 12 sacks in the barn. In Whinneyfield house the rooms &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;mentioned are: beds and bedding in the new chamber, &lt;/span&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;the same in the middle chamber; bed stocks and bedd&amp;shy;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;ing in the room above the stairs; bedding and one pair of bedstocks in the chamber above; pres
2 CONC umably a four bay house containing on ground level a kitchen, a but&amp;shy; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;tery, middle chamber and new chamber, and the old &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;chamber or hous
2 CONC e. The kitchen or fire house had a table, chairs and odd things valued at &amp;pound;1 16s. 6d.; pewter &amp;pound;1; earthenware 45. 6d.; wooden vessels &amp;pound;1 8s. 0d; turf and coals, &amp;pound;1 10s. 0d. His apparel,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;linen and woollen &amp;pound;2; money upon bond &amp;pound;96, and one tack of ground, &amp;pound;20.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;The absence of a milk market permitted onl
2 CONC y four &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;or five cows for milk, butter and cheese, and few beef &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;cattle; horses were clearly for traction and riding: the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span s
2 CONC tyle="font-family: times;"&gt;day of the ox had passed. The average yeoman&amp;rsquo;s house&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;had three or four bays, cruck built, straw thatched, with &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-f
2 CONC amily: times;"&gt;70&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;wattle and daub walls; some were of brick, containing on the ground floor, a house or hall, parlour, kitchen, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;bu
2 CONC ttery and sometimes an additional chamber, with &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;corresponding rooms upstairs for beds and storage.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;<
2 CONC /line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
