2 TITL Whittington Church
1 _OID 2ed4b6c4-014d-491e-9ccc-f1ae28305ea8
1 PLAC St Michael the Archangel, Whittington, Lancashire
1 _DSCR Both Richard Jackson and his son-in-law Thomas Bouch were Rector of Whittington. Whittington church stands on the mound of a Norman motte and bailey castle. Arkholme and Melling, nearby in the Lune valley, are also associated with these ancient sit
2 CONC es. Fragments of stone in the churchyard date back to this period. A church is believed to have been on the site since about 1200. However, the present church dates from the late C15 or early C16. The oldest part of St Michael's is the tower. It ri
2 CONC ses to fifty feet and is a prominent landmark from the west across the River Lune. The diagonal buttresses have seven set-offs between the base and the embattled parapet. On the west face is a door, a window, and between the latter and the bell ope
2 CONC ning, a statuary niche with a Victorian figure of the Good Shepherd. The tower contains a peal of 6 bells. 
