1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;John Moon was the author of a number of works&amp;nbsp;&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;published as tracts and broadsides. In 1654 he wrote &amp;lsquo;The Ranter&amp;rsquo;s Last Sermon&amp;rsquo; at the inception of his&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;miss
2 CONC ion and in 1657 &amp;lsquo;The True Light hath made Mani&amp;shy; fest Darknesse, or Sion builded up and Babylon cast &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;downe. With a true Answer of what the Baptist Teachers objected against the S
2 CONC ervant of the Lord who &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;in Scorn is called a Quaker, and known to the World&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;by the name of Iohn Moon in several places in Wales. etc.&
2 CONC amp;rsquo; (London 1657). Other works without date or nlace &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;are "The Great Trumpet of the Lord God Almighty &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;of Heaven and Earth blown&amp;rdqu
2 CONC o; etc. &amp;ldquo;A Tesuitical Designe discovered in a piece called &amp;lsquo;The Quaker&amp;rsquo;s Pedigree&amp;rsquo; or&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;a Dialogue between a Quaker and a Jesuit.&amp;rsquo; and &am
2 CONC p;ldquo;The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;True Light which shines in the Heart is that which &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;mamifests the Tree of Knowledge from the Tree of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: times;"&gt;Life, the one is not for food and the other is the true &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;food. Your dear Brother Iohn Moon. whose life is in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tim
2 CONC es;"&gt;the Lamb&amp;rdquo;. John Moon was one of the Friends who with William Penn and members of Iudge Fell&amp;rsquo;s family &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;of Swarthmoor hall testified their annrobation in a signed documen
2 CONC t to the marriage of Ceorsre For and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Margaret the widow of Tndqe Fell. (Cromwell&amp;rsquo;s former vice-chancellor of the duchy of Lancaster) in 1669.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><
2 CONC line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Moon wa
2 CONC s probably twice married, his second wife Margaret was the widow of Thomas Harrison of Carr house, Garstang. She was a daughter of John Townson of Chapel house, VVyresdale, and sister of the cele&amp;shy; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: t
2 CONC imes;"&gt;brated Jennet Cragg. The latter rode on horseback &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;from Quernmore to London and back on a galloway &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;returning with her two orphaned gr
2 CONC andchildren each &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;in a pannier. They were John and Joseph Kelsall, the former became a schoolmaster in Wales. He wrote a eulogy of his grandmother as &amp;lsquo;one who loved Truth and truly estee
2 CONC med the faithful friends thereof.&amp;rsquo; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;John Moon was executor of his father&amp;mdash;in-lawalong&amp;nbs
2 CONC p;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;with John Townson, junior, of Bawcliffe, in 1671. In&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;his latter years he lived at Carr house, Garstang. This&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;dwelling, greatly altered, still exists on the east side of t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;he old main road north of Garstang near its junction&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="fon
2 CONC t-family: times;"&gt;with the A6. Dilworth Abbott (Quaker Annals of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Fylde, 1921),considered that it was here in the orchard&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"
2 CONC &gt;George Fox held his &amp;lsquo;General Meeting&amp;rsquo; in 1657 on his&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;way to Cheshire, "and soe,&amp;rdquo; he says in his journal, &amp;ldquo;from&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: times;"&gt;thence wee past through ye country and had many&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;pretious meetings in severall places and came to&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times
2 CONC ;"&gt;Preston, and many come gazing about us, and I had&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;a General Meeting betwixt that (Preston) and Lancaster.&amp;rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;I
2 CONC n 1683 Thomas Butler of Kirkland prosecuted John Moon for default of tithe payment. A verdict was given against him for &amp;pound;4 7s. 0d. and this was trebled according to the &amp;lsquo;Statute of Treble Damage&amp;rsquo;. The bailiff seized al
2 CONC l the corn in the barn and on the ground, as well as all the household goods valued at &amp;pound;35, for the sum of &amp;pound;13 155. 0d., not even a bed was left in the house. (Nightingale &amp;lsquo;Early Stages of the Quaker Movement,&amp;rdqu
2 CONC o; London, 1921).&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;John Moon died at the Carr house 1689 and was buried 27 Nov. in the field near Eccleston. This ground was conveyed by William Brewer, a faithful friend of Little Eccles
2 CONC ton, in May, 1669, to Thomas Moon of Woodplumpton, brother of John, and John White of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;17&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Great Eccleston for a burial groun
2 CONC d for the Friends. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;It was often referred to as Brewers Yard. A meeting &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;house was built here, but with the decline of Quakerism &lt;/span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;it was converted into a cottage, the ground sold in 1906 and a new house built, the garden being the old grave yard.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><
2 CONC line>&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
