2 TITL John Moon The Quaker
1 _OID 2f5cb3c3-0d1f-463d-8dc2-15a166e613df
1 DATE abt 1650
1 PLAC Eaves, Lancashire, England
1 _DSCR John Moon
2 CONT Early persecution of Quakers in England 
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;During the year 1652 a man by the name of George Fox, son of the Leicesterhire weaver and apprenticed to a shomaker, visited the North West in his efforts as a missionary t
2 CONC o find converts.&amp;nbsp; It was during this time he had a meeting in Firbank Fell near Sedbergh.&amp;nbsp; In September&amp;nbsp; 1652 he was at Lancaster where many 'Seekers' from Amounderness joined his movement despite violent and relentless o
2 CONC pposition.&amp;nbsp; Alexander Paker of Lees from the Lancaster region Thomas Briggs of Boltonle-Sands, and Richard Hubberthorne of Yealand Conyers, John Lawson also of Lancaster, visited the Fvlde as 'first Publishers of Truth'. Edward Moon and hi
2 CONC s wife Isabel with sons John, Thomas and Edward joined the Quaker cause in 1653 and became ardent propagandists, all sufferiing for their faith. John Moone the eldest son of Edward was a currier and lived at the Tan House farm at Eaves, as well a
2 CONC s a small farmer in Eaves quarter. He was a writer and preacher.&amp;nbsp; His refusal to pay tithes, and swear oaths soon led to a conflict with the church and the magistrates.&amp;nbsp; In 1654 he, his father Edward, along with twenty-five others
2 CONC , were committed to prison because they esteemed it their Christian duty to exhort priests and people to repentance and amendment of life.&amp;nbsp; This resulted in persistent persecution.&amp;nbsp; In 1660 John Moon was arrested with John White
2 CONC , William and John Thompson, Henry Eccles, Christopher Parkinson and William Gibson, all were in Lancaster gaol. (Lancaster Castle used as a prison formerly military use and before that in 79 AD the site of a Roman fort) While imprisoned here whil
2 CONC e John lay a whole winter in 1776, in his own account of his sufferings he says "the Lord was with me in prison and made me mmore to rejoice then those that have abundance of riches of corn and wine and oil."&amp;nbsp; In John's life he endured man
2 CONC y sufferings along with disputations because of his commitment to what he believed.&amp;nbsp; Along the way he authored a number of works, published tracts and broadsides.&amp;nbsp; In 1654 he wrote "The Ranter's Last Sermon' at the inception of hi
2 CONC s mission and in 1657 "The True Light had made Manifest Darknesse, or Sion Building up and Babylon cast downe.&amp;nbsp; With a true Answer of what the Baptist Teachers objected against the Servant of the Lord who is Scorn is called a Quaker, and K
2 CONC nown to the World by the name of John Moon in several places in Wales.&amp;nbsp; Other works without date or place include, 'The Great Trumpet of the Lord God Almighty of Heaven and Earth blown" &amp;nbsp; "The Quaker's Pedigree" or dialogue betwee
2 CONC n a Quaker and a Jesuit." &amp;nbsp; "The True Light which shines in the Heart is that which manifests the Tree of Knowledge from the Tree of Life, the one is not for food and the other is the true food.&amp;nbsp; Your dear Brother John Moon whos
2 CONC e life is in the Lamb"&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;In 1683 Thomas Butler of Kirkland prosecuted John Moon for default of tithe payment.&amp;nbsp; A verdict was given against him for L
2 CONC 4 7s. Od. and this was trebled according to the 'Statute of Treble Damage'. The bailiff seized all the corn in the barn and on the ground, as well as all the houshold goods valued at L35, for the sum of L13 15s. Od., not even a bed was left in th
2 CONC e house.&amp;nbsp; (Nightingale 'Early Stages of the Quaker Movement," London, 1921) &amp;nbsp; John Moon died at the Carr house 1689 and was buried 27 Nov. in the field near Eccleston.&amp;nbsp; This ground was conveyed by William Brewer, a faithf
2 CONC ul friend of Little Eccleston, in May, 1669, to Thomas Moon of Woodplumpton, brother of John. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
