2 CONC f 1868. Temperance helped nurse Lydia during her illness. Henry&amp;rsquo;s descendants said the three wives got along well and helped each other. Temperance told her daughter, Olive Moon Potter, that Lydia always treated her with love and kindnes
2 CONC s and was like a mother to her in teaching and helping her in many ways.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;Map of Farmington Moon homes.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;Henry moved from Salt Lake City to Farmington early in 1870, but his counselo
2 CONC r Joseph Warburton wasn&amp;rsquo;t appointed Bishop until November 14 of that year. Henry owned a large fruit orchard in Farmington. Fruit trees were among the first things planted in that area. Lots of mulberry trees were planted in Farmington i
2 CONC n 1870 west of Main Street and south of Shepherd Lane, which would have been next to Henry&amp;rsquo;s land. The LDS Church experimented with the silk making process for a number of years.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Henry was called and set apart to serv
2 CONC e a mission to the United States, October 9, 1871. He was gone for four and one half months. During the cold winter months of 1871 and 1872, Henry served his mission in Iowa and Missouri. He returned home to Farmington, February 22, 1872. His journ
2 CONC al records his travels during his mission.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;In the Wednesday, April 10, 1872 edition of The Evening News, the fourth day of General Conference was reported. Henry Moon was the first speaker in the Tuesday afternoon session, and h
2 CONC e said: &amp;ldquo;I was one of the missionaries who were called to go to the United States last fall. In my travels I met with Mr. David Whitmer, one of the witnesses of the Book of Mormon. What made me visit him was, I was reading the testimony o
2 CONC f the witnesses to some people on Shoal Creek, in Caldwell County. One of them, Mr. John Lefler, was very anxious to see one of these witnesses, and to hear his testimony. I went down to Richmond with him. We got to Mr. David Whitmer&amp;rsquo;
2 CONC s a little after dark. I told Mr. Whitmer that I was from Utah. &amp;lsquo;From Utah?&amp;rsquo; said he. &amp;lsquo;Yes Sir.&amp;rsquo; &amp;lsquo;Well, you have a good deal of trouble, I suppose, in Utah?&amp;rsquo; Oh, not very much, I told him
2 CONC . He got up from his supper and went out of the house, and I followed him. I told him I wanted to have a few moments&amp;rsquo; talk with him. He said he had not time, he wanted to see after some horses, and his son was sick. But I hung to him, an
2 CONC d followed him in the street, and told him that this gentlemen, Mr. Lefler, who was with me, had come from Caldwell County, to see if that which was written in the Book of Mormon &amp;mdash; the testimony of the witnesses &amp;mdash; was true. Mr
2 CONC . Whitmer turned round to the Gentleman and said: &amp;lsquo;God Almighty requires at my hands to bear record of the truth of the Book of Mormon. That book is a true record; it is the pure gospel of Jesus Christ, translated by the gift and power o
2 CONC f God through Joseph Smith.&amp;rsquo; He then talked to Mr. Lefler, who also asked him a few questions. Then Mr. Whitmer talked a little to me about Utah. I asked the gentleman if he wanted any more conversation with Mr. Whitmer? He said, &amp;lsq
2 CONC uo;No,&amp;rsquo; he was quite satisfied, and we got on to the cars and went back to Caldwell County the same evening; &amp;ldquo;I am glad that I, with my brethren, can also bear witness with regard to the truth of the Book of Mormon and the estab
2 CONC lishment of the kingdom of God upon the earth, and that this is the kingdom established through Joseph Smith and Brigham Young. I am as satisfied of it, and I know it as well as I know that I am talking to this large congregation. Amen.&amp;rdquo;&
2 CONC lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;Moon Park in Farmington, UtahThe Primary was organized for the children of the church on August 25, 1878 right there in Farmington by Aurelia Spencer Rogers. Sister Rogers recorded the names of all 224 children who atte
2 CONC nded the first meeting. Temperance and Mary Ann both had children who attended this historic event. The names of Temperance&amp;rsquo;s children who attended are: Rowene, 13; Henry, 10; Edmund, 8; Philip, 6; and Mary Ann&amp;rsquo;s children: Orson
2 CONC , 9 and Amanda, 8.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Henry moved Mary Ann and her family to Woodland, Summit County, Utah in 1881. Henry&amp;rsquo;s second son, Joseph Henry purchased land there in Bench Creek in 1874. The family had another farm to sta
2 CONC rt so the whole family &amp;mdash; Lydia and Temperance&amp;rsquo;s children along with Mary Ann&amp;rsquo;s &amp;mdash; worked together cutting brush and picking rocks to clear the land. Henry claimed a homestead in Woodland for the purpose of goi
2 CONC ng into the stock business. His land lay along the Provo River in Summit County. It was the ideal place for cattle and sheep because of plenty of free range grass. Woodland proved to be better for ranching and timbering than farming because of it
2 CONC s short growing season. The 6,580 foot elevation meant hard cold winters. It wasn&amp;rsquo;t rare to have the fences covered up with snow.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Getting back and forth from Farmington to Woodland was sometimes very difficul
2 CONC t during the winter months. The first families in the area took up land claims on the south side of the Provo River called Bench Creek in Wasatch County. Before long, people lived on both sides of the river that ran down through the valley, which a
2 CONC cted as a dividing line for the two counties of Wasatch and Summit. Woodland was a six-mile strip of farmsteads on the north side of the Provo River. The mile-wide valley provided fertile ground to raise feed for their animals. Mary Ann&amp;rsquo;
2 CONC s parents, John and Sidney Thayn(e), probably moved from Salt Lake to Woodland sometime during this time. Many of the same family names in the Salt Lake 1st Ward Records show up in the Woodland Ward Records. Woodland was near the forested mountain
2 CONC s that supplied trees for John Thayn(e)&amp;rsquo;s steam saw mill and the lumber business.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;When the time came for a ward to be organized in Woodland, Daniel H. Wells presided at a meeting held in a two-room cabin on Sunday, Jul
2 CONC y 24, 1881. He created the new ward and suggested they name the ward &amp;ldquo;Woodland&amp;rdquo; because of all the cottonwood trees that covered the valley. Elder Wells ordained and set apart Henry to the office of Bishop. John Thomas Moon, Hen
2 CONC ry&amp;rsquo;s oldest son, and William R. Smith were sustained as his counselors.While Henry served as Bishop in Woodland, he organized the Sunday School, YMMIA, and Primary. The Relief Society was already functioning.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;</line><li
2 CONC ne>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&amp;nbsp;Moon Circle, Farmington, UtahAt one time Henry wanted to move Temperance and her family to Woodland so he could have the two families together, bu
2 CONC t Temperance was comfortable in her four room adobe home in Farmington. She loved her roses, trees and garden and couldn&amp;rsquo;t bear to leave. Even though Temperance chose to stay in Farmington, it is likely that Henry took the children back a
2 CONC nd forth from Woodland to Farmington on his various trips. Temperance&amp;rsquo;s son Phillip said in his later years, he would rather go to Woodland than Disneyland. He had very fond memories of his early adventures in Woodland.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt
2 CONC ;Henry owned big orchards and lots of land in Farmington and Woodland so his children were all taught to work in their youth. There were no idle hands around the homes. Hours were never set for the children, but they worked early and late. In Woodl
2 CONC and, the children attended school in one room log building with one teacher. Amanda said, &amp;ldquo;I think it is a wonder one did secure any education at all under the circumstances.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The Edmunds Act was passed in the U.S. Congr
2 CONC ess on March 14, 1882 aimed at trying to stop the Mormons from practicing polygamy. Polygamy was now officially against the law and U.S. Marshals were sent to arrest those breaking it. The fact that Temperance and Mary Ann lived in distant towns a
2 CONC t that time was probably good.&lt;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear
2 CONC : both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;br&gt;Henry suffered a stroke in 1888, a year after his last baby, Nephi, was born and a y
2 CONC ear before the Manifesto was issued. The stroke rendered Henry an invalid for the last six years of his life. All Nephi remembered about his father was &amp;ldquo;he was quite feeble.&amp;rdquo; What a hardship this must have been for the two wive
2 CONC s who still had young children in their care. No government aid such as Social Security was available to them. After Henry had the stroke, he remained in Farmington with his wife, Temperance, who nursed him. Henry Moon&amp;rsquo;s undaunted spiri
2 CONC t finally gave up and passed from this world to the next on November 14, 1894 at the age of seventy-five. Henry was buried in the Farmington City Cemetery next to his son Franklin.&lt;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: cente
2 CONC r;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>Henry tol
2 CONC d his daughter Amanda, &amp;ldquo;All my life has been spent in service for others and I am not sorry for it, for after all we get the most pleasure in doing good.&amp;rdquo; In Henry&amp;rsquo;s words, he says, &amp;ldquo;I am happy and I want yo
2 CONC u to be happy. I am for peace, rightness, happiness and every other principle that is for the happifying [sic] of the human family and to increase and grow in the knowledge of the truth and to rise until I attain to the perfect stature of a man i
2 CONC n Christ. How can that be done? By being faithful obedient to those holding the holy priesthood, keeping the commandments of God in all things. We are only sent here on a mission. We ought to fulfill that mission aright. The old ship Zion will lan
2 CONC d us safe if we will keep on board, but a great many jump off and that is the reason they get lost. If I had jumped off when I came to New York, the captain could not have landed me safe. It is just so with those that obey the gospel.&amp;rdquo;&lt
2 CONC ;br&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="clear: both; text-align: center;"&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;em&gt;Written by: Maureen Moon LaPray December 1999. It can be found in the book&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style="color: #936307;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Family of Henry Moon: Mormon Pioneer 1819-1894.&lt;/em&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
