2 TITL Jane Elizabeth Swift Van Tassell
1 _OID 316dce72-ffe0-4588-bc41-3adf111119ac
1 _DSCR Story by daughter, Annie and from the DUP of pioneer histories. 
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;Jane Elizabeth Swift&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;25 May 1847&amp;mdash;2 March 1924&lt;/p&gt;</line><l
2 CONC ine>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;Written by Annie L. Millard, daughter&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;JANE ELIZABETH SWIFT VAN TASSELL was born in Kael Hoek, Sout
2 CONC h Africa, May 25th, 1847. Her father, William Swift, was of English parents and her mother, Ann Toen Swift, was of English and Irish parents. She came to American in the year 1863; landed in New York on May 25th, her 16th birthday. They arrived i
2 CONC n due time at the Missouri River: her mother, one sister, six brothers and herself. There were others who came with them from Africa and, having means enough, they formed an independent company. A man by the name of Patterson, I have forgotten hi
2 CONC s first name, was captain of the company. They landed in Salt Lake City in September that same year and settled in the First Ward. She met father, James Dailey Van Tassell, and they were married in the old Endowment House December 14th, 1867. Of th
2 CONC at union ten children were born, six of whom are living at the present time. My mother was always a hard worker at home and in the church. I remember her as a Relief Society Secretary. She was a great worker among the sick, also a big help to the n
2 CONC eedy. I can remember her going out to help among the sick at any time and all hours. She also did lots of work in laying out the dead and helping to make their burial clothes. She would help to furnish part of the clothes, if not all, in a great ma
2 CONC ny cases for the poor people. She worked and supported herself and four children while my father filled a mission. In 1889 my parents moved to Woodland, Wasatch County. Woodland lies partly in Wasatch County and partly in Summit County. They were o
2 CONC n the Summit side but belonged to Wasatch Stake. Mother was the same good-hearted woman there as she was in Salt Lake City, only worked more among the sick and was reckoned the settlement doctor for years in confinement and diseases. She was Relie
2 CONC f Society President for a number of years until her health failed and the family moved to Riverton. They lived there about nine years. My mother made lots of friends wherever she went and in Riverton she was a Relief Society member and a teacher u
2 CONC p to the time of her death, which occurred last March 2nd, 1924. She was a good Latter-day Saint to the last and great believer in tithing and fast offerings along with other donations when called for.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bott
2 CONC om: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;Annie L. Millard&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;
2 CONC /p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;Biography of Jane Elizabeth Swift Van Tassell&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;From the DUP Collection of Pioneer Hi
2 CONC stories&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;Jane Elizabeth&amp;rsquo;s father was serving the English Army in South Africa when she was born. He was very successful in making boots and shoes. Later h
2 CONC e owned a diamond mine. The first LDS Missionaries arrived in South Africa in 1853. The Swift family accepted the message that the heavens are not sealed and God has spoken and manifested his will through a living prophet. Jane and her mother wer
2 CONC e baptized as members on January 3, 1856. They wanted to join the saints and were very nervous about emigrating to America. Her father did not accompany his wife and eight children. He gave them plenty of money to travel in comfort. They sailed o
2 CONC n the ship Henry Ellis from Cape Town. They were two months on the ocean before they arrived in New York on Jane&amp;rsquo;s sixteenth birthday in 1863. They went by train to Missouri where they and other saints from South Africa organized an indep
2 CONC endent wagon company with Captain Patterson in charge. Jane endured well the hardships of the dusty pioneer trail, walking most of the way following the wagon train. They arrived in the Salt Lake Valley on September 4, 1863. Even though Jane ha
2 CONC d a trunk full of silk dresses, she wanted a gingham dress like the girls in Salt Lake City wore. Jane got a job sewing and soon had a gingham dress to wear to dances, she was never so happy. Jane was a very good friend of Maude Adams who lived acr
2 CONC oss the street from her. When Maude went on the stage, Jane donated her trunk full of silk dresses for the cast. Jane Elizabeth married James Dailey Van Tassell in the Salt Lake Endowment House on December 14, 1867, sealed for time and eternity b
2 CONC y Joseph F. Smith. They settled in the First Ward and ten children were born to them. Annie Louisa was born November 29, 1868; James Henry was born on March 2, 1871 and died September 17, 1872. Frances Amelia was born March 8, 1873; William Heber b
2 CONC orn March 5, 1875 and George Daily on September 18, 1877. In September of 1878, her husband accepted a mission call. Jane was left to care for her four small children, milk the cow, feed some chickens and a pig, care for a small orchard, and dry th
2 CONC e fruit to sell. She was Relief Society Secretary and a great worker among the sick and needy. She started nursing, having no previous experience. She was a very spiritual woman and always prayed for guidance and wisdom. She claimed the Lord neve
2 CONC r failed her. Her husband returned home November 1, 1879. They lost their four year old son William on November 19, 1879; three year old George died March 11, 1880. Henry Owen was born August 27, 1880; John Alfred on September 15, 1882; Emily Henri
2 CONC etta on April 10, 1885; Charles Vincent on July 5, 1887; Joseph Clyde was born January 15, 1892. In 1889 they moved to Woodland, Wasatch County. She was Relief Society President for many years. She served in the capacity of mid-wife many times an
2 CONC d was recognized as the Woodland Settlement Doctor. For many years she waited on patients from Bench Creek to Wanship and in Park City. She was called to treat all kinds of sicknesses and to set broken bones. Jane was remembered as an excellent hom
2 CONC emaker. She left her testimony for others to remember the hardships she endured pioneering, but never regretted it. When her health failed, they moved to Riverton and lived there nine years until her death in 1924.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
