2 CONC "1920"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The current site of the Golden Spike National Historic Site, with replicas of No. 119 and the &lt;em&gt;Jupiter&lt;/em&gt; facing each other to re-enact th
2 CONC e driving of the Golden Spike.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id=".22Golden_Spike_Days_Celebration.22_.281939.29" class="mw-headline"&gt;"Golden Spik
2 CONC e Days Celebration" (1939)&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="width: 177px;"&gt;&lt;img class="thumbimage" src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Union_Pacific_World_Premiere_1939.jpg/175px-Union_Pacific_World_Premiere_
2 CONC 1939.jpg" alt="" width="175" height="236" data-file-width="1226" data-file-height="1653"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="width: 177px;"&gt;</line><line>&l
2 CONC t;div&gt;Golden Spike Days program, Omaha, 1939&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;An elaborate four-day event called the &lt;em&gt;"Golden Spike Days Celebration"&lt;/em&gt; was held in Omaha, NE, fr
2 CONC om April 26 to 29, 1939, to commemorate the 70th anniversary of the joining of the UP and CPRR rails and driving of the Last Spike at Promontory Summit, UT, in 1869. The center piece event of the celebration occurred on April 28 with the World Prem
2 CONC iere of the Cecil B. DeMille feature motion picture &lt;em&gt;Union Pacific&lt;/em&gt; which took place simultaneously in the city's Omaha, Orpheum, and Paramount theaters. The film features an elaborate reenactment of the original Golden Spike cer
2 CONC emony (filmed in Canoga Park, CA) as the motion picture's closing scene for which DeMille borrowed the actual Golden Spike from Stanford University to be held by Dr. W.H. Harkness (Stanley Andrews) as he delivered his remarks prior to its driving t
2 CONC o complete the railroad. (A prop spike was used for the actual hammering sequence.)&lt;sup&gt;[21]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="width: 152px;"&gt;&lt;img class="thumbimage" src="https://upload.wikimedia.o
2 CONC rg/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Golden_Spike_Monument_at_Council_Bluffs%2C_IA.jpg/150px-Golden_Spike_Monument_at_Council_Bluffs%2C_IA.jpg" alt="" width="150" height="218" data-file-width="292" data-file-height="425"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="width: 152px;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The Golden Spike Monument, Council Bluffs, IA. 1939&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Also included as a part of the overall celebration's major attractions was the &lt;em&gt;Golden Spike Historical Exposition&lt;/em&gt;, a large assemblage of artifacts (including the Golden Spike itse
2 CONC lf), tools, equipment, photographs, documents, and other materials from the construction of the Pacific Railroad that were put on display at Omaha's Municipal Auditorium. The four days of events drew over 250,000 people to Omaha during its run, a n
2 CONC umber roughly equivalent to the city's then population.&lt;sup&gt;[22]&lt;/sup&gt; The celebration was opened by President Franklin D. Roosevelt who inaugurated it by pressing a telegraph key at the White House in Washington, DC.&lt;sup&gt;[23]&lt;
2 CONC /sup&gt;&lt;sup&gt;[24]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sup&gt;[25]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;On the same day as the premiere of the movie, a still standing gold-colored concrete spike called the "Golden Spike Monument" and measuring some 56 feet (
2 CONC 17&amp;nbsp;m) in height was also unveiled at 21st Street and 9th Avenue in Council Bluffs, IA, adjacent to the UP's main yard, the location of milepost 0.0 of that road's portion of the Pacific Railroad.&lt;sup&gt;[26]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sup&gt;[27]&l
2 CONC t;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="In_popular_culture" class="mw-headline"&gt;In popular culture&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Artwork" class="mw-headline"&gt;Artwork&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;In 2
2 CONC 012, Artist Greg Stimac used the original "golden spike", on display at the Cantor Arts Center at Stanford University, to produce a series of photograms, or cameraless photographs.&lt;sup&gt;[28]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sup&gt;[29]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</l
2 CONC ine><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Films" class="mw-headline"&gt;Films&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;The first motion picture depiction of the driving of the golden spike occurred in &lt;em&gt;The Iron Horse&
2 CONC lt;/em&gt; 1924, a silent film directed by John Ford and produced by Fox Film.&lt;sup&gt;[30]&lt;/sup&gt; In 2011, this film was deemed "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant" by the United States Library of Congress and selected f
2 CONC or preservation in the National Film Registry.&lt;sup&gt;[31]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;In the fictional action adventure comedy film &lt;em&gt;Wild Wild West&lt;/em&gt; (1999), the joining ceremony is the setting of an assassina
2 CONC tion attempt on then U.S. President Ulysses S. Grant by the film's antagonist Dr. Arliss Loveless. (In reality Grant did not attend the Golden Spike ceremony.) The extensive Promontory Summit set for the film's Golden Spike ceremony scenes was buil
2 CONC t at the 20,000-acre Cook's Ranch near Santa Fe, New Mexico.&lt;sup&gt;[32]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;The Golden Spike Ceremony is depicted in the movie adaptation of &lt;em&gt;The Lone Ranger&lt;/em&gt; (2013) starring Johnny De
2 CONC pp.&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Television" class="mw-headline"&gt;Television&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;Hell on Wheels presents a multi-season arc on the construction of the transconti
2 CONC nental railroad. In Season 5, Episode 11, a flash forward sequence includes a picture of the railroad ceremony and a main character claiming to possess a ring made of gold crafted from part of the ceremonial golden spike. &lt;sup&gt;[33]&lt;/sup&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;In Lisa's Substitute, (The Simpsons, Season 2, Episode 19, 1991), Leland Stanford's driving of the golden spike is mentioned in a brief, romantic, portrayal of the railroad in the United States. &lt;sup&gt;[34]&lt
2 CONC ;/sup&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Toys" class="mw-headline"&gt;Toys&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;In 2015, a Lego model depicting the golden spike ceremony was submitted to the Lego Ideas website.&lt;sup&gt;[
2 CONC 35]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sup&gt;[36]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Music" class="mw-headline"&gt;Music&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;Railroad Earth, a bluegrass band, frequently performs a song about the event
2 CONC , titled "The Jupiter and the 119".&lt;sup&gt;[37]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sup&gt;[38]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Trains" class="mw-headline"&gt;Trains&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;li&gt;Th
2 CONC e &lt;em&gt;Inyo&lt;/em&gt;, a 4-4-0 steam locomotive built for the Virginia &amp;amp; Truckee Railroad (V&amp;amp;T #22) in 1875 by the Baldwin Locomotive Works in Philadelphia, appeared in both the Golden Spike ceremony scene in &lt;em&gt;Union P
2 CONC acific&lt;/em&gt; (1939) and in the 1960s TV series &lt;em&gt;The Wild Wild West&lt;/em&gt;. In May, 1969, the &lt;em&gt;Inyo&lt;/em&gt; participated in the Golden Spike Centennial at Promontory, Utah, and then served as the replica of the Centra
2 CONC l Pacific's &lt;em&gt;Jupiter&lt;/em&gt; (CPRR #60) at the Golden Spike National Historical Site, until the current replica was built in 1979. Purchased by the Nevada State Railroad Museum in Carson City, Nevada, in 1974, it was eventually brough
2 CONC t back to Nevada and fully restored there in 1983, where it still runs today.&lt;sup&gt;[39]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="See_also" class="mw-headline"&gt;See also&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="m
