2 TITL Alice Smith of the Clan Helena
1 _OID 3318dc2d-aed7-4c28-8f6f-8df24ea5eb56
1 DATE 20 thousand years ago
1 PLAC From the Dordogne to Lancashire 
1 _DSCR Family history and mitochondrial DNA
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0cm 0cm 10pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: calibri; font-size: medium;"&gt;When Alice Smith was born in Lancashire in the 1800s, DNA had not even been discovered. That would take at least anothe
2 CONC r 100 years. These days, however, genetics is being used increasingly as a tool by genealogists and anthropologists. Apart from the occasional mutation every 20 thousand years, mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) passes from mother to child unchanged from on
2 CONC e generation to the next. Since only females can pass it on, it forms an unbroken chain from mother to daughter, linking us to our remote female ancestors who lived thousands of years ago. According to Professor Brian Sykes of Oxford University, al
2 CONC most all Europeans are direct descendants of only seven females, clan mothers, who lived at varying times in the distant past. In 2004, I had my mtDNA analysed by Oxford Ancestors. It turns out that I am in Clan Helena (subclave H1a) with about 45
2 CONC % of all Europeans. Mutation rates are used to work out how long ago each clan mother lived. It has been estimated that Helena lived about 20 thousand years ago during the last ice age on the border between France and Spain. Some of her descendant
2 CONC s travelled north, arriving in England about 12 thousand years ago. Famous members of the Clan include Queen Victoria, Marie Antoinette, Empress Alexandra of Russia, Prince Philip and Pharaoh Ramses. Clan Helena is also well-represented amongst th
2 CONC e Basque people of Northern Spain. Unbeknown to her, Alice Smith, my 3 x great grandmother, was also a member of Clan Helena. I carry her mitochondrial DNA. By Sue Ashford&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
