2 TITL Activities of Robert and Richard Moon in Jamaica
1 _OID 3400792a-42c8-4189-ad0c-2b764761b59c
1 PLAC Kingston, Jamaica
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;There is no clue from these wills as to the source of the sudden increase of wealth, nor of the steps which &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span sty
2 CONC le="font-family: times;"&gt;led to the establishment of sugar plantations in Jamaica&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;but it is evident they possessed considerable property&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-fam
2 CONC ily: times;"&gt;near Kingston, and a number of slaves. Robert&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Moon, son of Richard of Catforth, and grandson of John of Whinneyfield House, is the first member of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&
2 CONC gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;family so far as we know who developed a business, first in Liverpool and later in Jamaica, but it seems &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;highly probable that other relations had preced
2 CONC ed him. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;In F. Cundall&amp;rsquo;s book &amp;ldquo;The Governors of Jamaica in the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;first half of the eighteenth century&amp;rdquo; men
2 CONC tion is made of John Moone as resident in Peters Lane, Iamaica, but &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;gives no details. Possibly he came from one of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;several Catfor
2 CONC th families and may have established himself in trade and prepared the way for Robert. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Certainly Robert and his sons were well established by &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;
2 CONC the second quarter of the eighteenth century in and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;near Kingston.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;In 1780 the Woodplumpton Moons were represented &lt;/span&gt;&lt;s
2 CONC pan style="font-family: times;"&gt;by Richard of Kingston in Jamaica, merchant; Edward &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;of Kingston, Jamaica, merchant; Willam of Bartle &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;quarte
2 CONC r and of Mawdesley near Croston, elected free &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;burgesses of Lancaster 1779/80. Mary, wife of Edward&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Hodkinson, and Ann and Hannah of C
2 CONC atforth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
