2 CONC t;/strong&gt;: Heber City, Utah &lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Roger sailed to America with his father and two sisters. His mother had been hidden by her brother until the ship sailed. She joined them in America thre
2 CONC e and a half years later. His father made a home for them in Heber City in a dirt-roofed cabin. His father and his sisters, as well as himself, were very good singers and they were often called on to perform at gatherings. Roger continued to use hi
2 CONC s musical talents all his life. He played a bass viola. He provided music for choirs, dances, and programs. He was a music director for 30 years and taught many to sing and play the organ and cello. They were a healthy family as both parents and al
2 CONC l eleven children lived into their eighties and nineties. Roger followed his father's vocation as a weaver of carpets and rugs. He also engaged in farming. He was called to serve a mission in England in 1894 . He was of medium build with sandy re
2 CONC d hair. They lived in Heber all their lives, after coming to Utah . &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium
