1 _DSCR The Phelps Family of America, and Their English Ancestors, With Copies of Wills, Deeds, Letters, and Other Interesting Papers, Coats of Arms and Valuable Records, Compiled By Judge Oliver Seymour Phelps, of Portland, Oregon, and Andrew T. Servin o
2 CONC f Lenox,
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;The Phelps Family of America, and Their English Ancestors, With Copies of Wills, Deeds, Letters, and Other Interesting Papers, Coats of Arms and Valuable Records, Compiled By Judge Oliver Seymour Phelps, of Po
2 CONC rtland, Oregon, and Andrew T. Servin of Lenox, Mass., In Two Volumes., Vol. I., Published By Eagle Publishing Company of Pittsfield, Mass., 1899.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;PREFACE.&lt;/div&gt;<
2 CONC /line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;EVERYTHING pertaining to the Phelps family, and Phelps ancestry, however trifling, whether in Old England, our fore-fathers' land, or in New England, our fathers' and mothers' bir
2 CONC th-place, or elsewhere, possesses peculiar interest and pleasure for us.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;We therefore propose in this work, in connection with the genealogy, to include interesting it
2 CONC ems such as Phelps deeds, wills, signatures, and scraps of history; and Phelps photographs, houses, etc, so that all who wear the Phelps name or have the Phelps blood, in either hemisphere, may desire it as a useful and beautiful book suitable fo
2 CONC r parlor or library.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;We send it forth with full assurance that as Genealogy is as old as the Bible, which is full of it, so our book of Phelpses and their records, wil
2 CONC l supply a long felt want in our families.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;As "Our lives are but records laid on a shelf" so posterity's sons and daughters may have their ancestors' lives a
2 CONC nd records.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;And may He who setteth the Solitary in families, who established the marriage relation, gracing it in the days of his humiliation, by his presence at the W
2 CONC edding Feast in Cana of Galilee; and whose own human Genealogy is given us in the Gospels, prosper it.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;By The Authors.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt
2 CONC ;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;It gives me pleasure to acknowledge my indebtedness to Rollin H. Cooke, of Pittsfield, Mass., for assistance in the final arrangement and revision of this work.&lt;/div&
2 CONC gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;Andrew T. Servin.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;Lenox, Mass., January, 1899&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt
2 CONC ;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;DEDICATED.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;To the Memory of our Pious Pare
2 CONC nts, whose eventful and beneficent lives have been likened unto that of one of old, who said of himself,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;"When the ear heard me then it blessed me; and whe
2 CONC n the eye saw me it gave witness to me; because I delivered the poor that cried, and the fatherless, and him that had none to help him; the blessings of him that was ready to perish came upon me, and I caused the Widow's heart to sing for joy.&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;I put on righteousness and it clothed me.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;My judgment was as a robe an
2 CONC d a diadem.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;I was eyes to the blind, and feet was I to the lame.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;
2 CONC I was a father to the poor, and a cause which I knew not, I searched out."&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;And whose memories are to us today, as fragran
2 CONC t and as precious as Mary's box of Ointment was to the Saviour, and as beautiful as "apples of gold in pictures of silver."&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;To their Sacred Memory this book is dedicat
2 CONC ed by the Authors.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;THE PHELP
2 CONC S FAMILY IN EUROPE.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;"Our Ancestors a Gallant Christian Race Patrons of every Virtue, every Grace."&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;<
2 CONC /line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;BESIDE the old Tewkesbury Abbey Church, Gloucestershire, England, founded by the Mercian Princes, Dukes Odo, and Dodo, two noble Saxo
2 CONC n brothers, who flourished at at the commencement of the Eighth Century: In the burying ground beneath its sacred shadow, lie interred some of our Ancestors, others sweetly sleep in the cemetery of Dursley, in Gloucestershire.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;
2 CONC </line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;Others still are interred in Porlock, Somersetshire, and in Staffordshire, while others rest from their labors in almost all the Shires of Old England.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&l
2 CONC t;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;Near Towbury Hill in the Parish of Twyning, near Tewkesbury, there is a large elevated encampment which Leland supposes was the site of the residen
2 CONC ce of one of the Mercian Princes, though there can be no doubt of its having been a "Roman Station," as Roman coins have been frequently found on the spot.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;T
2 CONC he form of this old Roman Encampment is an oblong, irregular square: the west side, which looks towards the Severn River, is nearly twice as long as the east.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&g
2 CONC t;The north and west sides are strongly defended by a precipitous declivity.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The camp comprises nearly twenty acres of fi
2 CONC ne fertile pasture-land, and forms part of the estate of the late William Law Phelps, Esq., who has a mansion contiguous called "PUCKRUP HOUSE."&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;
2 CONC </line><line>&lt;div&gt;(He is a descendant of the Tewkesbury Phelps family.)&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;In St. Martin's Church, Vevey, Switzerland
2 CONC , may be seen a monument of black marble, erected in 1882, through the liberality of the late Hon. William Walter Phelps, of New Jersey, and the Hon. Charles A. Phelps, M. D., of Massachusetts, which tells its own story, and of which the followin
2 CONC g is a photograph.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;We are told that this old St. Martin's Church building d
2 CONC ates back to the time of the introduction of Christianity in Switzerland.;.and was dedicated to St. Martin of Tours, of 397, A. D.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&
2 CONC lt;div&gt;The present building dates from 1492.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;This old church was despoiled in 1536 of every ornament which related t
2 CONC o the Roman Catholic worship, and became the property of the town.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The most remarkable monuments are one erected in mempr
2 CONC y of Martin Cowarin, one of the benefactors of the town, by "the gratitude of his fellow townsmen, and the tomb of the traveller Mate, who, after having visited most parts of Asia, Africa and Australia, retired to end his days in Vevey, and those o
2 CONC f four or five English regicides who died in exile.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;Edward Ludlow, who did good military work in Ireland, and was one of the judges who condemned Charles I
2 CONC , died at Vevey in 1693; by his side are buried Andrew Broughton, and John Phelps, clerks of the Court, the latter having read the sentence of the unfortunate King.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;High up in the steeple of St. Paul's Church, London, England, to-day hangs a big bell (like its brother "Big Ben," England's Giant in Victoria's Tower) whose sweet sounds call daily, sincere and devout wo
2 CONC rshipers of our father's God together for worship: bearing this inscription "Richard Phelps, Whitechapel, London, A. D. 1710."&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;These items, as well as many others we m
2 CONC ight record, relating to the family in England, enable us to understand the respect and esteem in which their descendants are still held there.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;Our effort to discove
2 CONC r the direct line of descent of William and George Phelps, who, we believe, were born in or near Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire, England, and who emigrated to New England, the first of the name to settle in America, and were the Ancestors of a large p
2 CONC roportion of the Phelps families in America, though other Phelps emigrations have been found at a later day, has led to an extended correspondence with many Phelps families of England, as well as others, and running through many years.&lt;/div&gt;<
2 CONC /line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;From this correspondence we make the following extracts and quotations, which will not only enable many of the American families to trace back their origin in England, but also th
2 CONC eir position and standing at the time of emigration, and also at the present time.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt
2 CONC ;div&gt;The following letters and extracts from letters of the late Rev. William Phelps Prior, a descendant of James Phelps, of Tewkesbury, England, give valuable information:&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&l
