2 CONC d his father Robert&amp;rsquo;s estate, others mentioned in the bond in 1837 were George Heves, husbandman, and James Wane, yeoman. According to the Tithe Award of 1841 Ralph Moon lived at Nursery house (No. 243 on the Tithe map) and held 30.3.24 a
2 CONC cres of freehold in his own occupation as follows:&amp;mdash;&amp;mdash;No.228, Higher Church field, meadow 5.2.13; 229, Lower Church field, meadowA4.0.7. These fields were left by Henry Moon&amp;rsquo;s will 11 November, 1769 to his second son Ral
2 CONC ph, who by will 31 December, 1791bequeathed them to his second son Robert, together with Hoscar&amp;rsquo;s tenement (except the Sidbrooke heys and Fielding&amp;rsquo;s cottage with its orchard and croft). On R0bert&amp;rsquo;s death,&amp;nbsp;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;1837, all passed to the elder son Ralph. 230, Pingle, pasture, 3.0.16; 233, Marl Pit field, pasture, 2.2.25; 236,&amp;raquo; Nook Meadow, meadow, 3.1.10; 237, Outlet, pasture, 2.3.23; 240, Daines Cro
2 CONC ft, orchard, 0.3.32; 241, road; 242, orchard, 0.1.14; 243, houses, 0.0.4; 244, buildings and orchard, 1.1.31; all north of Parr lane, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;part of the old Woodcock hall estate extending to the churchy
2 CONC ard. South of Parr lane bordering Lawrence&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;lane now covered by houses was 626, part of New&amp;shy; field (enclosure of the common), a nurserv garden,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span styl
2 CONC e="font-family: times;"&gt;2.0.10; 629, New field, 2.0.17; total acreage 30.3.24.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
