2 CONC p;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; A HISTORY OF ELIZA CATHERINE THAYNE JONES&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; (Written by herself)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My first three year
2 CONC s were spent in Iowa. I was born July 28, 1858 in Lynn County, Iowa. Though I can&amp;rsquo;t recall my early years, my Mother said that in Iowa we were prosperous and lived comfortably. When they heard of the gospel they readily accepted it. The
2 CONC y sold out and came to Utah, giving up all for the gospel. We crossed the plains to Utah in the year 1861.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The trek to Utah was a peaceful one, with no trouble from the Indians. My oldest brother, Nephi, drove one team of oxen acr
2 CONC oss the plains and my Father, John Johnson Thayn drove the other. My Mother&amp;rsquo;s name was Sidney Bower Thayne. There were seven children who came across the plains. My brothers and sisters names appear in the order of the oldest to the young
2 CONC est: Mary Ann, Nephi, Johnny, Ellen Jane, Fannie, myself, and Emmy. All of these have since died. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My Mother, while living in Iowa, devoted much of her time for the caring of the sick. She acted as midwife and her presence in a si
2 CONC ck room was very welcome. Her knowledge of medicine and caring for the sick was indeed a help while crossing the plains. She acted in her capacity as midwife and helped those with illness while on the trip.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Because of her understa
2 CONC nding of the children&amp;rsquo;s need for nourishing food she walked most of the way and drove a small herd of cows. She strained the milk in the churn in the morning, and hung the churn on the side of the wagon. By nightfall we would have butte
2 CONC r to eat so we were provided for better than many.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We settled in Salt Lake City. My Father started in the saw mill business in Mill Creek Canyon. There is a canyon there now named Thayn&amp;rsquo;s Canyon after my Father. Everyon
2 CONC e was quite poor. We could not all afford to have shoes so the smaller children went barefoot in the summer and in the winter months, Mother made shoes by sewing overall cloth on our stockings. When we went outside we put on heavy old shoes over th
2 CONC em. She spun yarn and knitted good warm stockings for us to wear.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We couldn&amp;rsquo;t all afford to go to school either, because of the tuition fee or lack of clothes. The fee for tuition was three dollars a quarter. Though it s
2 CONC eems a small sum now, at that time money was scarce.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the evenings my Mother would sew for other people after the children were put to bed. When we were older we went to Sunday School and Meeting. Later, concerts were held onc
2 CONC e each week on Thursday that we were privileged to attend. At the big Theatre we attended a matinee of Cinderella and the Glass Slipper.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My Father hauled granite used in the building of the Temple. Conference days were days we loo
2 CONC ked forward to Our folks from Farmington came for conference. I often went with my folks to the tithing yard. In the fall of the year they loaded vegetables or lumber or eggs to pay their tithing. My Father helped guard President Brigham Young whe
2 CONC n some of the people would try to make trouble.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My sister Fannie, one year and three months older than I, and myself did the house work and all the washing for the large family from the time we were big enough o work while Mothe
2 CONC r worked in the garden and continued her work as a midwife. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A thrilling experience in my life occurred when I was twelve. My Mother sent me to the stream for a pail of water. While dipping the water I heard a shrill whistle. It w
2 CONC as the arrival of the first train to Salt Lake City.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I was very proud of my calico dress. I wore it only to Sunday School and Meeting. As soon as I came home I had to take it off and keep it just for best.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I live
2 CONC d in Salt Lake with my parents until I was seventeen. Then I met a young fellow from Maine, who was employed by my Father. His name was Elisha Allen Jones. My parents had discouraged our friendship because Elisha was not a member of the church. A
2 CONC s a man he was very well thought of. At last we had decided to take the step. Elisha sent me a letter in which he urged my to run away with him. It had always been difficult keeping secrets from my sister Fannie, who was so mischievous. So I tucke
2 CONC d it in my petticoats and thought it safe. That evening when I retired I found the letter was missing. I was afraid to think what would happen if my sister Fannie should find it. I retraced my step during the day but to no avail. That evening wa
2 CONC s a wedding party for my brother Johnny. Elisha was invited. Unknown to me Father had found the letter and given Elisha his consent to our marriage. That was one of my happiest evenings.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We were married on June 9, 1875. We moved u
2 CONC p in Mill Creek Canyon where my Father owned and operated a saw mill and I cooked all that summer for Father&amp;rsquo;s hired help. Shortly after we were married Elisha was converted and baptized into the Church. Since then he took active part i
2 CONC n the music departments in the organizations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We moved to Castle Valley in 1885 on the old Bill Powell Ranch or Wilson Ranch, southeast of Castle Dale. Later we were one of the first families to settle in Castle Dale. Here Elish
2 CONC a owned and operated a brick yard. Some of the bricks used in the old meeting house, that has since fallen down, were made by Elisha.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I have been an active member in Relief Society until my health failed. I have had nine children
2 CONC : Allen Elisha, Jr., John Edward, Lindley Elmer, Edith Adella, Murvin Leroy, Ida Mae, Wallace Raymond, Rolland Orlando, Herbert Arthur and have 51 grandchildren and 43 great grandchildren.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;(Eliza Catherine Thayne Jones died at th
2 CONC e age of 83 on December 27, 1941. She had been a widow since March 17, 1931. Two weeks preceding her death she fell and received a fractured hip. Her death was caused from age and complications.)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; Compiled by Ida Wall and Velda Jensen &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nb
2 CONC sp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Obituary of Eliza Catherine Thayn Jones&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;GRIM REAPER OF EMERY COUNTY CALLS PIONEER&
2 CONC lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Castle Dale - Mrs. Eliza Catherine Thayn Jones, 83, Castle Dale Pioneer, died Friday at her home in this city. Mrs. Jones suffered a fall on December 113, which owing to her advanced age, indirectly caused her death. She was bor
2 CONC n July 28, 1858 at Lynn County, Iowa a daughter of John Johnson and Sidney Bower Thayn. She was married to Elisha Allen Jones, June 9, 1875. They resided at Woodland for nine years then moved to Castle Valley. Surviving are a daughter and six sons
2 CONC ; Mrs.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ida Wall, Elisha Allen, John Edward, Lindley Elmer, Marvin Leroy, Wallace Raymond and Arthur Herbert Jones all of Castle Dale. Three brothers, Alvin and Edwin Thayn, Wellington, and Edgar Thayn of Salt Lake City. 45 grandch
2 CONC ildren and 41 great grandchildren. Funeral services were held Monday conducted by EA. Nelson. The opening song was offered by the ward quartet and the opening prayer by Hans Wickman. A vocal duet by Mrs. Merle Johansen and Mrs. Erma Lowry. First&lt
2 CONC ;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;speaker was Seth Allen, vocal solo by Nola Jones Stilson, second speaker Louis P. Oveson, quartet number, closing prayer by Leo Petersen. The grave was dedicated by Carl Bott. Burial was directed by Wallace Mortuary.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&
2 CONC gt;Another History of Eliza Catherine Thayn Jones&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As told to Velda Jones on Sept. 27th 1939&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I was born in Lynn Co. Iowa in 1858 and emigrated across the plains to Utah in 1861 when I was three years old. I rememb
2 CONC er that my mother said that we had it better than others because mother walked most of the way and drove the cows. She strained the milk in the churn and at night and at night we would have butter to eat. My oldest brother, Nephi, drove one team ac
2 CONC ross and my father John Johnson Thayne drove the other. There was seven from the oldest to the youngest: Mary Ann, Nephi, Johnny, Ellen Jane, Fanny and myself All of these have since died. I have heard my mother say that before we moved here we ha
2 CONC d plenty and lived comfortably, but when they heard the gospel they sold out and came to Utah, giving up all for the gospel.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The trek to Utah was a peaceful one without any trouble from the Indians. We settled in Salt Lake. Everyb
2 CONC ody was quite poor in those &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;days. We couldn&amp;rsquo;t all afford to have shoes so the smaller children went bare foot in the summer time and in the winter, mother made shoes for us by sewing overall cloth on stockings. When w
2 CONC e went outside we put on heavy old shoes over them. She spun yarn and knit good warm stocking for us to wear.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We couldn&amp;rsquo;t all afford to go to school in the winter because we didn&amp;rsquo;t have shoes and we had to pa
2 CONC y for our schooling. The fee was three dollars a quarter. The smaller children went barefoot and the larger children went barefoot in the winter.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the evenings my mother would sew for people after she put the children to bed. Wh
2 CONC en we were older we went to Sunday School and Meeting. After a while they held concerts once each week on Thursday. At Big Theater in Salt Lake we attended a matinee of Cinderella and the Glass Slipper.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On conference days our folk
2 CONC s from Farmington came for conference and always stayed for dinner. In the evening it was seldom we went out. I had one calico dress that I kept just for Sunday School and Meeting and as soon as I came home I had to take it off and keep it for best
2 CONC . &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I had a sister Fanny just one year and three months older than I was and from the time we were big enough to work my sister and I did all the washing on the board for the large family while my mothe
2 CONC r worked in the garden. She was a hard working woman. In the fall of the year she would load some of her vegetables in a wagon and send them to the tithing office to pay tithing. I remember my mother saying that father helped guard President Brigha
2 CONC m Young when some of the people would try to make trouble. I went with a girl who worked for President Young and she showed me about in his home, the Lion House,. I can remember a few times when mother let me mix bread. I was so small I had to stan
2 CONC d on a box to reach the table.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I lived in Salt Lake with my parents until I was seventeen. Then I met a young fellow from Maine, who was employed by me father. His name was Elisha Allen Jones. My parents had discouraged our friend
2 CONC ship because Elisha was not a member of the Church. As a man he was very well thought of. At last we had decided to take the step. Elisha sent me a letter in which he urged me to runaway with him. It had always been difficult to keep secrets from m
2 CONC y sister Fanny, who was so mischievous. So I thought the best place to keep the letter would be with me. I tucked it in my petticoats and thought it safe. That evening when I retired I found the letter was missing. I retraced my steps during the da
2 CONC y, but to no avail. I was afraid she would go to father . However, the following day nothing was said. That evening was the wedding party for my brother Johnny. Elisha was invited. Unknown to me father had found the letter and given Elisha his cons
2 CONC ent to our marriage. That was one of the happiest evenings. I was married when I was seventeen to Elisha Allen Jones. I moved up in Mill Creek Canyon where my father owned a saw mill and I cooked all that summer for the men that worked in the mill.
2 CONC &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My father hauled granite used in building the Temple.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A thrilling experience in my life occurred when I was twelve. My mother sent me to the stream for a pail of water. While dipping the water I heard a shrill w
2 CONC histle. It was the arrival of the first train to Salt Lake City. We moved to Woodland on the Provo river where we made our home. The first winter we were there the snow was so deep and drifted that we built steps in the snow. In the evening we rea
2 CONC d to each other.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We moved to Castle Valley in 1885 and I have lived here since. When we first moved here there wasn&amp;rsquo;t too many houses here. Grandpa made brick and it was used in the old meeting house that has since falle
2 CONC n down. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I have had nine children. Now 51 grandchildren, 30 great-grandchildren and I was 81 years old on the 28th of July.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
