2 TITL History for Andrew Dudley Jr.
1 _OID 546ceab0-5f3a-42e1-bb69-69473a79fa71
1 PLAC Dudley Family History
1 _DSCR History for Andrew Dudley Jr.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;font size="1"&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Andrew Dudley Jr. was born August 8, 1870 in Spanish Fork, Utah, the first son of Andrew Dudley Sr. and Mary Lucy Markham. Of his early life, he writes in his journal &a
2 CONC mp;quot;we children of the neighbor would congregate at Grandmother Markham&amp;#39;s home and play many great games of childhood like still sticks, picket, wild bear, run sheep, the lions down and many others. Grandmother Markham was loved by al
2 CONC l and we were always entertained at her home, winter or summer.&amp;quot; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;My first school teacher was Mary Ann McClane, next James Higenson, then Irvin Wilson and Geroge H. Brimhall
2 CONC . I had a mile and a half to walk to school each day.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;In the fall of 1885, my father in company with Zacherian West and others came out to Ashley Valley and bargained for 160 a
2 CONC cres of land at the present site of our location at Jensen, Uintah County, Utah.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;In the spring of 1886 father started for the Ashley Valley to make preparations for moving int
2 CONC o this new country. On March 10 of that year, Wm. McKee and Emily Markham (mother&amp;#39;s sister) were married and accompanied father. Father returned to Spanish Fork, Utah, that fall and intended moving his family out to the Green River, but i
2 CONC t got to late in the season, and so he waited until the next spring, 1887. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;When he returned in the spring, George Smith and I came with him. He shipped his outfit, consisting of wagons, plows, ha
2 CONC rrows, seed grain, teams etc to Price by train, leaving Springville on the 18th of March of that year. At Price, we got our wagons together, loaded, and started for our new home, arriving here the 28th of March, 1887. Father rented the Isaac Burto
2 CONC n farm that spring, where we spent the summer. There was a ferry boat on the ranch with we operated. We ferried quit a lot of cattle, horses, wagons and horsemen across Green River that summer.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size
2 CONC ="5"&gt;&amp;quot;About the latter part of October, Father brought his family and household goods out. We also brought small bunch cattle. We were apart 10 days making the trip.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;That win
2 CONC ter was very cold and a long one. We had only one room log cabin to live in. The neighbors got together and hired Uncle Bill Ainge, a cripple to teach school. There were about 13 students. School was held in one room of George Ainge&amp;#39;s home
2 CONC . I don&amp;#39;t remember all who attended that winter but the following were among them: Luther, Mary and Gerald Stewart, Bill, Alf, Harriot, and John Ainge, Wm. E. Chatwin and my sisters Sarah, Helen and I also attended.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&l
2 CONC t;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;In July of 1889, I went to Spanish fork to take Grandmother Markham home, and to bring some of our household goods back. It was at that time, on the 24th of July that I met for the first time Sina Jensen, my fu
2 CONC ture wife.&amp;quot; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;In the fall of 1889, Albert W. Wilkins and his wife, (my half sister Mary Ellen) came out here to visit and decided to move out so father let me go back with them a
2 CONC nd assist them.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;I worked with father on the farm for several years; the irrigating was very difficult for the soil would wash so badly. It was hard to plow for the plow would n
2 CONC ot scower. We got the water from the canal in July 1887.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;The fall of 1892, I went back to Spanish Fork where I remained until February 1894. It was during this time, that I sta
2 CONC rted keeping company with Sina Jensen which resulted in our marriage January 10, 1900.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;On my return to the Uintah Basin, I continued to work with my father on the farm, and i
2 CONC n that winter I got some more schooling. The winter of 1894 and 1895, I went to school to J.P. Rudy in Glines Ward, staying with my sister Sarah and Ephraim Rasmussen. The next winter I attended the Uintah Stake Academy in Vernal under Andrew B. An
2 CONC derson. I also attended the following winter until February 1897.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;&amp;quot;In the late summer of 1897, I received a call to go on a L.D.S. Mission to the northern states and was schedul
2 CONC ed to leave September 2, 1897. I spent two years in the mission field, arriving back in Salt Lake December 20, 1899. I left immediately for Spanish Fork where I was met by Sina Jensen, John L. Larson and Ann Markham.&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;On January 10, 1900 Andrew was married to Sina Jensen in the Salt Lake Temple. He remained with his bride in Spanish Fork until about the middle of February, when he started for Jensen, Utah. His wife Sina was teachin
2 CONC g school and it was thought advinable for her to finish the year before joining her husband. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;In May, he returned to Spanish Fork for his wife, and they arrived in Jensen, the forepart of June. Th
2 CONC ey started life together very humbly, living in a two-room log house for a number of years. Later, Andrew built a five room brick house, close to the log house. The little log house was moved back to the north and was used for a shelter for the liv
2 CONC estock. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;After his eldest son DeMarr&amp;#39;s marriage, DeMarr built a two room log house across the drive west of his parent&amp;#39;s home. This two room log house gradually was added on to unt
2 CONC il it became the two-story large stucco house in which DeMarr and his wife still live.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Andrew and Sina had seven children: Andrew DeMarr born May 6, 1901 Irene born November 13, 1902 Myrtle born M
2 CONC ay 6, 1904, Parley Wendell born July 26, 1906, Howard Jensen born October 4, 1908, Lynne Hosmer born February 19, 1912, and Lucy born May 21, 1914. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Andrew was an unusually good farmer. He loved t
2 CONC he soil and the long hours he spent in irrigating, planning, harvesting etc where a pleasure to him. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;He took an active part in the affairs in the Jensen Ward, serving in the bishopric of the Jens
2 CONC en Ward as second counselor to bishop Archie S. Richardson from August 22, 1908 until May 28, 1916.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;He became interested in genealogy and became research work on the Dudley family. He was chosen t
2 CONC o work in the Uintah Stake Genealogical Board. Later he was made chairman and worked until released in June 21, 1931. In his later life, during the winter months he and his wife went to St. George and also to Salt Lake to do Genealogical and Templ
2 CONC e work. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;He took an active part in community affairs. He served as President if the Burns Bench Irrigation Canal for several years. He was in charge of the plotting of the Jensen Cemetary and he k
2 CONC ept a map of each lot taken. To get a lot for burial, one must clear it with Andrew Dudley. After Andrew&amp;#39;s death his son DeMarr carried on this work for a time. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Andrew died April 30, 194
2 CONC 4 at his home in Jensen. His wife Sina survived him until March 27, 1959. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Although Andrew and Sina had seven children, only two sons remained in Jensen to build their homes and raise their famili
2 CONC es, A. DeMarr and Lynne H.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Daughter Irene is at this writing still remembered by older residents for her years spent in Jensen teaching school. She first taught in a little school that was built a
2 CONC nd maintained in the early days on Brush Creek. Then she left Jensen, married then divorced Peter Wankier. After the death of her father, she returned to Jensen with his daughter Ann to be with her widowed mother, and she taught school again in Jen
2 CONC sen and in Vernal. She then went to Murray and continued teaching. She died January 14, 1974.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Myrtle returned to Jensen, after attending school at the Brigham Young University, and after going o
2 CONC n a mission to the North-western States. She taught school in Jensen and helped her husband Alton Peterson got his degree from the B.Y.U. Then they both went to Nevada where they both taught school until their retirement. Myrtle is still living a
2 CONC t Henderson, Nevada. Her husband Alton died March 12, 1982. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Lucy married Lee Johnson and they lived in Salt Lake, then California and finally in Portland, Oregon where she is still living. Her hu
2 CONC sband died Dec. 5, 1982. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Wendell Parley lived with his Mother most of his life. At the time of his death Aug. 16, 1955, he was married to Elma Stanely of Salt Lake City.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Lynne Hosmer Dudley built a home on land he got from his parents on the south side of Highway 40 and across the highway from the old Dudley home and his brother DeMarr&amp;#39;s home. He married Naomi Davenport and they h
2 CONC ad three children, Ryan Lynne, Frank Andrew, Marie (married Ernest Smith of Texas). Lynne&amp;#39;s wife Naomi died Dec, 20, 1967 and he married Iola Dudley, (widow of Orville Dudley). They were divorced and Sept. 28, 1972, he married Leola Frame o
2 CONC f Murray, Utah, and was living with her at the time of Lynne&amp;#39;s death April 2, 1986. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Lynne farmed the land he got from his parents and he also owned his father&amp;#39;s ranch east of Gree
2 CONC n River, north of the Escalante Ranch. He was a good farmer and enjoyed farming, as did his father before him. Besides farming, he worked for several years at the oil refining plant in Jensen, and later when it was moved to Rangely, he went there t
2 CONC o work. When he quit, he got work with the R.E.A. building additional lines thru-out the counties of Uintah and Duchesne. After retirement, he worked on his farms and his garden. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;His son Ryan lef
2 CONC t Jensen when he went away to school. He spent several years in the air force of the U.S. and at this writing is living in Washington D.C. and employed with the Bureau of Land Management. He married Elizabeth Lee Jackowski January 17, 1987.&lt;/fon
2 CONC t&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Son Frank served in the Army of the U.S. and did overseas duty in Germany for several years. While stationed in Germany he married Gisela Inge Lehmann and had two sons; frank Andrew Jr. and Michael John
2 CONC . He was later divorced and married Carol Sue Fullen. Carol all ready had two children Tracy and Mark. Frank and Carol have one child Marlena Kay. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;When Frank returned from Germany he completed hi
2 CONC s studies at East Texas State University, and the Utah State. He returned to Vernal to live and got employment with the Bureau of Land Management. At this writing he is also administrating his fathers estate and farm in Jensen.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&g
2 CONC t;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Daughter Marie married Ernest Smith and since her marriage has lived in Fate, Texas. They have four daughters, Gina Marie, Melissa, Susanne, and Emily. Marie taught school, and is continuing her education in Texas.&l
2 CONC t;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Dudley Family History&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
