2 TITL Jacob Moroni Secrist obituary
1 _OID 57b6c39c-76a8-47d4-9c71-5c4e9f10f976
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>      &lt;!-- SN:TREESUI10 --&gt;  &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr id="sRes-0" class="tblrow record keySelect" valign="top" style="border-bottom-color: #cdc7be; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-style: solid"&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;
2 CONC table border="0" width="100%" style="border-bottom-color: currentColor; border-bottom-width: medium; border-bottom-style: none"&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr valign="top"&gt;&lt;td class="body" align="left" style="border: currentColor"&gt;Jacob Moroni Sec
2 CONC rist, who has departed this life, was born in Salt Lake City, August 15, 1850, a son of Jacob F. and Ann Eliza (Logan) Secrist, who were natives of Pennsylvania and came to Utah in the second company in 1847. They settled first in Salt Lake City an
2 CONC d a few years later on a farm at Farmington, now known as the Secrist homestead, which is one of the old landmarks of the district. The father went on a mission to Great Britain, where he labored for three years, and died while crossing the plain
2 CONC s on the way home. The mother resided on the farm in Davis county until her death. They had a family of five children, of whom but one is now living. &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr id="sRes-1" class="
2 CONC tblrowalt record" valign="top" style="border-bottom-color: #cdc7be; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-style: solid"&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;table border="0" width="100%" style="border-bottom-color: currentColor; border-bottom-width: medium; border-b
2 CONC ottom-style: none"&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr valign="top"&gt;&lt;td class="body" align="left" style="border: currentColor"&gt;Jacob M. Secrist was reared in Davis county and pursued his education in the public schools. He purchased the home farm, whic
2 CONC h he conducted very sucessfully. He became a member of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and was very active in the work of the church, serving as bishop of the Farmington ward for twenty-four years, or from 1882 until his death. He p
2 CONC articipated in the Black Hawk war and in nearly all of the Indian troubles of the early days. In commercial and financial circles he became well known as president of the Farmington Commercial &amp;amp; Manufacturing Company and as a director of th
2 CONC e Davis County Bank at Farmington. &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr id="sRes-2" class="tblrow record" valign="top" style="border-bottom-color: #cdc7be; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-style: sol
2 CONC id"&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;table border="0" width="100%" style="border-bottom-color: currentColor; border-bottom-width: medium; border-bottom-style: none"&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr valign="top"&gt;&lt;td class="body" align="left" style="border: currentColor
2 CONC "&gt;It was on the 29th of April, 1903, that Mr. Secrist was married to Mrs. H. Priscilla (Bourne) Potter, whose first husband was Ernest H. Potter, to whom she was married October 11, 1875. By her first marriage she had seven children: William E.
2 CONC , who married Olive L. Moon and now owns the old Secrist homestead; Horace C., who is living with his mother; Alice M., at home; Wallace G., who married Clara Robinson and resided in Farmington, where he died at the age of thirty-five years; Estell
2 CONC a, the wife of Albert Moon, of Davis county; Vernon L., who died in infancy; and Inez A., the wife of Edward F. Clark, of Farmington. Mrs. Secrist was a daughter of Charles and Jane (Alder) Bourne, both natives of England, whence they came to Ameri
2 CONC ca in 1852. After a brief period spent in St. Louis, Missouri, they started for Salt Lake City with ox team and wagon and were about five months in making the trip across the long, hot stretches of sand and over the mountain passes to their destina
2 CONC tion. Mr. Bourne settled in Salt Lake City, where he engaged in business as a contractor and builder, following that pursuit for a number of years. Later he settled on a farm in Davis county and both he and his wife died upon that place, the forme
2 CONC r on the 12th of February, 1885, and the latter on the 8th of July, 1889. &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr id="sRes-3" class="tblrowalt record" valign="top" style="border-bottom-color: #cdc7be; border-b
2 CONC ottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-style: solid"&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;table border="0" width="100%" style="border-bottom-color: currentColor; border-bottom-width: medium; border-bottom-style: none"&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr valign="top"&gt;&lt;td class="body
2 CONC " align="left" style="border: currentColor"&gt;The death of Mr. Secrist occurred November 5, 1906, and deep regret was felt at his passing, for he was well established in public regard as a substantial and valued citizen--one whose interest in th
2 CONC e welfare and upbuilding of the community was manifest in many tangible ways. He gave his political allegiance to the republican party from the time of its organization and ably served as county commissioner. &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt; </line></content></metadataxml>
