2 CONC tiful. After the martyrdom of the Prophet, Anson was called to assist Sheriff Backenstoes in helping prevent the mob from committing outrages against the saints. He was bishop in three wards in Bountiful, Utah, from 1849-1851, 1874-77. In 1851 he w
2 CONC as called to help settle Parowan, Utah, and was Justice of Peace there. He was appointed Probate Judge of Millard County and Utah territory Legislator. Brigham Young called him to help settle Fillmore and Anson was a postmaster and Deputy Marshal
2 CONC l while there. He was then called to help settle Carson Valley, Nevada. With his sons, he took part in the Echo Canyon during the Utah War with Johnson Army. He founded Calls Fort in Box Elder County, Utah. He was a counselor to President William R
2 CONC . Smith at the organization of Davis Stake. He was an adjutant general&amp;nbsp;in the Silver Grays (Mountain Sharps) and helped in the rescue of the Martin Handcart Company. He was a successful merchant and farmer and helped with the constructio
2 CONC n of the railroad. He was a loyal member of the church. Children of 1st wife: Anson Vasco, b. 9 Jul 1834. Md. 1st, 28 Jan 1853, Charlotte Holbrook. Md. 2nd 10 Nov 1856, Eliza Catherine Kent. d. 4 Aug 1867. Mary Vashta, b. 27 Mar 1836. Md. 10 Apr 18
