2 TITL Paintings of the newly built Custom House of 1722/3
1 _OID 5f590974-1507-40ce-9082-4c0962fb0320
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;The former Custom House had been located at the bottom of Water Street near the centre of the town and its relatively long distance from the new dock had led to some operational problems. A new Custom House adjo
2 CONC ining the dock was therefore proposed and in 1719 plans were laid before the Commissioneers. Building work began in 1721 and was completed the following year. It was a red brick building faced with stone and an arched entrance arcade in the centr
2 CONC e with the Royal arms carved in stone above. There was a flight of steps at the entrance which led to an open lobby or piazza, above which was the "long room" for the transaction of Custom business; behind the building was a spacious yard with suit
2 CONC able warehouses. It was very much part of the eighteenth century scene.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The artist WG Herdman was born in 1805 and would have known the Customs House in his childhood but his painting is a reconstruction from earlie
2 CONC r pictures.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;A previous unrecorded&amp;nbsp;view of Liverpool " A Prospect of Liverpool about 1725" was acquired in December 2000 by National Museums Liverpool for the collections of the Merseyside Maritime Museum. Alt
2 CONC hough almost contemporaneous with the familiar engraving, "The South West Prospect of Liverpoole",&amp;nbsp;by Samuel and Nathaniel Buck (published in 1728), this view is of particular interest for highlighting, and probably being the earliest repr
2 CONC esentation of, the first dock and the newly-built Custom House.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The painting, probably in oil, is 26.2in by 58.2in wide. The artist has adopted an elevated view from the Mersey at the southern end of the town lookin
2 CONC g north towards the mouth of the river. This places the area around the dock to the right foreground and the town is then depicted across the centre of the canvas receding into the distance. Various vessels are shown on the river, including a grou
2 CONC p of three in the centre foreground firing a salute. The painting is not signed or marked and identification of the artist has not been established. The composition is relatively sophisticated and suggests that it was executed by someone who was fa
2 CONC miliar with this type of topographical work.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;It has not been possible to trace the history of the painting before it entered the London art market in the late 1970s when it was no longer identified or recognised a
2 CONC s a view of Liverpool. The re-identification of the painting was made by Paul Breen of 'The Boydell Galleries'. It may possibly have belonged to Liverpool antiquarian and historian Robert Gladstone as there is a tantalising entry in the catalogue o
2 CONC f the 'Liverpool Shipping Week' in 1931 which reads 'No 436 Oil Painting of Liverpool c1720, with ships in foreground. Robert Gladstone Esq'&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;It therefore remains a matter of speculation as to who commissioned it and f
2 CONC or what purpose. The rough and unfinished nature of the reverse of the panel has led to the suggestion that this painting was originally intended as a decorative feature, perhaps set in panelling or over a fireplace. One might imagine that it was p
2 CONC ainted for somone associated with the town, perhaps someone who was no longer actually living there. One possible candidate is Sylvester Moorcroft, a former bailiff, mayor and alderman, who was responsible for building the Custom House.&lt;/p&gt;</
2 CONC line><line>&lt;p&gt;There seems no reason to doubt that "A Prospect of Liverpool" is a significant discovery and an important addition to the small group of pre-nineteenth century views of the port and town.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Sources
2 CONC : "Liverpool: A People's History" by Peter Aughton,&amp;nbsp; 'A new painting of Liverpool: " A Prospect of Liverpool about 1725" ' by Anthony Tibbles HSLC Vol 152 2003 &amp;amp; "Old Liverpool" by Rev Richard Postance MA 1889&lt;/p&gt;</line><line
2 CONC >&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
