1 _OID 60b8b1c3-3a8e-443e-90e0-25f669a7cb4f
1 DATE 1868
1 PLAC Benton Wyoming to Salt Lake City Utah
1 _DSCR Daniel D. McArthur Company (1868)
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel, 1847&amp;ndash;1868 [database]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Daniel D. McArthur Company (1868)&lt;br&gt;Departure: 14 August 1868&lt;br&gt;
2 CONC Arrival: 2 September 1868&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;411 individuals and 51 wagons were in the company when it began its journey from the outfitting post at Benton, Wyoming.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Daniel D. McArthur&lt;/stro
2 CONC ng&gt;, from St. George, led a group of out-and-back teamsters to Benton, Wyoming. They left Salt Lake in mid-June, traveling east by way of Parley's Canyon, where they encountered a blinding spring blizzard. It also took them a full day to swim th
2 CONC eir herd of oxen across the Green River, which was swollen from the spring runoff. They reached the end-of-track terminus of Benton in July and waited about six weeks for the emigrants, most of whom had crossed the Atlantic on the steamship Colorad
2 CONC o. It took the emigrants about a week to travel from New York to the outfitting place on the train. The emigrants arrived in Benton, where they met their teamsters on August 7 and left a week later. The company included 411 passengers and 61 wagons
2 CONC . Twenty-five of the wagons were loaded with goods destined for stores in Salt Lake, including the new Zion's Cooperative Mercantile Institution. They traveled in a northwesterly direction from Benton through Whiskey Gap and northward from there un
2 CONC til they reached the Sweetwater River and joined the old emigrant road. They arrived in Salt Lake on September 2. Seven people, mostly young children, died en route.&lt;br&gt;_________________&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Source of Trail Excerpt: Garner, Sar
2 CONC ah Rebecca Davis, Autobiographical Sketch, in &lt;em&gt;Genealogical Charts and Biographical Sketches of Members of the L.D.S. Church, Ogden Stake&lt;/em&gt;, 26 vols., 25:15.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Read Trail Excerpt: On July, Tuesday 14th, 1868 we le
2 CONC ft on the steamship Colorado which sailed from Liverpool England with six hundred Saints, unde[r] direction of &lt;strong&gt;William B. Preston&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp; We arrived at New York July 28th and the company reached Benton Aug 7th, 1868.
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; Aug 14th, we left Benton for Utah, with an ox team of sixty one wagon&amp;rsquo;s &amp;amp; Four-hundred &amp;amp; eleven passenger&amp;rsquo;s under direction of &lt;strong&gt;Captain Daniel D. Mc Arthur&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp; Arrive
2 CONC d in Salt Lake Valley in Sept of the same year[.] There were five children and one Sister died on journey. It was a long journey but can say a pleasant one compared with some of the other companies who came before.&amp;nbsp; We enjoyed the pleasan
2 CONC t times in the evening when gathered around the big camp fire, singing songs also listening to the talk&amp;rsquo;s given by some of the Brethern. Another time dancing on the ground by the tune of a violin. We had not the hardships to pass throug
2 CONC h as did some of the Saints when crossing the plains. There were pretty good roads and we walked lots of the way.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;br&gt;-----&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;James Robb&lt;/strong&gt;, age 41, born 7 Feb 1827, died 22 July 1875, 7 i
2 CONC n family, origin Scotland&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ann Turner Robb&lt;/strong&gt;, age 41, born 20 Jan 1827, died 6 Feb 1901&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Jennie Robb&lt;/strong&gt;, age 20, born 11 July 1848, died 29 Oct 1903&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Isabell
2 CONC e Robb&lt;/strong&gt;. age 19, born 8 May 1849, died 14 Mar 1923&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Elizabeth Robb&lt;/strong&gt;, age 13, born 17 Nov 1855, died 4 Jan 1923&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;John Robb&lt;/strong&gt;, age 5, born 21 June 1863, died 22 Jul
2 CONC y 1875&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Jessie Robb&lt;/strong&gt; (female), age 2, born 21 Dec 1865, died 30 Mar 1946&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;http://www.lds.org/churchhistory/library/pioneercompany/1,15797,4017-1-197,00.html&lt;/p
2 CONC &gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
