2 CONC s council I went out to our camp&amp;mdash;Oliver's sickness requiring mypresence&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sunday 14th. By external applications Sweating &amp;amp; administering in the name of the Lor
2 CONC d we obtained some power over the disease. Received accessions to our camp so that it now numbered 50 waggons.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Friday [May] 19 Having continued to work until Wednesday Br. Heber requeste
2 CONC d me to go &amp;amp; examine a rout[e] for a new Bridge on the Papaean[Papillion] creek, went in company with Joel Ricks, Daniel davis &amp;amp; Bro Foohe [Foot], recon[n]oitored the route &amp;amp; returned to camp in the evening &amp;amp; reporte
2 CONC d[.] after deliberdtion it was decided to build the Bridge in a fine grove a mile above the old one by which a good camping ground will be obtained a mile or two saved in going to the Elk Horn&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;stron
2 CONC g&gt;Satturday 20th Travelled over to the Papaean with our company top 16 wagons, Br Lorenzo Snow's Pisgah com[.] &amp;amp;, Some others making in all 61 Waggons&amp;mdash;12 miles.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sunda
2 CONC y May 21st Went to work and built our Bridge having it completed by two oclock after which the camp came together at the sound of the horn &amp;amp; held publik meeting. Br. Snow preached to all concerning the advantage of trials to the Saints &amp
2 CONC ;amp; the necessity of their exercising themselves in Patience &amp;amp; forbearance loving one another, A heavy shower of rain fell in the evening&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Monday [May] 22 On account of the groun
2 CONC d being wet we remained incamped, &amp;amp; in the afternoon had heavy showers again[.] Had large numbers of waggons arrived &amp;amp; some went over on our new Bridge, the mud being very bad&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;strong&gt;Tuesday 25 Rained hard through the night &amp;amp; continued until 12 oclock when it cleared off with N. [&lt;em&gt;illegible&lt;/em&gt;] a hundred waggons arrived here today wallowing through the mud&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><
2 CONC line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Wednesday [May] 24th Washed our sheets &amp;amp; I went back to town&amp;mdash;About a hundred Waggons stated for the Horn&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thursday [May] 25 This mor
2 CONC ning I had the good fortune to trade the demand I held against Capt Brown for repairing Waggons, to Cyrenus Taylor for which I received $7.25 in cash which enabled me to obtain some necessary articles of clothing &amp;amp;c.&amp;mdash;when I return
2 CONC ed to our camp&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Friday 26th Our camp journeyed over to the Horn 8 miles arrived about noon &amp;amp; found our Brothers had constructed a raft &amp;amp; comenced crossing yesterd
2 CONC ay having over a hundred waggons across&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Saturday 27th Continued ferrying over Waggons[.] Br. Gregory's waggon accidently ran off the raft into the river which damaged very muc
2 CONC h his provisions[,] clothing , &amp;amp;c.&amp;mdash;Br Brigham arrived in the evening, with his company&amp;mdash;Sister Neff's son was drowned&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sunday [May] 28th A tremendous heavy rain t
2 CONC his morning[.] we remained encamped no crossing to day&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Monday 29th The President's company &amp;amp; family crossed over to day&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&
2 CONC lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tuesday May 30th I repaired Doctor [Samuel Lindsey] Sprague's waggon &amp;amp; received in pay some medicine for Oliver [Jacob], he is rendered helpless by rheumatic pains.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;str
2 CONC ong&gt;Wednesday 31st We made an early move &amp;amp; crossed only ten over encamped &amp;amp; made report of [--] to Br [Thomas] Bullock the Recorder&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thursday first day of June
2 CONC &amp;mdash;The camp of Israel began its march to the mountains by sending forward Capt Lorenzo Snow's company of waggons[.] H.C. Kimbal arrived with about fifty&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Friday [June] 2nd Another c
2 CONC ompany under Capt Pulsipher&amp;mdash;a great deal of electioneering by some persons to those who formerly belonged to Elder Kimbal's company to join others &amp;amp; in many cases they succeeded to draw them off&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
2 CONC </line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Satterday [June] 3rd Wm Perkin's hundred started &amp;amp; moved on in the rain. Our Ten was now increased by the arrival of Jerry Root &amp;amp; Alfred Randal [Randall] to 18 Waggons &amp;amp; Joel Ricks appoint
2 CONC ed captain&amp;mdash;twoo more children &amp;amp; a woman Burried here&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sunday [June] 4th A small &amp;lt;number&amp;gt; gathered &amp;amp; held meeting at Br Kimballs camp where we receive
2 CONC d some excelent instruction from Prest. Morley, Bishop [Newel Kimball] Whitney, &amp;amp; H.C. Kimball&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Monday 5th President Young moved out with his company &amp;amp; some mor
2 CONC e arrived from Winter Quarters&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tuesday 6th About 10 oclock A M an alarm was raised that the Indians were driving off our cattle, in a few minutes several men were in pursuit, fo
2 CONC llowing them to the river they took our ox from them, but they had killed one. Br [George] Clawson got sight of one in the act of tying up his meat &amp;amp; fired upon him[.] the Indians fled across the River &amp;amp; Wm. Kimbal [Kimball], Howar
2 CONC d Eagan, Thomas Ricks &amp;amp; [Noah] Willis Bartholomew followed the Indians across the river &amp;amp; coming up with them, they presented their guns[.] when our boys commenced firing with their revolvers the Indians returned the fire &amp;amp
2 CONC ; shot Ricks in the back with three Buck Shot &amp;amp; Egan through the wrist with a ball Just as he was firing his revolver which threw the pistol from his hand &amp;amp; he lost it[.] his Horse was also shot in the top of the neck, &amp;amp; Kim
2 CONC bals in the top of the hip. the Indians altho the most numerous finaly fled &amp;amp; our Boys brot off their wounded man&amp;mdash;the news coming to Br [Joel] Ricks, he &amp;amp; Tho[ma]s [Levi] Whittle &amp;amp; a Boy started with a Buggy to mee
2 CONC t them, but they missed the company&amp;amp; fell in with the Indians who threatened their lives &amp;amp; robbed them of a yearling colt[,] about thirty dollars worth of clothing &amp;amp; some other articles when they told them to go home sayin
2 CONC g that &amp;lt;they&amp;gt; had four men killed &amp;amp; some wounded&amp;mdash;This was a foolish affair on the part of our men. Elders of Israel are not called to make war upon the Lamanites&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Som
2 CONC e forty waggons crossed over to day &amp;amp; President Kimbal[l] broke up his camp here &amp;amp; moved out two miles &amp;amp; formed a carel [corral] with some hundred &amp;amp; fifty Waggons &amp;amp; set a strong guard&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Wednesday 7th Moved over to the Platte[.] made 15 miles &amp;amp; camped&amp;mdash;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thursday[June] 8th This morning my wife [Emily Heaton Jac
2 CONC ob] sprained &amp;amp; bruised her ancle severely&amp;mdash;Our wounded man is doing well, &amp;amp; Oliver is much better, but we have our share of invalids&amp;mdash;moved on eight miles, &amp;amp; camped on the bank of the river in three company
2 CONC s &amp;lt;225 waggons&amp;gt; Br Kimbal[l]s company contained 104 Waggons, he called us together and organized his companys with Henry Heriman [Harriman] as Capt of hundred, Titus Billings, &amp;amp; John Pack Capts of fifty with six capts of Ten i
