2 TITL Pearletta Jane Mecham Taylor
1 _OID 69c0a8a9-4b44-4b5f-acbf-3f394d040424
1 _DSCR Book: Mecham Family History, 1985; pages 43-44. It is out of print but I have a copy of the book.  Robert Bernard Mecham Jr was the editor and compiler of the book.  
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;Pearletta Jane Mecham was born October 6, 1875, at Wallsburg, Wasatch County, Utah.&amp;nbsp; She was the oldest child of Moses Moroni Mecham and Almira Jane Duke.&amp;nbsp; The family lived in Wallsburg for abo
2 CONC ut nine years from the time her mother and father were married on Dec 26, 1875.&amp;nbsp; Her mother, Almira Jane Duke, had lived there prior to this time but was born in Parowan, Iron County, Utah.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Pioneer life was never easy. It was almost a luxury to have more than a one room log house.&amp;nbsp; Life in Wallsburg was like that in most "Mormon" communities.&amp;nbsp; They had a general store which handled eve
2 CONC rything from clothing to farm tools and also served as a post office.&amp;nbsp; Farming was the main industry as was typical of small communities at that time.&amp;nbsp; They had a grist mill where farmers could have their wheat milled; also a mola
2 CONC sses mill, and blacksmith shop.&amp;nbsp; Meeting houses where church and activities were held were a main part of the community.&amp;nbsp; Most families knew the news about each other and they also pulled together in times of need.&amp;nbsp; &lt;b
2 CONC r&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Pearletta Jane, being the oldest, had a great amount of responsibility in helping with her younger sisters and brothers.&amp;nbsp; She had a very kind, gentle nature and w
2 CONC as much like her mother.&amp;nbsp; At a young age she had a strong testimony which never wavered throughout her life. &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;A handsome man, Jabez W. Taylor, came to work i
2 CONC n the area where Pearl lived and very soon made her acquaintance.&amp;nbsp; He knew that she was the special young lady for whom he had been looking.&amp;nbsp; They were married on the 26th of April, 1893, by Bishop Frank Billings in Jensen.&amp;nb
2 CONC sp; They went to Wales, Sanpete County, Utah, to run the farm which formerly belonged to Jabe's father, William Taylor; his father had died in 1891 and left the property to Jabe.&amp;nbsp; Now they were near the Manti Temple and they went there t
2 CONC o have their marriage recorded for all eternity and to receive their special blessings of the Endowment.&amp;nbsp; Traveling to a temple before getting married was not practical as it took three days or more; camping along the way was difficult an
2 CONC d most young people went as soon as possible after their civil marriage.&amp;nbsp; They received their Temple Blessings on the 28th of September, 1893.&amp;nbsp; &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;At t
2 CONC his time Wales was known for its mining industry as well as being a farming area.&amp;nbsp; While living there their oldest child, Eliza Jane, always known from childhood as Jennie, was born on the 15th of January, 1894.&amp;nbsp; Just two and one-
2 CONC half months after little Jennie's birth, Almira Jane, Pearl's mother, gave birth to a little girl, Sarah.&amp;nbsp; She never got out of the bed after her baby was born; complications set in and this dear mother passed away May 3, 1894, leaving he
2 CONC r little baby daughter in the family's care.&amp;nbsp; Pearl's father, Moses Moroni Mecham, sent for her to come and help with the baby. Pearl and Jabe moved to Ashley Creek at this time.&amp;nbsp; They lived for a while with Pearl's father until J
2 CONC abe could build a one room log house just through the fields from him.&amp;nbsp; Pearl took the little baby and nursed her right along with her own little daughter.&amp;nbsp; Sarah was in the Taylor home most of the time until they moved to Hayden
2 CONC , Duchesne County, Utah, where they went to homestead 80 acres of land.&amp;nbsp; &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Memories of Pearl's sister-in-law, Zina D. Mecham, are very helpful in knowing o
2 CONC f a few of the incidents in Peal's life at this time, because the Duncans were also homesteading in Hayden, and their familes were very close.&amp;nbsp; Pearl's brother, James D. Mecham, came and stayed with her quite often; in fact, this is wher
2 CONC e Zina Duncan met him and later on married him.&amp;nbsp; James, known to everyone as Jim, bought his sister a sewing maching at this time and she sewed all the family's clothing and made quilts on the machine for her brother. She was an excellen
2 CONC t seamstress.&amp;nbsp; Zina recalls "Aunt Pearl" making her and Jennie beautiful blue dresses just alike which they would save to wear to the most special dances and on other important occasions. &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Jabe and Pearl were most certainly not enjoying prosperity.&amp;nbsp; They grew many kinds of vegetables, but their main crops were hay and grain.&amp;nbsp; They would take their wheat to Roosevelt, about sixteen mile
2 CONC s south of Hayden, to the flour mill and have it milled for their winter's supply of flour.&amp;nbsp; Most prosperous farmers sold enough to buy many supplies for their needs as well as clothing for their families.&amp;nbsp; One fall Zina Duncan'
2 CONC s father bought shoes for the Taylor family for which they were in great need at that time.&amp;nbsp; Jabe was probably a much better miner or freighter than farmer because his acreage did not prosper as well as those who put more time into their f
2 CONC arms.&amp;nbsp; &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Pearl's house had home-woven rugs on the floors, nice clean curtains at the windows, and good meals on the table which generally consisted of hot bisc
2 CONC uits along with the other food.&amp;nbsp; Jabe dearly loved his wife but homesteading was difficult for her and all the family.&amp;nbsp; At times when he was depressed or out of sorts he would walk through the fields and his dear companion would g
2 CONC o after him, and in her tender, loving way would talk to him, console him in his troubles and they would come back to the house with Jabe in good spirits again.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The gr
2 CONC ound in Hayden was rich and productive and the water was good.&amp;nbsp; The family lived close to the church and Pearl was able to attend with no difficulty.&amp;nbsp; While there she held the position of secretary in the Primary as well as bein
2 CONC g a Relief Society visiting teacher; these positions were all that could be found on the records at that time.&amp;nbsp; In those days, the sisters rode horseback or went with a team and wagon to visit their assigned families.&amp;nbsp; She was a t
2 CONC eaching companion with Zina D. Mecham's mother while living in this area.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Before 1915, Jabe took a homestead at Crescent which was located about three miles southeas
2 CONC t of Cedarview.&amp;nbsp; This move brought many hardships to the family.&amp;nbsp; The ground was a more clay type with rocks, shallow top soil and acres of sagebrush which had to be cleared.&amp;nbsp; They did move into a better house which Pear
2 CONC l and the four older girls appreciated.&amp;nbsp; At this time they had seven children:&amp;nbsp; one boy, Jabez Elmer, had died while the family lived in Jensen.&amp;nbsp; The family consisted of:&amp;nbsp; Jennie, Pearletta, Almira, Laura, Earl
2 CONC , and Maurel; all these were born in Jensen with the exception of Maurel, who was born while they were in Hayden.&amp;nbsp; (My note: Jennie was born in Wales, Utah.)&amp;nbsp; After moving to Cresent they had two more daughters: Alta and Mary.&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp; Alta died when she was only four days old; her death greatly saddened the family.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Pearl had a difficult time to attend her meetings at that time.&amp;nbsp; To b
2 CONC e active in church was always a great desire of her heart but they had to travel three miles to Cedarview by horse and wagon or else walk.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Pearl had very high blood pr
2 CONC essure which caused her death at the early age of 47 and one-half years.&amp;nbsp; In March of 1924, her second daughter, Pearletta, had a baby by the name of Gertha who became very ill which fact upset Pearl very much.&amp;nbsp; Her husband, Jabe
2 CONC , and oldest son Earl, had gone to Carbon County to work in helping remove bodies from the Castlegate Mine explosion; their being away was extremely difficult for her.&amp;nbsp; When word came that her little graddaughter, Gertha, had passed away i
2 CONC t seemed more than she could bear.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;At the time of her stroke, (My note: so this must have caused a stroke.) her youngest child, Mary, knew her mother was very ill.&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp; Little Mary, then five years old, had seen the consecrated oil used to heal the sick.&amp;nbsp; She couldn't think of anything else to use to hlep her mother, so she climbed up in the cupboard, got the oil, and put some on her mother's head.
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; Pearl lived a day or two with the paralysis increasing; this stroke was on her right side.&amp;nbsp; Family and friends helped to care for her.&amp;nbsp; She wrote messages to her loved ones with her left hand.&amp;nbsp; She wrote of ho
2 CONC w extremely anxious she was about "Papa and Earl" because of their being at the mines.&amp;nbsp; On the 18th of March, she wrote a letter to Jennie, this was prior to her stoke, and pleaded with her to come back to Duchesne County to live because s
2 CONC he had heard of her oldest daughter's poor health in the mining camp.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Peal's daughter, Jennie along with her husband Sam Roberts, and Pearl's husband Jabe, and son Ear
2 CONC l, traveled back to the Basin as soon as they received word of Pearl's illness.&amp;nbsp; When Pearl passed away on March 28, 1924, her family was able to be there, with the exception of Jennie's little girls who were left at home with friends beca
2 CONC use of the terrible weather conditions and hazardous roads over the mountains.&amp;nbsp; Jennie's little sister, Mary, came back to the coal camp along with Earl, Maurel, and her father Jabe.&amp;nbsp; Pearl's sister, Sarah or Rhae Mecham Barton, a
2 CONC s she ws later known, was unable to have children of her own, so she persuaded Jabe to her her rear Mary as her own daughter; he gave his consent so Mary went to Monticello to live.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line>
2 CONC <line>&lt;p&gt;Pearletta Jane Mecham Taylor was buried in the Cedarview Cemetery on the 31st of March, 1924.&amp;nbsp; Many testimonies have been given, over the years, of the sweet kind nature which she possessed.&amp;nbsp; She loved people and ha
2 CONC d the ability to maintain her special gifts through trials, hardships and adversities.&amp;nbsp; Her strong testimony of the Gospel never waivered and her great faith truly helped her family then, as well as now, it being a great strength to all he
2 CONC r posterity.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
