2 TITL Quenby Hall
1 _OID 6aee96d5-6498-4fd0-b35c-adab39408348
1 DATE 1627
1 PLAC Lecester, England
1 _DSCR Quenby Hall was built in 1627. It remains a perfect and unspoiled jewel of a High Jacobean country house – the finest in Leicestershire and one of only a handful of such houses remaining in the UK. Many of the rooms are the same as they were when t
2 CONC he architect conceived them with beautiful proportions, intricate plaster ceilings, finely worked panelling and fireplaces. It has the advantage of presenting a spectacular face to the world whilst being of a manageable size.
2 CONT   
2 CONT  
2 CONT       
2 CONT       
2 CONT  Quenby’s great claim to international fame is that Stilton cheese was first made here, by the housekeeper. Her daughter lived at the staging inn at Stilton, and sold her mother’s cheeses…the rest is history. The Old Dairy is now available for func
2 CONC tions: Stilton on the menu is optional!
2 CONT 
2 CONT It is the home of the de Lisle family whose ancestry in England dates back to the Norman conquest of 1066, and have lived in Leicestershire for over 300 years. Quenby was built by George Ashby, and it only passed out of the Ashby family’s hands a
2 CONC t the turn of the twentieth century. 
2 CONT 
2 CONT For most of the nineteenth century, as a popular part of the Quorn hunt territory, it had been rented to various keen hunting enthusiasts including the 5th Marquess of Waterford and Viscount Downe and possibly the Empress of Austria: the house wa
2 CONC s widely famed for its hospitality and many wild post-hunting parties – it was Waterford and his friends who were the origin of a well known phrase when they supposedly ‘painted the town red’ (Melton) after a particularly lively session! A ballad o
2 CONC f the time is featured below.
2 CONT 
2 CONT It was bought in 1904 and restored by Lady Henry Grosvenor who made many Edwardian improvements and restored much of the Jacobean interior after it had been georgianised by Shuckburgh Ashby in the mid eighteenth century. 
2 CONT 
2 CONT Only twenty years later it was bought by Sir Harold Nutting who lived and hunted happily here for 50 years until his death in 1972. We know that the Queen of Denmark and Princess Alice, Duchess of Gloucester and David Niven stayed here during thi
2 CONC s era.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Squire de Lisle then bought Quenby, to replace the family seat at Garendon Hall which was demolished in 1964. Quenby has been extensively restored and now offers the highest level of warmth and comfort whilst remaining faithful to its Jacobea
2 CONC n heritage. 
2 CONT   
2 CONT   
2 CONT   
2 CONT     
2 CONT       
2 CONT  The Ballard of Quenby Hall 
2 CONT 
2 CONT By The Hon. Gilbert Leigh of Hayne, Tiverton, N. Devon 
2 CONT (thought to be written between 1850 – 1900)
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1
2 CONT Ye gentlefolks of Melton, 
2 CONT Of Harborough and Belton,
2 CONT From Leicester and Dalby,
2 CONT from Somersby Hall,
2 CONT Where Welland’s river glideth and Austria’s Empress rideth,
2 CONT Come hearken to the Steeplechase 
2 CONT We rode at Quenby Hall.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 2
2 CONT From Canterbury’s city,
2 CONT Where Beckett prayed for pity,
2 CONT Where the Black Prince’s helmet hangs
2 CONT Mouldering on the wall,
2 CONT Where officer and soldier
2 CONT Entrance each fair beholder,
2 CONT Came gallant Major Phillipps to 
2 CONT Ride at Quenby Hall.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 3
2 CONT Alas! The Coplow Bottom,
2 CONT Alas! The fools, ( ‘od rot ‘em)
2 CONT Who filled his glass with sherry
2 CONT When much he wished for port,
2 CONT Alas the course so hilly
2 CONT That jumped out ‘Diebry (?) Billy’
2 CONT The liquor died within him and spoiled
2 CONT The Major’s sport.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 4
2 CONT From that proud club so partial,
2 CONT So stately and so martial,
2 CONT Where feeds the festive Coldstream
2 CONT And drinks the Grenadier,
2 CONT Came many a dashing hero
2 CONT Without a thought of fear Oh!
2 CONT They took the train to Leicester and dropped a parting tear.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 5
2 CONT What brook or rail can frighten
2 CONT The pride of the Allrighton,
2 CONT As ‘Donington’ and Foster 
2 CONT Come proudly stepping forth.
2 CONT And Thomas Wood Esquire
2 CONT Whom the ladies all admire,
2 CONT And Anderton from Yorkshire
2 CONT And Napier from the North.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 6
2 CONT All hail to thee my ‘Hoppy’
2 CONT The ground though wet and sloppy
2 CONT And the fences that gape at us the 
2 CONT Stoutest hearts appal.
2 CONT Yet the powder of your cellar
2 CONT Would embolden any feller
2 CONT To start for the Great steeplechase
2 CONT We rode at Quenby Hall.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 7
2 CONT Upon a lantry flier
2 CONT Here’s one from Warwickshire
2 CONT Tho’ trembling in his stockings for
2 CONT Much he fears a fall.
2 CONT But wave the banners o’er us
2 CONT And ring the bells in chorus,
2 CONT For here comes Charlie Wolseley
2 CONT Who won at Quenby Hall.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 8
2 CONT How shall I sing their glory
2 CONT Or how relate the story
2 CONT The hedges and the ditches, the bullfinches and all.
2 CONT The osiers and the railings,
2 CONT The bottoms and the palings
2 CONT We crossed in the Great Steeplechase
2 CONT We rode at Quenby Hall.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 9
2 CONT And when the chase was over, sir,
2 CONT And ‘Flirt’ the prize had won, sir,
2 CONT We slaked our thirsty palate
2 CONT In the sparkling wines of Gaul.
2 CONT We toasted deep the winner,
2 CONT And then we went to dinner.
2 CONT Thus ended the Great Steeplechase 
2 CONT We rode at Quenby Hall.
2 CONT   
2 CONT       
2 CONT       
2 CONT 
