1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;Extracts from a letter wrtten by his brother Francis in 1987 to James&amp;#39;s family in Australia.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;#39; After leaving school at the age of thirteen Jim went to work in a cotton mill fo
2 CONC r a couple of years then worked in a coal mine for a year or two and spent the rest of his working years in England at Leyland Motor Works as a dresser, a job consisting of smoothing engine castings of brass and aluminium in readiness for assembly
2 CONC . In the year 1927 during the time of the spring he immigrated to Australia. The British Government painted a glowing picture of Australia, plenty of work to be had and good wages, also a grant to assist immigrants&amp;nbsp;in their passage to be p
2 CONC aid back when they had security of work. Well things were not so rosy when he arrived in Australia, there was plenty of unemployment and very few jobs. In the autumn of 1927 the stock market crashed and the industrial world was plunged into the gre
2 CONC at depression which lasted until 1939 when world war two started. Jim received a letter once in a while reminding him of the debt he owed the British Government. He felt that under the circumstances he owed them nothing and changed his name from Mo
2 CONC on to Hagan.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
