2 TITL Enterprise of the Moon family in Liverpool
1 _OID 7382e699-9247-4fd0-acd6-2460734745d9
1 DATE 1819
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;When the will of Robert Moon was proved 16 August, 1819, An
2 CONC n Moon, the widow was of Catterall in the parish of Garstang, and her sons&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;James, Richard, Edward, and William, were&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;merchan
2 CONC ts in Liverpool. James Moon was enrolled as a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;free burgess of Lancaster 18067, and described as son of Robert Moon of Catterall and manufacturer of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="
2 CONC font-family: times;"&gt;Manchester. In 1817/18 the burgess roll of Lancaster enters John and Richard Moon, sons of Robert Moon of Catterall, merchants, of Liverpool. Cores&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Directory of L
2 CONC iverpool, 1821, gives Moon Bros. &amp;lsquo;count&amp;shy; ing house&amp;rsquo; as 10, Manesty Lane, and Richard Moon of 4, Irvine Street, Mount Vernon, William, 53, Isling&amp;shy; ton, and Edward, 25, Pembroke Place. In 1827 the Moon&amp;rsquo;
2 CONC s oflice was 121, Tithebarn Street, Edward was at 75, Islington, James, 17, Everton Terrace, and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Richard at 5, Irvine Street, Edgehill. In 1829the Moon&amp;rsquo;s oflice was 125, Titheb
2 CONC arn Street, Edward lived at 1, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Soho Square, James, 4,'Lodge Lane, Everton, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Richard, 5, Irvine Street, Edgehill. By 1835the counting hous
2 CONC e was at 96, Tithebarn Street, Edward was at 9, Shaw Street, Everton, James at the Rectory, Walton, Richard at 47, Shaw Street, Everton. In 1841, the Moon&amp;rsquo;s oflice was 10, Tithebarn Street, Edward had&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=
2 CONC "font-family: times;"&gt;moved to 103, Shaw Street, Everton, Richard, his brother was at 3, Shaw Street, and his son Richard at 4, Shaw Street, Everton, James being still at the rectory, Walton. By 1845 Moon Brothers, general&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;merchants, were still at 10, Tithebarn Street, Edward was now at the Lawn, Aigburth (later the home of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;80&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style
2 CONC ="font-family: times;"&gt;Parkers) James at the rectory, Walton, and their &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;nephew Richard at Claremont, Sandforth road, West &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Derby. In 1853, E
2 CONC dward Moon, merchant, was of Bank Chambers and The Lawn, Aigburth, James of Bronte House, Everton, and 34, Exchange Street, Robert &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Moon, barrister at law, younger brother of Richard &lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;and son of the older Richard, was at 26, Bedford Street, South, and William Moon, merchant, also of 34, Exchange Street and of Oxton Hall.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><l
2 CONC ine>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
