2 TITL Family History by William Alvin Thayn Part I
1 _OID 75e5db59-0f7d-4023-a625-8e92e5e64547
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The Provo River bottom was then a jungle. A person could hardly get through it. There were red willows a foot through, cottonwood, elder, kinni-kinnick
2 CONC , hop vines and grasses that grew as high as a man's head. If horses ate much of it, it made them crazy, so we called it crazy grass. There were lots of choke cherries, the bushes were three or four inches thick and twenty or more feet high. Sarve
2 CONC s berries grew on bushes as large as the choke cherries. The berries were large, nearly as large as tame ones.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; It was a dry fall and my father st
2 CONC arted&amp;nbsp;a fire in it. It was a terrible fire. The blaze would go as high as the tall cottonwood trees. I never saw the fire, but I saw what it did after the fire went out. The willows, dry grass and hop vines had accumulated for years. In pl
2 CONC aces it burned the roots to the ground.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Aunt Sydney made her own lye and soap. She had a letch where she put the ashes in. It was made by hewin
2 CONC g a trench in a log, as one would make a pig trough. Nailed boards about four foot long, the bottom in the trench in the log, and two feet&amp;nbsp;wide at the top and both ends were nailed up. Water was put in the ashes and drained, and run out th
2 CONC e ends of the trough into buckets. they would put in fresh ashes and let it go through again until it was strong enough to eat up the fats and grease as well as the lye you buy now. There was so much timber that you could shovel up bushels of ashe
2 CONC s in places.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; I did not stay long at home. I was only ten years old, but I was sent with a team and wagon back to the sawmill in Thayn's Canyon. I
2 CONC t was about 25 miles. I got to Round Valley about sundown. I was afraid to go up the canyon alone, as it&amp;nbsp;would be dark long before I got to the mill. I stayed at a ranch, Killfarl Ranch. I was just going to ask them if I could stay all nig
2 CONC ht, when one of them, one of the men working at the mill came along, and wasn't I glad ? I stayed at the mill until they closed down for winter. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;Elisha and Eliza were running the sawmill. They move
2 CONC d the mill to Woodland.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; We had several cows and yearlings to drive, Albert Peters and I. It was dark going down Rose Hollow and Alber
2 CONC t droe a hawthorne in his foot (we both were barefoot). Then I had to drive the cows alone. We stopped all night at Cuffs. We reached home the next day.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The folks had houses built then. That was the fall of 1875. The snow came early. We had a pair of horses, a white and a bay. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;They ran away and went back to the old home in Salt La
2 CONC ke. My father had them fed that winter.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; My father took me to Salt Lake and I stayed that winter with my Aunt Fanny. I went to school in the&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;Second Ward. Aunt Fanny was sure a good cook. She had what we called a steptower stove. The front lids were about six inches lower than the front lids. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;She sure could make the finest biscuit
2 CONC s I ever ate. I thought so. The stove was in front of the old fireplace whichwas boarded up and used as a woodbox. All I had to do was keep it full of wood, and she would let me go skating or anyplace I wanted to go.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Aunt Fanny never had any children. She married a German. He was well educated. His name was William Ammernan. My mother lived in the next lot, ( the lot where his mot
2 CONC her lived before she passed away), and I saw her almost every day.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; As far back as&amp;nbsp;I can remember, William sat in a big chair a
2 CONC nd never lay down. When it was nice weather he would walk outof the house with his cane and Aunt Fanny would put his chair wherever he wanted it. At night he sat by her bed with his cane, should he want anything he could touch her with his cane, an
2 CONC d&amp;nbsp;she would get it for him. The winter of 1875 she was a widow. Uncle William had been dead several years.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; A man by the name of Ball wa
2 CONC s our teacher. He had about 120 pupils, all grades, from beginners to boys grown. I did not learn much. The teacher had no time to spend with classes and when he went home for dinner, which he always did, he locked the door of the school house. Th
2 CONC e greater number of students could not go home for dinner. One cold snowy day, some large boys, as large as men, kicked the pickets off the fence which was in front of the school house and made a fire for the children to warm by. After that the sch
2 CONC ool was left unlocked. The snow was a foot deep. That was the last of February 1876.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Shortly after that, there was two boys, Flagstaff and Wilson
2 CONC . They were fully grown. They were wrestling just before recess was called. Flagstaff threw Wilson down and as they were getting up, he pushed Wagstaff and that started a fight. The bell rung just&amp;nbsp;as they were starting to fight. They agree
2 CONC d to have it out after school. After school, they went three blocks from the school. They took off their coats and belted up their pants. It was a stand up and a knock down. They went in for it for all they were worth. When they got tired they woul
2 CONC d rest and go at it again. Finally they went down in the Brigham Young farm and fought it out. Wagstaff or Flagstaff made Wilson say enough.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Th
2 CONC e very last of March 1876 my father and I, and I think Nephi Thayn was with us, started for home, to Woodland from Salt Lake. We started with wagons and finished with sleighs. The snow at Woodland was two or three feet deep everywhere except on th
2 CONC e hills. Hay could not be bought for $100 per ton.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Eliza Jones had her first baby April 16, 1876, Easter Sunday, Elisha Allan Jones. Mrs. Russel
2 CONC l was the mid-wife. The roads then were impassable and they needed some medicine and some syringes. Albert Peters and I went to Kamas for them. The snow all over the bench was about a foot and a half deep, but where the sleighs had run it was&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;three feet deep and hard. We walked over and back about 14 miles. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; It was about the 1st of May that we started to plow. We planted some whea
2 CONC t and potatoes. Aunt Sydney had a fine garden. There were so many rabbits that she had to fence it so the rabbits and chickens couldn't get in. She cut stakes about three inches through and five and a half feet long. We sharpened and&amp;nbsp;drov
2 CONC e them into the ground, took willows and wove them around to the top.&amp;nbsp;They sure kept out the chickens and rabbits. The sawmill was moved over from Thayn's Canyon on to Provo River. The logs &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&g
2 CONC t;were hauled from Riley's Canyon and Bench Creek. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; I never saw anything like it, there was a fisherman, he would fish with hook and line. He wou
2 CONC ld get between 35 and 40 pounds before breakfast. We had a fish trap, we caught all the fish we could eat the year round. The grouse and prairie chickens, when they were half grown, could be killed with clubs. We would get a half of dozen or more
2 CONC . Once in awhile we would see a gray wolf or a lynx. The next winter I&amp;nbsp;did not go to school (1877).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The next spring we cleared off some&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;more ground. Quaking Ash, Choke Cherry and other brush. We plowed with two yolk of oxen. In part of this land, we plowed up dozens of buffalo heads. Some of them measured 18 inches between the eyes. You could pick up buffalo horns, all siz
2 CONC es, all over the country. There were lots of buffalo wallers especially up in Pine Valley.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The first spring we lived there, bears would come with
2 CONC in a hundred feet of the house. The front paws of the grizzly bear was so wide it took both hands to cover one track. The pigs had to be put in the pens that the bears could not take them out. A couple of years later, Elisha Jones had a big fat pi
2 CONC g that slept under the shed. About 12 o'clock a grizzly bear took the pig. He must have carried it in his front paws. Ephraim&amp;nbsp;Green was with Elisha Jones, they said it squealed. When he would set it down, it would grunt, he set it down thr
2 CONC ee times as he carried it over the river, then it gave one big squeal and he killed it. The next morning they found where he had killed it and eaten some of it. They were satisfied that the bear would return for more pig so they set a gun, so if th
2 CONC e bear came back it would fire the gun and be killed. The bear came back and set the gun off and shot his lower jaw off. The bear escaped. Then Galloway, an old trapper and hunter followed the bear up Bench Creek. He found the bear lying under a bu
2 CONC nch of willows. The bear was only a rod or so away when Galloway first saw him. The bear made for him once. The hunter killed him before&amp;nbsp;he could do him harm. They said he weighed 1,100 pounds. &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small
2 CONC ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
1 _CREA 2021-08-04 10:09:08.000
1 _USER 5zlwyTokkr1Jeyb6oJq1/RkypEC5kBXh7sGmWzBrzrE0HjOKr6aNQObXzlbw5nUHr3ttR38hqbdlcHa1ncWVng==
