2 TITL Letters I received giving Historical Data by Nathan Andrew Kemmish
1 _OID 7aa81339-1fc9-4a79-b306-8390dadbb837
1 _DSCR More excerpt from Nathan Andrew Kemmish Genealogy Book
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Under this heading will be many interesting letters I received while trying to get authentic history of the family in England.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&amp;nbsp; In 1902, I wrote a letter addressed to :&amp;quot;Anyone named KEMMISH at Portsmouth, England.&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp; I received the following reply:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;39 Victoria Road, Southsea, Portsmouth, May 28, 1902.&lt;
2 CONC /p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sir:&amp;nbsp; Your letter of May 24 was delivered to me.&amp;nbsp; My name being Kemmis (by Marriage only).&amp;nbsp; As the name of your familty is only slightly different having an H at the end and mistakes often made in my name
2 CONC , you will I am sure, excuse it being opened by me, owing to the difficult address to trace who it was meant for.&amp;nbsp; I will try and find out if anyone of the same name is in Portsmouth now and your letter shall be given to them through the P
2 CONC ostal Authorities.&amp;nbsp; I am the widow of the late Captain George Kemmis.&amp;nbsp; He had an uncle named Richard who also died some years ago but these are from an Irish family, so suppose these are not&amp;nbsp;the people you desire to trace
2 CONC .&amp;nbsp; I shall do what I can to help you.&amp;nbsp; Faithfully yours, (Noria Kemmis to Nathan Kemmish)&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; In 1903, I discovered the Cosmopolitan magazine, Vol. 30, for Novembe
2 CONC r 1900, April 1901, Page 380, a picture of a Valentine printed by W. Kemmish, No. 17 King Street, Borough, Southwick, England, dated 1797.&amp;nbsp; This was the oldest known valentine in existence.&amp;nbsp; The article was written by Fred H. Baer
2 CONC , Chamber 203 of Commerce, Cleveland, Ohio.&amp;nbsp; I wrote Mr Baer in February 1903 and received the following reply:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;New York, Chicago &amp;amp; St. Louis Railway Co., Frank H. Baer, Chief Contracting Agent, Clevelan
2 CONC d, Ohio, February 21, 1903&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mr. N.A. Kemmish, Lincoln, Nebraska&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dear Sir:&amp;nbsp; Replying to your letter of February 18, I regret that I am unable to give you any information about W. Kemmish, who was the publi
2 CONC sher of the valentine writer you refer to.&amp;nbsp; So far as I have been able to discover he was the &lt;u&gt;first publisher of valentine writers, &lt;/u&gt;and a copy which I have dated 1797, seems to be the earliest publication of its kind tha
2 CONC t has turned up.&amp;nbsp; I have never seen any other book bearing his imprint and have never learned anything about him.&amp;nbsp; Jonathan King, 304 Essic Road, London, England, an old and retired manufacturer of valentines might be able to giv
2 CONC e you some information about W. Kemmish, as he has been in the historical side of valentine manufacturing and I know he has dug up a good may facts about early makes.&amp;nbsp; He is a courteous old gentleman and would, I am sure, not be displease
2 CONC d to hear from you.&amp;nbsp; Regretting that I cannot be of better service to you, I am,&amp;nbsp; Yours very truly, Frank H. Baer&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; I, thereupon, wrote Mr. King and he sent me th
2 CONC e following reply:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp;302 Essen Road London N.&amp;nbsp; 20-3-03&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dear Sir:&amp;nbsp; I am unable to trace the name from your description.&amp;nbsp; If I saw the valentine, I may recognize how t
2 CONC o trace it.&amp;nbsp; There is no such name (W. Kemmish) in the London Directory now.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Yours truly, J. King&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Having only a magazine print of the valentine I could not send him the valentine.&l
2 CONC t;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The following article appeared in a Honolulu Newspaper February 1952, under a column BROADWAY AND ELSEWHERE by Jack Lait:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &amp;quot
2 CONC ;For 200 Years, Valentines were fashioned by hand; made of everything from paper to satin; written in ink, or carved by knife.&amp;nbsp; The masses had to wait for the invention of lithography.&amp;nbsp; But when they were still being done by han
2 CONC d they could find help in such pamphlets as &amp;quot;Polite Valentine Writers&amp;quot;.&amp;nbsp; These contained proper sentiments for many callings and trades, so a fishmonger could write: &amp;quot;You are the girl I take delight in, much mor
2 CONC e than Haddock, Smelts or Whiting.&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp; The oldest know of these booklets was published in Britain in 1797, and was know as Kemmish&amp;#39;s Annual and Universal Valentine Writer, or the Lover&amp;#39;s Instructor.&amp;quot;&lt;/p&g
2 CONC t;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; In 1908, I wrote another letter to Southsea and received the following card from Mr. C. H. Tyffe:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;St Hellens College, Southsea, May 17, 1908&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dear Sir:
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; There is a Sidney John Kemmish listed in the directory at 54 Tennyson Road, Landport and a William Kemish (with one &amp;quot;m&amp;quot;)29 Hope Street, Landport.) The only ones in the neighborhood of Portsmouth.&amp;nbsp; C.H. Tyffe&am
2 CONC p;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; On May 31, 1908, I wrote to Sidney John Kemmish and recieved the following reply.&amp;nbsp; (However, I did not write to William Kemish.)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;34 Southsea Str
2 CONC eet, Southsea, Portsmouth, Hampshire, England, July 6, 1908&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dear Sir:&amp;nbsp; I received your letter of May 31 addressed to me at Tennyson Road, Landport Hampshire, from which address I have moved.&amp;nbsp; I am now residing a
2 CONC t Southsea.&amp;nbsp; Replying to your inquiry regarding my ancestors I am very pleased to furnish you with all the information I possess.&amp;nbsp; Since recieving your letter I have written several relatives on my late mother&amp;#39;s side for a
2 CONC ny information they could give in reference to the KEMMISH family. I gained very little.&amp;nbsp; I know personally scarcely anything of my father&amp;#39;s relatives.&amp;nbsp; My Father, John, who died in 1884, was for 27 years the Deputy Chie
2 CONC f Constable of West Sussex England Police Force.&amp;nbsp; He lost his father when he was very young.&amp;nbsp; He had several brothers and two or more sisters.&amp;nbsp; One brother went to America many years ago (Charles?) perhaps.&amp;nbsp; Anot
2 CONC her went to Jersey or Guernsey.&amp;nbsp; One settled down in New Forest, England.&amp;nbsp; My father was born in Buckershard, a small village near Beauliew Hampshire, about 20 miles from here.&amp;nbsp; His name was John.&amp;nbsp; One brother&am
2 CONC p;#39;s name was James.&amp;nbsp; You say your Grandfather left England about 1850 or 1853.&amp;nbsp; That was several years before I was born.&amp;nbsp; One of my father&amp;#39;s sisters, named Mrs. Harding, was for years the village schoolmistre
2 CONC ss at Buckleshard.&amp;nbsp; I think most of my relation living at the above named village were in the farming line.&amp;nbsp; A KEMMISH who lived at Southsea years ago was, I am told, carrying a business as fruiteres and greengrocer.&amp;nbsp; The
2 CONC re is not another KEMMISH in Portsmouth or Southsea at the present time, but myself.&amp;nbsp; When I was a boy living with my parents at Petworth, Sussex, I remember my father saying he had relations at Southsea.&amp;nbsp; In one of the Portsmout
2 CONC h and Southsea cemeteries I found records of the following deaths:&amp;nbsp; 1. Mary Ann, wife of Joseph Kemmish, died April 25, 1857, aged 67 years.&amp;nbsp; 2. William Kemmish, died February 28, 1852 aged 42 years&amp;nbsp; 3.&amp;nbsp; Henry Ke
2 CONC mmish, died April 28, 1841, age 23 years.&amp;nbsp; 4.&amp;nbsp; Joseph Kemmish, died April 24, 1873, age 88 years.&amp;nbsp; 5.&amp;nbsp; John Grant Kemmish, (Grandson) died in infancy.&amp;nbsp; Have you the Christain name of your Father and Gran
2 CONC sfather?&amp;nbsp; I should like very much to know how you obtained my name and address.&amp;nbsp; I must now draw to a close hoping the little information my letter contains will be of some service to you.&amp;nbsp; I shall be very pleased to hea
2 CONC r from you again.&amp;nbsp; Yours truly, Sidney John Kemmish&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mr. N. A. Kemmish 2819 Vine St., Lincoln, Nebraska&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></con
2 CONC tent></metadataxml>
