2 TITL Funeral Services for Andrew Dudley Jr.
1 _OID 8095e3e8-a9d2-4823-947d-fa638a5f5b06
1 DATE 11 May 1944
1 PLAC Vernal Express
1 _DSCR Funeral Services for Andrew Dudley Jr.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;font size="1"&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;FUNERAL HELD AT JENSEN FOR ANDREW DUDLEY&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;JENSEN (Special)--A large crowd attended the funeral services for Andrew Dud
2 CONC ley, 73, on Thursday at 2 p.m. at the Jensen Ward Chapel. Mr. Dudley died Sunday April 30 at 5:45 a.m. at the family home following a three day illness of bronchial pneumonia.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Wallace Moon of th
2 CONC e Jensen Ward bishopric conducted the services. Rebecca Stewart had charge of the decorations and the profuse floral offerings.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;The opening song &amp;quot;I Know That My Redeemer Lives&amp;quot; w
2 CONC as sung by a ladies chorus composed of Mary Gardner, Winona Wilkins, Josephine Stewart and Ellen Wilkins. Mrs. Mary Slaugh accompanied all the selections. The invocation was offered by Melvin Wilkins, Mary Gardner and Josephine Stewart sang &amp;qu
2 CONC ot;Cast Thy Bread Upon The Water&amp;quot;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;The first speaker Bishop J. Ross Merrell told of Mr. Dudley filing a 27 month mission to the Northern States. He served under Bishop George Billings lat
2 CONC er under Bishop Archie S. Richardson as a counselor in the bishopric. At the time of his death he was a high priest and had spent several winters doing temple work. He came to Jensen 57 years ago.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt
2 CONC ;Archie S. Richardson a former bishop of Jensen Ward told of his association with Mr. Dudley as a counselor in the bishopric. Carwin Snow and Naomi Dudley sang a duet, &amp;quot;In The Garden&amp;quot;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size=
2 CONC "4"&gt;President Archie Johnson was the concluding speaker. Remarks of appreciation were given by Wallace Moon. The closing song was by a ladies chorus &amp;quot;How Firm a Foundation&amp;quot;. Benediction Ira S. Gardner.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;The pallbearers were A. Demar. Parley, Wendell, Howard J. and Lynn H. Dudley, four sons of the deceased. Lee Johnson , a son-in-law and Curtis Dudley, a nephew. Interment was in the Jensen Cemetery under the direction o
2 CONC f the Swain Funeral Home. Charles Crow, an uncle dedicated the grave.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Andrew Dudley is survived by his wife Sina Jensen Dudley four sons, A. Demar, Parley, Wendell and Lynn H. of Jensen; Howard J.
2 CONC , Arlington, Virginia; three daughters, Mrs. Lucy Johnson of Los Angeles, California; Mrs. Irene Wankier of Whitney, Nevada and Mrs. Mrs. Myrtle Peterson of Boulder City, Nevada. All were present at the funeral. Also four brothers, Joseph S. Dudle
2 CONC y of Jensen; Sanfors Dudley of Grand Junction; Stephen M., Alberta, Canada and Hosmer Dudley, Greenwood; Alta Canada. Three sister, Mrs. James Campbell of Vernal; Mrs. Joseph Butcher of Ballard and Mrs. Otis Witherell of Salt Lake City, Utah also s
2 CONC urvive.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;-Vernal Express, May 11, 1944, transcribed by Rhonda Holton&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
