2 CONC an id="Community" class="mw-headline"&gt;Community&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The village contains a primary school, the Wheatsheaf pub and a church. The school is C of E and has a strong link with the church.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;
2 CONC St Anne's Church is the location of the grave of Meg Shelton, the notorious Fylde Hag, who was buried there in 1705 after being accused of witchcraft.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Parish" class="mw-headline"&gt;Parish&lt;/span&gt;</line><line
2 CONC >&lt;p&gt;The parish includes the villages of Eaves, Catforth and Lower Bartle. Higher Plumpton is also included in the parish, but like Lower Bartle, is a very small hamlet.&lt;sup&gt;[1]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;In the 19th cent
2 CONC ury Woodplumpton was known as &lt;strong&gt;Plumpton-Wood&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;sup&gt;[2]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The parish was part of Preston Rural District throughout its existence from 1894 to 1974.&lt;sup&gt;[3]&lt;/sup&gt; I
