2 TITL History of Martha Marena Woodland Gruwell
1 _OID 84d15cd1-aec4-4390-8251-96f4655d94ae
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line> 	 	&lt;div style="width: 100%"&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;History of Martha Marena Woodland Gruwell&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;Martha Ma
2 CONC rena Woodland&lt;br&gt; Dec 17, 1855 &amp;ndash; Apr 25, 1919&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt;      The parents of Martha Marena Woodland were Thomas Steepleford  Woodland and Margaret Whitaker.  They crossed the plains in 1849; their  mode of travel being a c
2 CONC ow and horse hitched to a wagon.  They had one  baby, Thomas, and were quite comfortable and very happy.  Margaret said  they would drive the cow (Little Pidey) all day and then milked at  night.  They lived in Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah, fo
2 CONC r awhile and  then moved to Willard, Box Elder, Utah, where they eventually built a  spacious, beautiful rock house which is still standing (2007).  In this  house they raised nine children.  At first they were very poor, but  being frugal they alw
2 CONC ays had the necessities and comforts of life.   Before long there were &amp;ldquo;well-to-do&amp;rdquo;.&lt;br&gt;      Martha Marena was born 17 December 1855 in Willard, Box Elder,  Utah.  She was a twin and her twin&amp;rsquo;s name was Margare
2 CONC t Malena.  They  were the third and fourth children of the nine.  Little is known of  Martha&amp;rsquo;s youth except that the twins changed places many time for fun.&lt;br&gt;      She was baptized by William McKee in July 1869.&lt;br&gt;      O
2 CONC n 21 April 1873, at the age of 18, she married a widower, Melvin  Lowry Gruwell.  He had three children from his previous marriage to  Martha&amp;rsquo;s aunt, Martha Jane Woodland, who passed away 29 June 1872.  The  oldest was five, the younges
2 CONC t was a little over a year.  This was quite  an undertaking for a girl her age.  They were married in the Salt Lake  Endowment House, Salt Lake City, Utah, and went to live at Melvin  Lowry&amp;rsquo;s ranch in Marsh Valley, Oneida, Idaho.  His fir
2 CONC st wife, Martha  Jane, had a child named Parley Gould from a previous marriage, and he  was also living with Melvin Lowry.  &lt;br&gt;      It was in Marsh Valley that Martha Marena began her family with the  birth of Oliver Lowry on 11 March 1874
2 CONC .  The second child, Martha  Mevina, was born 9 May 1875, a little over a year later.&lt;br&gt;      The Indians would occasionally go on the warpath.  One day when  Melvin Lowry was away from the ranch house, two buck Indians rode into  the yard a
2 CONC nd sharpened their knives on the grindstone.  One took the  other by the hair and drew his knife across his throat, pointed to  Martha and said &amp;ldquo;squaw.&amp;rdquo;  This frightened Martha so badly she fainted.   When she regained conscious
2 CONC ness, the Indians had gone, leaving her  unharmed, but she was so frightened and upset that she gave birth to a  premature baby.  The baby, named Cornelius, was born 23 February 1876  and the death date is given as the same.&lt;br&gt;      The nex
2 CONC t child, a boy named Eugene, was born in 1877 and died in  1877.  Margaret Josephine was born on 24 September 1878.  Daniel E., the  nine-year-old son of Melvin Lowry and Martha Jane, died 17 October  1879.  Also that year Melvin Lowry was called a
2 CONC s a branch president, and  then on 16 November 1879, he was called as bishop of a newly-formed  ward.  Preston Thomas was born 27 February 1880.&lt;br&gt;    In the 1880 Census, Martha Marena was with her father in Willard, Box  elder, Utah.  Prest
2 CONC on Thomas was a baby and Martha was listed as having  a wound in the leg.  She was evidently there for medical care.&lt;br&gt;      She gave birth to Orval Merrill, 8 July 1882, back in Marsh Valley.   During this time (1882-83) Melvin Lowry serve
2 CONC d in the Legislature and  House of Representatives.&lt;br&gt;      At this time, polygamy was practiced in the LDS Church.   Consequently, Melvin Lowry married 21-year-old Helen Gemmell on 1 May  1884 in the Endowment House.  Helen (Mellie) is desc
2 CONC ribed as &amp;ldquo;a  beautiful, talented, and well-educated [Scottish] girl who received her  education in a convent&amp;hellip; and was loved by everyone. [Johnson, Annie,  Heatbeats of Colonia Diaz]  Several months after the marriage, Melvin  a
2 CONC nd Martha&amp;rsquo;s eighth child, Aaron Card was born 3 October 1884.&lt;br&gt;      Helen gave birth to a daughter, Rebecca, in Marsh Valley on 11  February 1885.  She died that same month.  She then had a son, Angus  Reeder, born in Hyde Park o
2 CONC n 21 January 1887.  [Why this child was born  is Hyde Park is not known.]  Ruth Melena was born to Martha on 30 May  1887 in Marsh Valley.&lt;br&gt;      After Melvin lost his arm in a farming accident and also became  hard of hearing, Martha Maren
2 CONC a was a faithful little helpmate and  assumed many a burden to lighten his load.&lt;br&gt;      Melvin Lowry was released as bishop on 3 October 1887.&lt;br&gt;      In 1887, Melvin went to Mexico with his third wife, Helen, and left  the ranch t
2 CONC o be sold by Martha Marena.  She sold the rights to the  ranch the next year to her brother, Thomas, and with her children began  the trek to Colonia Diaz, Mexico.  The family and all their belongings  were loaded in a wagon pulled by a team of mul
2 CONC es.  Each night they would  hobble the mules.  When they were in the middle of Utah, one night the  mules got away.  The boys tracked them back about ten miles, then  brought them back and hooked them on the wagon so they could proceed.   We can on
2 CONC ly imagine the thoughts and feelings that must have been in  Martha Marena&amp;rsquo;s mind as she waited and wondered if she would see her  boys or the mules again.  Millicent Jane was then 16, the youngest child  of Melvin Lowry and his first wif
2 CONC e.  Oliver, Martha Marena&amp;rsquo;s oldest boy  was then 14 and Preston was 8.&lt;br&gt;      On 21 September 1888, Lorin Taylor was born to Helen in Colonia Diaz.&lt;br&gt;      The first child born to Melvin and Martha Marena in Colonia Diaz  w
2 CONC as named Charlottie.  She was born 27 March 1889 and was always called  &amp;ldquo;Lottie.&amp;rdquo;  Helen had a little girl named Mahettable on 6 April 1890.   Eliza Ann was born 6 February 1891 and died in February of that same  year to Marth
2 CONC a Marena.&lt;br&gt;      Nora was born to Helen on 19 January 1892.  On 7 February 1892,  Lorin Taylor, Helen&amp;rsquo;s son, died at three-and-a-half years old.  On 3  March 1892 Helen&amp;rsquo;s Mahettable, who was almost two years old, fell  t
2 CONC hrough some loose boards into the well and was drowned.&lt;br&gt;      On 21 April 1892 Lester James was born to Martha Marena and Melvin  Lowry.  That same year Melvin&amp;rsquo;s third wife, Helen, died on 7 September  1892 at age 29 leaving tw
2 CONC o children for Martha Marena to raise&amp;mdash;Nora then  eight months old and Angus Reeder who was five years old.  Martha had  eight children of her own ranging from 18 to four-and-a-half months at  this time.  &lt;br&gt;      Three years late
2 CONC r on 7 February 1895, Clarice was born to Martha.   On 30 December 1897, another daughter, Eva, was born to her.&lt;br&gt;      In 1898, Martha went to Willard, Utah, by train to visit her folks, passing the Grand Canyon on the way.&lt;br&gt
2 CONC ;      In or around 1899 Martha Marena and her son, Preston, contracted  typhoid fever.  This was probably caused from storing food in buckets in  open wells to keep it cool.  Both recovered from the disease, but  Martha Marena was never the same a
2 CONC fter having this disease.  Modern ways  of treating typhoid were not known at that time.  As a result of their  having typhoid, there were compelled to close the open wells.&lt;br&gt;      On 9 July 1900, Oliver Lowry, 26 years old, the oldest so
2 CONC n of  Martha marena and Melvin, was shot and killed by a man named Adams  following an argument at a cattle auction.  Because Melvin could not  hear, Oliver was representing his father.  The man who shot him fled and  actually rode his horse paste
2 CONC d the Gruwell home and never returned.&lt;br&gt;      Melvin and Martha Marena&amp;rsquo;s last child, Nellie, was born 31 July  1902, when Martha was 47 years of age.  In later years Nellie changed  her name to Maurine.  When Nellie was about te
2 CONC n years old, Melvin Lowry  died on 4 June 1912 in Colonia Diaz at age 78.&lt;br&gt;      Martha Marena never liked anyone saying anything against Melvin  Lowry.  When any of the girls talked about anything that was not  complimentary to Melvin, sh
2 CONC e would crochet faster and tap her foot at an  accelerated pace.&lt;br&gt;      In 1912 the Mexican Revolution began and all the white people were  forced to leave old Mexico.  They packed their personal belongings in  wagons and left everything el
2 CONC se and came to the States to make their  home.  Martha couldn&amp;rsquo;t bring their live chickens, so she fried them  before leaving.  Out of 8,000 head of cattle and the land they owned,  they got out with only their personal belongings, wagons
2 CONC , and 32 head of  horses.&lt;br&gt;     They came out of Mexico by way of New Mexico.  They spent one week  at Dog Spring Ranch in New Mexico and one month at Hachita.  From Here  they went into El Paso, Texas, to the railroad and went by rail t
2 CONC o  Idaho.  From here the family scattered in all directions.  Marta Marena  settled in Burley, Cassia, Idaho, bought a home and took in boarders for  a living.  She died 25 April 1918 at the age of 63 in Salt Lake City,  Utah, and was buried in Bur
2 CONC ley, Cassia, Idaho at the Mountain View  Cemetery.  The trials of this life that were overcome by this good woman  attest to the fact that she was, indeed, a choice daughter of her  Heavenly Father.&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt;      Following are some obse
2 CONC rvations about Martha Marena from those who knew her:&lt;br&gt;      Edith Gruwell, daughter-in-law:  &amp;ldquo;She always wore a &amp;ldquo;Fascination  Bonnet,&amp;rsquo; crocheted and like a scarf.  Nora was always very attentive and  made sur
2 CONC e her mother&amp;rsquo;s hair was tucked in and that she looked &amp;lsquo;just  right.&amp;rsquo;&amp;rdquo;&lt;br&gt;      Nora Mallory, step-daughter:  &amp;ldquo;Martha Marena&amp;rsquo;s philosophy was, &amp;lsquo;He  that serves most&amp;hell
2 CONC ip;&amp;rsquo;  She was the most wonderful woman I ever knew.  She  kept a picture over the fireplace and would tell me, &amp;lsquo;This is your  mother.&amp;rsquo;&amp;rdquo;&lt;br&gt;      Orval Gruwell, son:  &amp;ldquo;She could sew anything&am
2 CONC p;mdash;with her family she  had to!  She could make the best-baked powder biscuits I ever ate.  She  had very snappy black eyes and black hair.&amp;rdquo;&lt;br&gt;      Estella Gruwell, daughter-in-law:  &amp;ldquo;Here is Martha Marena&amp;rsquo
2 CONC ;s chili sauce recipe&amp;rdquo;:&lt;br&gt;           24 ground-skinned tomatoes&lt;br&gt;             6 good-sized chopped onions&lt;br&gt;             2 T. salt&lt;br&gt;             1 T. small peppers&lt;br&gt;              &amp;frac12; cup suga
2 CONC r&lt;br&gt;              &amp;frac12; cup horseradish&lt;br&gt;      Add cloves, cinnamon and allspice in a cloth.  Boil until thick and seal.&lt;br&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
