2 TITL Family History written by William Alvin Thayn Part 2
1 _OID 88bcdbc4-7d3a-435b-8d79-3343b6f3bf3d
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; There were lots of berries, gooseberries and wild strawberries. We used to go up the south fork of Provo River, stay all night, pick bushels of raspberr
2 CONC ies. There seemed to be no end of them. Most of the women and children picked hops. They grew on the river bottom. They picked tons of them and sold them in Salt Lake City. I think the brewer bought them. The women used hops in bread making.&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; My father went to Salt Lake City and asked to be ordained a missionary to the United States, Canada, and Great Britain. He started without purse or scr
2 CONC ipt like the old missionaries. I know he must have, and started without money. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Aunt Sydney's sister married a man by the name of Jonathan Hall
2 CONC . I think Aunt Sydney's sister was alive at the time. My father traveled to Salt Lake City and stayed with her. He traveled to New York, across the ocean to Liverpool and to Scotland and back to Canada. There he borrowed enough money to get home fr
2 CONC om his cousin, John Haggart.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; My father and Brigham, myself and Hyrum got out poles to fence his farm and we got the money to pay his ti
2 CONC cket or what he borrowed to get him home. When my father went on his mission he turned the sawmill and teams over to Nephi. Then Nephi became mine and Albert Peter's boss. The first winter we did not do much. The next spring the sawmill was up on B
2 CONC ench Creek. Albert&amp;nbsp;Peters and I had to go and stay at the mill all the time, night and day. We had no house to live in. We had a kind of shed over the belt to keep it dry. We made a kind of shed out of lumber to sleep in and keep the rai
2 CONC n off. Nephi and John M. Reid and others that helped do the logging and run the mill went home every night and got a good supper and breakfast. All Dutch and I had to eat was what they brought up each morning, and they brought us some potatoes an
2 CONC d some salt side. If you think that wasn't a poor living! Elizabeth, Nephi's wife was a poor bread maker and all the bread we got was what she&amp;nbsp;sent us. I could not tell you what we endured and went through that summer.&amp;nbsp;I was 13 ye
2 CONC ars old and Albert or Dutch as we called him was 14.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The first thing that happened to me was&amp;nbsp;that Nephi had to have a letter or messag
2 CONC e sent across the river which was as high as it got that year. John M. Reid was four or five years older than I, but I was sent. I was scared to death, but I could not refuse to go. I guess he figured I could keep my head better than the others
2 CONC . I had to follow the reffle in a circle, if I got below or above , the horse and I would have gone down the river. I will say that Aunt Sydney, my stepmother, was mad at Nephi for sending me, and she told him never to do a trick like that again, s
2 CONC end a boy where he was afraid to go himself.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Albert and I stayed there all summer, at the mill. There was one electric storm. We weren't 40 or 5
2 CONC 0 feet from where the lightning struck. We two little boys sure got a wetting from the rain storm that evening. We went all summer without shoes, and in October when we would go home Sunday morning there was frost on the ground. We would go as far&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;as we could until our feet got cold. Then we would sit down and hold them in our hands until they got warm, then we would go on again. Of course we got shoes before the snow fell. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style
2 CONC ="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; We left the mill and went&amp;nbsp;home. Aunt&amp;nbsp;Sydney gave Albert a whipping for something. I can't remeber what it was for. I was off doing something and did not hear her whip him nor hear wha
2 CONC t he said, but I suppose he said plenty.&amp;nbsp;I never got to say goodbye. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;The&amp;nbsp;last time I saw him he was going down the bench toward Kamas. I heard he stayed that night at Silas Peck'
2 CONC s place. That was the last time I saw him for about five years. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; We stayed home that winter. Ephraim Green went with my half sister, Sy
2 CONC dney Florence. She was red headed. He would come to see her nearly every night. He was a good singer. We would&amp;nbsp;have him sing every night. Every night Aunt Sydney would sing, and we did enjoy the singing until our bedtime. Some nights Ephra
2 CONC im would stay all night and sleep with some of the boys. Albert was always playing jokes on him, so we could hear when he went to bed. He would tie a rope across the hall, or a bucket or chair or anything to make a&amp;nbsp;noise.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/
2 CONC p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Aunt Sydney got letters from my father often. I know he wrote to Sidie to put off getting married to Ephraim until he got home. I don't think he was in favor of t
2 CONC he marriage. I don't know why they did not wait. April 1879, Nephi took Ephraim and Sidie to Salt Lake. They were married in the Endowment House. There was no temple then. It was about 50 miles from Woodland to Salt Lake and coming back they founde
2 CONC red one of the horses. He was so stiff they could not get him home. They borrowed a little mule from H.H. Cuff. The next day or two I had to take the mule back to Cuff's place, about 15 miles. The mule was sure skittish. He threw me off a dozen tim
2 CONC es. If a bird flew up he would jump sideways and turn around, but I got through him&amp;nbsp;alright. The horse I brought home was so stiff I walked all the way home.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt
2 CONC ;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; It was a very light winter that year and the Provo River never got so high we could not ford it.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; My father got home the fir
2 CONC st part of June or the latter part of May. He had borrowed money from his cousin, John Haggart. They ran a&amp;nbsp;large foundry and machine shop in Canada and were well fixed. I think they&amp;nbsp;lived in Brampton in Canada. Nephi sent my fathe
2 CONC r money and he borrowed the rest from his cousin to pay his fare from Canada to Salt Lake City. He brought some presents for us all. He had a nice lot of cards he brought from Paisley, Scotland where the great thread factory is located, J.P. Coate
2 CONC s and O.N.T. They were mostly cards advertising their thread. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; He brought&amp;nbsp;each of us a fancy colored handwork made by&amp;nbsp;Canadia
2 CONC n squaws. Mine ad Edgar's were in the shape of a heart. Some were made in the shape of a purse and in the shape of a box to hold perfume or cards. They&amp;nbsp;were sure beautiful. They were all made by Indians in Canada. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</
2 CONC line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; As soon as my father got home the first thing he did was to get out several hundred poles for Henry Moon, Mary Ames's husband to get money to send back to John Haggart.&
2 CONC amp;nbsp; The money my father borrowed. We got the poles and caled it the Duckman's Bason. They were fine poles, we generally got two poles out of one tree. We coud only take a wagon part way up and take the first part of the wagon to where the pol
2 CONC es were cut. We loaded the poles on the front part and let the back drag the same as you would with a cart. When we got down to where we left our hind wheels of our wagon, then we coupled the wagons together and loaded them on the wagon. We had on
2 CONC e Baso&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;n wagon and as Brigham was turning the hind wheel around to couple them , he started to, and dropped his hands on a rock and tore the nails and the flesh loose from the two middle fingers. W
2 CONC e pressed them back and washed them. They hurt him so that he could not ride and he had to walk and hold his hand all the way home. They got well and left only a small scar. Hyrum Thayn was with us and drove one of the teams. I drove the other.&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; My father moved the mill&amp;nbsp;up&amp;nbsp;Riley's Canyon and we sawed lumber there. That must have been about 1880. We had built a small frame h
2 CONC ouse to live in. We had a place for door and window, but we had not put the door and window in yet. We had a bunk where we slept in the corner away from the door and window. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: sm
2 CONC all;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Brigham and I and a boy about our age by the name of Dave Jackson, (he was the brother of Sufernia Ellis) were at the mill one night when we were visited by a brown bear. When the dog saw the bear he yelped and ran und
2 CONC er the house, when the bear stuck his nose into the doorway and sniffed. We were sure a scared bunch, but the bear went about his business. We had no gun, not even an axe to defend ourselves, but the bear could not have found us in that fix again!&
2 CONC lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; We sawed&amp;nbsp;lumber there that summer and fall of 1881. I drove two yoke of oxen and had a new wagon--nearly a new one. We loade
2 CONC d freight at Park City for Fort Thamburge, situated on the Green River, afterward called Oray. We left Heber City, went up Daniel's Canyon to the Summit and down in Strawberry Valley, we traveled very slowly. There must have been at least 16 teams
2 CONC . they were all two yoke teams. We crossed Red Creek&amp;nbsp;(the soil is red, something like the Park at the head of Coal Creek and Soldier Creek), we crossed Curn Creek&amp;nbsp; and then down a steep&amp;nbsp;mountain road to the Duchesne River
2 CONC . The hill was line shale, cedar, and pinion pine and greasewood on the bottoms. The river was quite wide where we crossed. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; We camped there fo
2 CONC r noon. There were about six horse teams came along. They called them Lankford's&amp;nbsp;teams. They had been freighting in Montana before hauling freight&amp;nbsp;from Park City. They drove different than I ever seen before. The driver rode the n
2 CONC ear wheel horse and drove he leaders with a pair of lines. Of course, he could handle the wheel horses as he rode one of them. The pair in&amp;nbsp;the swing he drove as you would oxen, gee and haw. They were fine teams, some were all grays, other
2 CONC s blacks and bays.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;(Unfortunately this is where the story ends, The othe
2 CONC r pages are missing)- Madaline Thayne-Hussey&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
