2 TITL About Christopher Tiffany
1 _OID 90d283ae-b14c-4cff-8c06-5c89c448b93d
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;span style="font-family: arial, 'helvetica neue', helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12px"&gt;&lt;div style="border-bottom-style: solid; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-color: #cccccc"&gt;About Christopher Tif
2 CONC fany&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial, 'helvetica neue', helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; line-height: 17px"&gt;The following was found on worldconnect.rootsweb.com as compiled by Ara O. And Annie R. Call. Van Jesen
2 CONC , who posted the information, can be reached at:&amp;nbsp;vanjensen@@hotmail.com&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial, 'helvetica neue', helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12px"&gt;&lt;div style="word-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-wo
2 CONC rd; padding-right: 0px; padding-bottom: 0px"&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Christopher Tiffany (Tiffaner) (~1763-1809)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&
2 CONC gt;[1] In the Vermont Federal census of 1790 [actually done 1791 when VT became a state] we found Christopher Tiffany [spelled Tiffaner&amp;mdash;Original spelling??] living in Cambridge, Lamoille County. The family is listed as 1 male and 5 female
2 CONC s. This would have consisted of Christopher, the father; his wife, Rebecca Ellis Tiffany; and their four oldest daughters, Betsy, Lovisa, Susan, and Sarah (or Sally, our ancestor). Searching through the early town records of Cambridge, Vermont we f
2 CONC ound recorded there: 2 June 1787 a page filled with births of children to quite a number of different families. Many of the older children were listed as having been born in Bennington, Vermont. Others showed no place of birth. Among those withou
2 CONC t a place of birth shown were Betsy Tiffany who was born 15 Aug 1784, and her sister Lovisa [Louisa?], born 20 December 1786, born to Christopher Tiffany and his wife Rebeccah. From this record it appears that many of these people had once lived i
2 CONC n Bennington, but they had arrived in Cambridge as a group shortly before the recording of the names of their children. We then looked in the volumes of the Vermont Gazetteer to see if we could find any further information on the Tiffany family. I
2 CONC n volume II, pages 611 and 622, we found lists of the names of the early settlers of Cambridge. Following the name of Christopher Tiffany we read that he was one of Burgoyne&amp;#39;s Dutch [Deutsch/German] soldiers. The writer further pointed ou
2 CONC t that most of the settlers of Cambridge from 1780 to 1800 had immigrated from Bennington.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;[Burgoyne was a British general whose army of about 8,000 men, about hal
2 CONC f German and half British including contingents of Loyalists and Iroquois Indians, was captured in the American Revolution Battles of Bennington and Saratoga in August to September 1777. The captured British and German soldiers (called Hessians bec
2 CONC ause many came from the German principality of Hess; although many of the German soldiers captured at Saratoga and Bennington were actually from the German principality of Brunswick) were sent to prisoner of war camps in Massachusetts with the Germ
2 CONC ans eventually being shuttled to camps in Pennsylvania and Virginia before the war ended in 1783. Ironically as part of the Saratoga campaign, Burgoyne had ordered British loyalists and Mohawk and other Iroquois Indian allies to advance down the Mo
2 CONC hawk river and join him. American New York militia ( more than 2/3 were second/third generation German-American farmers) under General Herkimer intercepted them and fought them to a draw in the bloody battle of Oriskany (Aug 7 1777). The American
2 CONC s had their German allies too. The captured German soldiers, whose parole could have been bought for some money and the promise not to fight against the Americans, were not wanted back in Brunswick etc. because the German princes thought they woul
2 CONC d be bad for recruiting additional forces. About 5,000 of the 30,000 German troops (mostly drafted or impressed) hired by the British never returned to Germany after the war was over. Some were killed or died but most had decided to stay in America
2 CONC n and Canada. The Americans encouraged them to fight on their side or desert and many of them did. Figuring, probably correctly, that living in America where land was almost free and many opportunities existed was better than going back to a German
2 CONC y where they were nearly always low man on the totem pole.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Tiffany is not a German name&amp;mdash;its English. Christopher&amp;rsquo;s name as listed in the censu
2 CONC s of 1790 and 1800 is Tiffaner (not Tiffany). Since Christopher may have had no opportunities to learn how to read or write he may or may not of known how to spell his original name and so the name listed is probably how the census taker thought i
2 CONC t sounded. He may also have been trying to make his name more English sounding than whatever his original name was? Records exist of most of the hired German troops so in principle it may be possible to track down his &amp;ldquo;real&amp;rdquo; nam
2 CONC e &amp;ndash;Tiffner, Diefenbacher, Tiffenbacher, Diener, Diefer or&amp;hellip;?? One other question is his date of birth, some list it as 1763 (in 1777 he would have been 14&amp;mdash;not likely), others in 1740 (more plausible but is someone gues
2 CONC sing or is there some other information). Since we don&amp;rsquo;t know for sure when and where he is was born or even his original name it is a interesting puzzle]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt
2 CONC ;From the Encyclopedia Britannica, 1965, V19, p 988 we quote: &amp;quot;Burgoyne, with about 8,000 men, including seven regiments of British regulars and 3,900 Germans, reached Ticonderoga July 1 [1777], ....A German detachment,....was sent to Benn
2 CONC ington to seize horses and supplies, but was surrounded and almost &amp;ldquo;annihilated&amp;rdquo; [about 200 killed and 700 captured] by the [New Hampshire and Massachusetts milita] including some [Vermont] Green Mountain militia, under Genera
2 CONC l John Stark (Aug. 16).&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Another quote from the same reference, 3, 482; gives further information: &amp;quot;...as Green Mountain Boys under the leadershi
2 CONC p of Ethan Allen and Seth Warner, they organized in August 1777 to resist Gen. John Burgoyne and his Loyalists and Hessians on their march to capture the Bennington stores of arms and supplies. The patriots set forth from the Catamount tavern unde
2 CONC r Gen. John Stark [from New Hampshire] and defeated the British at the battle of Bennington August 16, 1777, a battle which was [the start of] a turning point in the American Revolution.&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;pre style="white-space: normal !import
2 CONC ant"&gt;[The New England colonies had required since their start in the 1630&amp;rsquo;s that essentially all able bodied men sign up to militia units and receive militia training. Lexington, Concord, Bunker hill etc. were all fought by these force
2 CONC s. They weren&amp;rsquo;t the best trained or equipped troops in the world but they would and could fight for what they believed in or to defend their homes as the British had already found out. Washington had sent word for all available militia fr
2 CONC om Massachusetts, New Hampshire, Connecticut and Vermont to congregate and help General Gates defeat British General Burgoyne. Unfortunately for the Germans and British soldiers, Bennington was chosen as a militia congregation point by General Star
2 CONC k so they attacked a force of over 2000 militia instead of the 400 at most they expected. To help Gates, Washington also sent one of his most aggressive Generals, Benedict Arnold, and one of his most effective forces, Morgan&amp;rsquo;s Rifles, und
2 CONC er the Colonel Daniel Morgan of the 11th Virginia Regiment with over 400 specially selected Virginia riflemen, chosen for their sharpshooting ability. All, the militia, Arnold and Morgan, did outstanding service in the Battle of Saratoga. Gates Ame
2 CONC rican army strongly supported by militia forces from New York, Massachusetts (included Maine), New Hampshire, Connecticut, Vermont, Morgan&amp;rsquo;s Virginia sharpshooters and the vigorous leadership of General Benedict Arnold eventually out foug
2 CONC ht and overwhelmed Burgoyne&amp;rsquo;s army and forced its surrender. This in turn encouraged the French and Spanish (both ruled by Kings) to support the Americans&amp;mdash;not because they believed in their cause but because they hated the Briti
2 CONC sh. This support was crucial in getting arms and money needed for the long war and in the final battle of Yorktown in 1781 the French fleet held off the British fleet long enough for a good part of the British army to finally surrender.]&lt;/pre&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;From Salde&amp;#39;s State Papers of Vermont (pg 62) we learn that prisoners taken in this battle [of Bennigton] were held for security in a church in Bennington. Vermont G
2 CONC azetteer, I, pg 666, states that on 17 Oct. 1777, Gen. Burgoyne, after exhausting his food and ammunition was compelled to surrender 5,751 British and German troops to Gen. Gates at Saratoga. The British campaign of [1777] began in Canada the earl
2 CONC y part of June. Burgoyne&amp;#39;s proud troops, consisted of about 3,900 German mercenaries (called Hessians) 3,000 British and 650 Canadian plus Indian auxiliaries, ascended Lakes Champlain and George. They captured Crown Point and Ticonderoga. T
2 CONC he strategy was that they were to receive reinforcements from the west [defeated in the bloody battle of Oriskany] and [from General William Howe] from the south and cut New England off form the rest of the colonies. [Howe decided to attack Philade
2 CONC lphia instead, which he did capture and forced the Continental Congress to move. Later he decided holding Philadelphia was too big a strain on British resources and they retreated back to friendlier environs of New York City.] These reinforcement t
2 CONC roops and supplies never arrived and the campaign failed to attain its objectives. Other references say these Hessian soldiers from Germany, who made up a substantial part of Burgoyne&amp;#39;s forces [3,900], were not merely hired rabble, but wer
2 CONC e &amp;ldquo;professionals&amp;rdquo; ...well paid and well fed (both the pay and the food were very poor by today&amp;rsquo;s standards). They were well disciplined, both in warfare and in their mode of life. Religion was a powerful influence in t
2 CONC heir lives. Since England didn&amp;#39;t have sufficient army for the American War, these Hessian soldiers were hired because money was needed [by the princes in charge of] their principalities. [Germany at this time was not a united country bu
2 CONC t a large group principalities; each mostly independent. King George III of England was well acquainted with these German princes since that&amp;rsquo;s where his family came from&amp;mdash;George I was ruler of the German Kingdom of Hanover and Gr
2 CONC eat Britain when he became king in 1719!] They did their duty bravely and faithfully. They made a good impression on the Americas. Only a few deserted during the war, but after, many [about 5,000] remained in America.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-r
2 CONC ight: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;In reading of these battles against Burgoyne, we learned that many members of the Ellis family served with the Americas. Barnabas Ellis, who came from Hebron, Conn. ---the same town where Rebecc
2 CONC a Ellis was born-- was a member of an expedition under the command of Ethan Allen in defending Ticonderoga and Crown Point. In the battle of Bennington he served as a lieutenant under General Stark. Lyman Ellis was in Bennington and saw the Hessia
2 CONC n Prisoners. His unit took part in the pursuit [and capture] of General Burgoyne at Saratoga, New York.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Facts which led us to look to the Bennington, Vermont record
2 CONC s were as follows: 1) Many early settlers of Cambridge, Vermont came from Bennington; 2) Christopher Tiffany served under Gen. Burgoyne; 3) Burgoyne&amp;#39;s troops (at least some of them) were held as prisoners in a Bennington church; 4) Christop
2 CONC her Tiffany&amp;#39;s wife was Rebecca Ellis; and 5) the Ellis&amp;#39; were active in the battles against Burgoyne.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;In the genealogical library in Salt Lake the Be
2 CONC nnington town records are given on film 6615, pt. 1. On page 195 we found the following entry: &amp;quot;March 23, 1784 Christopher Tiffiner took Rebeckah Ellis to his wedded wife and likewise Rebeckah Ellis took the said Christopher Tiffener to (b
2 CONC lank) her wedded husband&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Nathan Clarke&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Justice of the peace&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&
2 CONC lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;We continued through the records, looking for births of children to this couple, but we found none. On page 198, however, we found the following entry: &amp;quot;To the Const
2 CONC able In the name and by the authority of the freemen of the state of Vermont you are hereby commanded to warn Christopher Tiffeney and Rebeckah his wife to depart immediately. Given under our hands 21st day of April 1784 Bennington&amp;quot;&lt;/p&
2 CONC gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;We found the same notice given to many other families also. We were told that during that period of time families who supported or were [suspected of being] sympathetic t
2 CONC o the British were asked to leave settled communities. It is quite probable that these families who were asked to leave, left as a group and traveled together to find new homes...with babies being born along the way. They settled in north central V
2 CONC ermont in the settlement of Cambridge in Lamoille County. Christopher took the freeman oath in Cambridge 6 September 1785, a year and a half after being expelled from Bennington. Almost two years later on 2 June 1787, he registered the births of hi
2 CONC s two oldest daughters on the same page with births of the children of many other families. Since Betsey was born in [18 Aug] 1784 [in Cambridge], she was probably born somewhere en route, and Lovisa was born in [20 Dec] 1786 in Cambridge, a year a
2 CONC fter her father became a freeman.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Volume II of the Vermont Gazetteer states that Christopher Tiffany was a Congregationalist [the name of the original Puritan churc
2 CONC h] who had &amp;quot;helped lay foundations of Cambridge&amp;#39;s social institutions in rite and thruth.&amp;quot; These early settlers of Cambridge, with Christopher listed as one of them, were all good citizens of their community.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;
2 CONC p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Christopher Tiffany signed his name with a cross when he sold land in Cambridge 19 December 1793. Between 1787 and 1800 four more daughters and two sons were born to Christophe
2 CONC r and Rebecca Tiffany in Cambridge, making eight children in all.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Rebecca died 14 June 1805. Christopher then married Abigail ______and had two sons by her. Hyrum w
2 CONC as born 19 May 1807 and Christopher was born 21 May 1808, two months after the death of his father, Christopher who died 21 March 1809. [others say he D: 1815 Cambridge, Lamoille, VT]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; li
2 CONC ne-height: 17px"&gt;After Christopher&amp;#39;s death, Abigail married Theolopus Larabee on 31 December 1816. Births and deaths of this family are recorded in film 2593, pts. 255 and 256. Christopher&amp;#39;s will was probated six weeks after Abig
2 CONC ail&amp;#39;s marriage or on 17 February 1817. [Film 6741, pt.3 G vol]. The will is given on pages 164 to 174. The estate totaled 1558.02. Although no dollar mark is given, it is assumed to a sum in dollars. In part, the will states: &amp;quot;Chri
2 CONC stopher Tiffany late of Cambridge Co of Franklin, District of Georgia, 3 day of January 1816 to Abigail Tiffany of Cambridge....&amp;quot; Mentioned besides Abigail was a minor daughter, Phebe Tiffany, unmarried, and 17 years old.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p st
2 CONC yle="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;In volume H of the Cambridge records, pages 354-365, under the date of 17 February 1817 it lists Abigail Tiffany as having married Theopholes Larabee. Heirs listed as receiving du
2 CONC e notice were: Ebenzer Bellow, Cyral Call, Stephen Phillips, Robert Griswald, and Johnathan Turrell. [These 5 are all sons in law]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Abigail received &amp;#39;1 ful
2 CONC l part of all real estate of late Christopher Tiffany and 1/3 of homes, barns, etc. except two rooms.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Hiram Tiffiner received &amp;#39;27 acres and 1/3 o
2 CONC f home and barns, but 2 rooms.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Christopher Tiffiner received &amp;#39;24 acres and 1/3 of home and barns, except 2 rooms.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="ma
2 CONC rgin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Phebe Tiffiner received &amp;#39;7 acres of land, 2 rooms of house, $37.62.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;The 5 married daugh
2 CONC ters were remembered as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Betsy Turrell: &amp;#39;3.4 acres and 1/5 part of square room in house.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bo
2 CONC ttom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Louise Bellows: &amp;#39;3 acres and 1/5 part of square room in house.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Sally Call: &amp;#39;3 acres and 1/5 part of squar
2 CONC e room in house.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Susanna Phillips: &amp;#39;5 1/3 acres and 1/5 part of square room in house.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bott
2 CONC om: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Polly Griswald: 3.8 acres and 1/5 part of square room in house.&amp;#39;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;All the children have common privilege to the well in the w
2 CONC idow&amp;#39;s premises, and sufficient land around the house for depositing wood. All received use of common road through farm to get wood to the house. All received free access to such parts of the house as they respectfully own. All receive comm
2 CONC on privilege to the cellar and oven for all necessary uses. All have free access to the kitchen fire for one day in each week for washing. Fifty dollars was to be paid to the judge for administering the will or estate.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-
2 CONC right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Film 6741, pt.3 H pages 12-16 records that Samuel Tiffany of Cambridge died 3 January 1816 and that Cyril Call was appointed to administer his estate. He received $8 from the estate of Christop
2 CONC her, and had properties and cash amounting to $315.40. This included 40 areas of land. The signers, or those who agreed to the settlement, leaving most everything to Phebe Tiffany, were: Cyril Call, Ebenezer Bellows, Robert Griswald, and Stephen Ph
2 CONC illips. It was dated 24 Aug 1816.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;To try to get data on the ancestry of Christopher Tiffany in Germany, or possibly Holland, is an interesting genealogical challeng
2 CONC e. [Anson Call (son of Cyril Call and Sally Tiffany) in his autobiography states that Christopher was German.]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 15px; line-height: 17px"&gt;Compiled by Ara O. And Annie R. Call.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;
