2 CONC p;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;"EMIGRATION. . . . Elder Theodore Turley, who is on his return to his family, sailed from Liverpool for New York, in company with several others, on the 7th of August,&lt;/strong&gt;&
2 CONC lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[September] on board the packet ship, North America. We bid them God speed . . . ."&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;MS, 1:5 (Sep. 1840), p.136&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;"SECOND COMPANY. -- North America, abo
2 CONC ut 200 souls. Saturday, September 5th, 1840, Apostles Brigham Young and Willard Richards went from Manchester to Liverpool, and in the evening organized a company of Saints bound for New York, by choosing Elder Theodore Turley, a returning missiona
2 CONC ry, to preside, with six counselors, among whom was Elder William Clayton, one of the earliest English converts. Apostles Brigham Young and Willard Richards went on board the North America on Monday the 7th, and remained with the Saints on board ov
2 CONC er night. On Tuesday morning, about nine o'clock, the vessel was tugged out by a steamer. The Apostles accompanied the emigrants about fifteen miles, and then left them in good spirits.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The company had a prospe
2 CONC rous voyage to New York, where they arrived in the beginning of October, and from there they continued the journey to Buffalo, New York. Owing to the expensiveness of the route many of the emigrants fell short of means to complete the journey to Na
2 CONC uvoo; they therefore divided at Buffalo, a part going to settle in and around Kirtland, Ohio, while the balance, under the leadership of Theodore Turley, continued the journey to Nauvoo, to which place Joseph the Prophet states he had the pleasur
2 CONC e of welcoming about one hundred of them, about the middle of October, 1841."&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&g
