1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="CENTER"&gt;Deseret Evening News&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p
2 CONC &gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="CENTER"&gt;18 July 1918, 3/3&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="CENTER"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&
2 CONC gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="CENTER"&gt;Voice of Sweet Singer, Vilate Clayton Young, Is Stilled in Death&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="JUSTIFY"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&
2 CONC lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="JUSTIFY"&gt;Mrs. Vilate Clayton Young, wife of Heber Young, 163 B street, died this morning after a week&amp;rsquo;s illness following a stroke of paralysis on July 10. Mrs. Young had been in po
2 CONC or health for about 18 months but was not confined to her bed until last week. Yesterday morning she was considered to be much improved, but in the afternoon took a sudden turn for the worse and gradually grew weaker until her death. She was 73 yea
2 CONC rs old.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="JUSTIFY"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="JUSTIFY"&gt;Born in Nauvoo, Ill., Dec. 8, 1844, Mrs. Young wa
2 CONC s he daughter of William Clayton, the pioneer poet and historian, and his wife, Ruth Moon Clayton. In 1848 she came to Utah wit her parents and had lived in this state practically all of the time since. She was educated in the schools of the city a
2 CONC nd when still quite young exhibited her natural and inherited artistic ability along histrionic and musical lines. She was widely known in her early life as a singer and as a member of the old Deseret Dramatic association in which she was associate
2 CONC d with David McKenzie, John Lindsay, Phil Margetts and many others well known in the theatrical circles of those days. She was also a member of the tabernacle choir for over 25 years, having an unusually fine contralto voice and being well-remember
2 CONC ed by old timers for her duets with Mrs. Lavinia Careless, leading soprano of the choir in those historic days. Later she identified herself actively and zealously with the Relief society and other Church work. She was a lovely character &amp;ndash
2 CONC ; refined, gentle and a superior woman in every sense.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="JUSTIFY"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="JUSTIFY"&gt;On N
2 CONC ov. 24, 1870, she married Heber Young, who, with two daughters, Mrs. Ala Y. Godbe and Miss Clint Young, survive her. Besides her husband and children, Mrs. Young is survived by the following brothers and sisters: Newell H., A.J. and B.J. Clayton, M
2 CONC rs. Sarah Partridge and Mrs. Margaret Kimball, of Salt Lake, and a large number of half-brothers and sisters. Her brother David H. died only a few weeks ago.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p align="JUSTIFY"&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The funeral will be held Saturday at 4 p.m., at the residence of her son-in-law, M.C. Godbe, 101 B street. Interment will be made in the City cemetery.&lt;/p&gt;</li
2 CONC ne><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
