2 CONC 808 Elizabeth, the daughter of William B. Frodsham, and died in 1842, and is des&amp;shy; cribed as merchant in his will made June, 1842. He left &amp;pound;500, his gold watch, rings and trinkets to his son Richard; to his second son, Robert, &amp
2 CONC ;pound;500, his silver cups and silver bottle coasters. All his books and prints &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;were given equally between Richard and Robert. To &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;his daughte
2 CONC r, Elizabeth Ann Brocklebank, his silver coffee and tea pots, sugar basin, tongs and cream ewer, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;silver teaspoons, all plated articles, china and her&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-fa
2 CONC mily: times;"&gt;mother&amp;rsquo;s trinkets. To his daughter, Alice Mary, a silver&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;cream ewer and all his silver prongs or forks. His&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family
2 CONC : times;"&gt;daughter, Alice Jane, all silver table spoons and table&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;linen. Daughter Isabella all silver desert spoons,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;marr
2 CONC ow spoons and silver skewers. Daughter Mary&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Elizabeth, his silver fish slice, soup and sauce ladles,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;butter knife and sauc
2 CONC e boats. His grand pianoforte to&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;be enjoyed equally by his daughters, Isabella and Mary Elizabeth, and the first who married to receive it. To&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-
2 CONC family: times;"&gt;Ann, wife of Charles Pennington Eden, an annuity of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;&amp;pound;50, and &amp;pound;10 per annum to Charlotte and James Collins. He appointed his brother Edward, and sons Richar
2 CONC d &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;and Robert Moon to sell the residue of his estate and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;after settlement of the debts to raise &amp;pound;10,000 each for&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&g
2 CONC t;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;his children (I) Eliza Ann; (2) Richard; (3) Robert (4) Alice Mary; (5) Ann Jane; (6) Isabella; (7) Mary Elizabeth; in all &amp;pound;70,000 for his family.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&
