1 _OID 9e441f11-61d4-4d79-ac4b-a05af4c4c185
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-: 0px; line-height: 1.5em;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sir Richard Vyvyan, 1st Baronet&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(c
2 CONC . 1613 &amp;ndash; 3 October 1665) was an English politician who sat in the&amp;nbsp;House of Commons&amp;nbsp;at various times between 1640 and 1665. He supported the&amp;nbsp;Royalist&amp;nbsp;cause in the&amp;nbsp;English Civil War.&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-: 0px; line-height: 1.5em;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;Vyvyan was the eldest son of&amp;nbsp;Sir Francis Vyvyan&amp;nbsp;(1575&amp;nda
2 CONC sh;1635), head of one of Cornwall's leading families who had been both a Member of Parliament and&amp;nbsp;High Sheriff of Cornwall. He was of&amp;nbsp;Trelowarren,&amp;nbsp;Cornwall, when he was knighted at Whitehall on 1 March 1636,&lt;sup style=
2 CONC "line-height: 1em; font-weight: normal; font-style: normal;"&gt;[1]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;shortly after his father's death.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-: 0px; line-h
2 CONC eight: 1.5em;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;In April 1640, Vyvyan was elected&amp;nbsp;Member of Parliament&amp;nbsp;for&amp;nbsp;Penryn&amp;nbsp;in the&amp;nbsp;Short Parliament. He was elected MP forTregony&amp;nbsp;for the&amp;nbsp;L
2 CONC ong Parliament&amp;nbsp;in November 1640.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1em; font-weight: normal; font-style: normal;"&gt;[2]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;He supported the King in the Civil War and joined the&amp;nbsp;King'sParliament at Oxford rather than re
2 CONC maining at Westminster. He established a Royalist mint in Truro in 1642-43 and was Master of the Mint there and when it was moved to&amp;nbsp;Exeter&amp;nbsp;in September 1643.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1em; font-weight: normal; font-style: normal
2 CONC ;"&gt;[3]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sup style="line-height: 1em; font-weight: normal; font-style: normal;"&gt;[4]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;He was disabled from sitting in the House of Commons in January 1644. King Charles rewarded him for his loyalty by creatin
2 CONC g him a&amp;nbsp;baronet&amp;nbsp;on 12 February 1645. He was subsequently fined &amp;pound;600 and had his estate sequestrated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-: 0px
2 CONC ; line-height: 1.5em;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;After the&amp;nbsp;Restoration, Vyvyan returned once more to public life. He stood for as MP for&amp;nbsp;St Mawes&amp;nbsp;for the&amp;nbsp;Cavalier Parliament&amp;nbsp;in 1661 but wa
2 CONC s excluded in a double return. He was later elected MP for St Mawes in a by-election in 1663 and sat until his death in 1665.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1em; font-weight: normal; font-style: normal;"&gt;[5]&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line>
2 CONC <line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-: 0px; line-height: 1.5em;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;Vyvyan married Mary Bulteel, and was succeeded in the baronetcy by their eldest son,&amp;nbsp;Vy
2 CONC ell.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-: 0px; line-height: 1.5em;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 22px; line-height: 21px; font-family: sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="fon
2 CONC t-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-: 0px; line-height: 1.5em;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 22px; line-height: 21px; font-famil
