2 TITL Stories My Grandfather Tells Me
1 _OID a289491e-13a7-444a-8f8f-51125d3bd36f
1 PLAC Utah Territory
1 _DSCR As told to me by my Grandfather (Lurel Dean Hall) who knew Thomas Edward King (his grandfather) very well. 
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="text-indent: 0.5in; line-height: 200%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="text-indent: 0.5in; line-height: 200%"&gt;Thomas Edward King was born in 1867 in Utah and lived until 1964. He attended the first Pr
2 CONC imary organized by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and lived from a time when the fastest mode of transportation was a running horse until the United States successfully put a man on the moon. He was a very hard worker, always telli
2 CONC ng people that they were not working fast enough, but also a very caring man. &lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-indent: 0.5in; line-height: 200%"&gt;When he was a small boy, he was left at home for a short while with a young girl, about a year older th
2 CONC an him. The young girl was staying with the King family. At one point, a large Indian came around the house and took a liking to the young girl and her lovely hair. Thomas, seeing this as a golden opportunity, traded the young girl for some knick-k
2 CONC nacks from the Indian. The little girl was carried off by the Indian, leaving Thomas by himself.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-indent: 0.5in; line-height: 200%"&gt;When Thomas&amp;rsquo; parents (Thomas Franklin King and Lucy Ann Ogden) arrived at h
2 CONC ome, they asked Thomas where the young girl was. Thomas told his parents that he had traded her for some things from an Indian. Of course, young Thomas got into a lot of trouble for this and his father had to go out, find the Indian and trade him f
2 CONC lour and other things to get the little girl back.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
