2 TITL Sidney Boyer  1824 - 1901
1 _OID a293df3c-27b3-498d-bc44-586202022196
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;font size="1"&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sidney Boyer&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;(1824 - 1901) &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sidney Boyer was born in Pennsylvania in 1824. As a young girl she had a dream showing the very man she would marry. He was we
2 CONC aring Scottish clothing and she could see his face very plainly.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1848, a man came to her father&amp;rsquo;s door, dressed as in her dream and his features the same as she remembered. He was selling household cement. Her fathe
2 CONC r was interested and ask the young man to make their home his headquarters. This was John Johnson Thayne and in time his acquaintance with Sidney ripened into love and they wee married in October, 1848 in Pennsylvania.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;They live
2 CONC d in Ontario, Canada for a time where four of there eleven children were born and then moved to Iowa. Here they learned of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and accepted the gospel. They joined with the McArthur Company in 1861 and ca
2 CONC me across the plains. By this time they had seven children and Sidney was expecting another baby. They fared better than most because they had all the milk and butter that they needed on the journey. They arrived in Salt Lake on the third of Septem
2 CONC ber 1861.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;John worked at a sawmill up Millcreek Canyon, then they moved to Woodland, Utah and later to Wellington, Utah, where they lived the rest of their lives. The family had sheep and Sidney made good use of the wool; knitting
2 CONC &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;gloves, stocking, and sweaters. She made moccasins, earmuffs, quilts, and crocheted beautiful hairpin lace. She loved plants and flowers. She was tall and Straight and had piercing black eyes and black hair. She wore a little bla
2 CONC ck cap with two rows of lace around the edge. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sidney passed away in Wellington, Utah in 1901. Her husband passed away in 1910 also in Wellington, Carbon County, Utah.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1880 census living Peoa, Summit, Utah with h
2 CONC usband John J. Thayne and children Hyrum, William, Brigham, Edgar, Annie and Ebenezer Listed as a farmer&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
