1 _OID a2a800b0-808f-43a7-a462-263c18f9d39f
1 DATE 1830s
1 _DSCR Extract from Bland Mill story by Judith robinson - Sedbergh historic society
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px;"&gt;The &amp;ldquo;Extraordinary Termination&amp;rdquo; of Bland Mill in Howgill&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p
2 CONC &gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Judith Robinson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span styl
2 CONC e="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;On Sunday 2nd October 1870, a police constable from Kendal arrested the manager of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&
2 CONC gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Bland Mill in his bed at Mill House, Howgill, and took him back to Kendal to stand tria
2 CONC l for&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;larceny. It was not the first time tha
2 CONC t Henry Lawton had appeared in court but it was to be&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 1
2 CONC 4px;"&gt;the end of his tenure of the Mill.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Henry Lawto
2 CONC n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Henry Lawton was born about 1835 near Huddersfield, t
2 CONC he third child of a shoemaker and,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;after hi
2 CONC s father&amp;rsquo;s early death, was brought up by his mother, a milliner. By the age of 15, he&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, s
2 CONC ans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;had left Yorkshire and was apprenticed in a woollen mill at Meal Bank, near Kendal. After&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-fa
2 CONC mily: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;his apprenticeship, he stayed on at the mill as a spinner and in 1856 married a local girl,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&
2 CONC lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Mary Ann Long, who bore him two children1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;s
2 CONC pan style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;and then died aged 27 in 1862. When Bland&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Mill was offered for sale or to let in May 1866, Henry purchased the property, including the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Mill House and two cottages, from the retiring owner, William Greenwoo
2 CONC d Best. Henry&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;remarried the same year and liv
2 CONC ed in the Mill House with his two children, his second wife,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-
2 CONC size: 14px;"&gt;Jane Blythe, and subsequently his third child, Margaret Jane, baptised in Sedbergh in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, ari
2 CONC al, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;1868.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14
2 CONC px;"&gt;The Blythe family&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;The Westmorland Gazette recor
2 CONC ded Henry Lawton&amp;rsquo;s second marriage: &amp;lsquo;at St George&amp;rsquo;s&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;church in this town, by the Rev. E. Gabriel, Mr Henry Lawton of Bland Mill, Howgill, Yorks,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-
2 CONC regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;to Miss Jane Blythe, eldest daughter of Mr John Blythe, grocer of this town&amp;rsquo;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="col
2 CONC or: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;and the year&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto
2 CONC -regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;before, the death of Jane&amp;rsquo;s mother, &amp;lsquo;at No 2 Far Cross Bank, suddenly, Margaret, wife of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><l
2 CONC ine>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Mr John Blythe, grocer of this town, aged 52 years - much and deservedly respected.&amp;rsquo;3&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;There were eleven children in the family, of whom Jane wa
2 CONC s the eldest. It all sounds very&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;respectabl
2 CONC e - but all was not as it seemed in the Blythe family. John Blythe&amp;rsquo;s father&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"
2 CONC &gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Alexander (1769-1840), came from Wensleydale, where his family had for some&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, hel
2 CONC veticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;generations owned and run woollen mills and dye works, in Askrigg and later in Hawes.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color
2 CONC : #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Shortly before 1800, Alexander, a dyer, moved to Sedbergh, where his four children&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</lin
2 CONC e><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Richard, George, John and Elizabeth, were born in the early years of the nineteenth&amp;nbsp;&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;century. He returned to Hawes, with Richard and Elizabet
2 CONC h but the other two sons moved&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;to Kendal. I
2 CONC n 1830, his son George Blythe, a weaver, married Margaret Crawford, who had&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;sp
2 CONC an style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;been baptised in Sedbergh in 1813, as &amp;lsquo;Margaret, natural daughter of Esther Crawford,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-re
2 CONC gular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Pauper of Workhouse&amp;rsquo;. George died two years after his marriage and their only child, Mary&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;born in 1831, went to live with her Aunt Elizabeth in Hawes. But in the 1841 census,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Margaret is shown living in Far Cross Bank, Kendal, a &amp;lsquo;labour
2 CONC er&amp;rsquo; with two children, Jane&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;age
2 CONC d 7 and John aged 4. John&amp;rsquo;s baptismal record gives the mother as &amp;lsquo;Margaret Blythe,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, ar
2 CONC ial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;spinster&amp;rsquo;, although in fact she was a widow, as had been shown on the record of Jane&amp;rsquo;s&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="
2 CONC color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;baptism, when Margaret was living in Captain French Lane. And then, later in 1841, she&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p
2 CONC &gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;married her brother-in-law John Blythe, who at census date had been living in Finkle&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;Street, Kendal. He now moved to Far Cross Ban
2 CONC k where he and Margaret had a further&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;nine ch
2 CONC ildren, several of whom feature later in this article. So whether Miss Jane Blythe&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;was really the &amp;lsquo;eldest daughter of Mr John Blythe&amp;rsquo;, or in fact his step-daughter, is&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-f
2 CONC amily: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14px;"&gt;unknown.4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #222222; font-family: roboto-regular, helveticaneue, arial
