1 _OID a2daa2e3-f617-4963-999f-9fc9f2f39bf3
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div style="color: #333331; font-family: verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 14px;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>AUNT DAISY MOON</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-s
2 CONC ize: 17px; line-height: 28px; margin-: 22px; margin-bottom: 0px; font-family: verdana; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -0.1px; white-space: pre-line;"&gt;Author Unknown Aunt Daisy Moon was born August 23, 1892 at Woodruff, Idaho to Ephraim Thomas a
2 CONC nd Mary Ann Davis Moon. She was the 4th child in a family of 5 children, plus 1 half-sister and 2 half-brothers from her mother&amp;rsquo;s previous marriage to Wm. Aldredge. This was a very close-knit and loving family. During Aunt Daisy&amp;rsquo
2 CONC ;s 85 years, she has experienced the keen sorrow of parting with all of her immediate family. Her arrival over there will complete this family circle and it will be a most happy family reunion. She spent much of her life on the Henderson Creek ranc
2 CONC h. She loved the land and enjoyed being out in the fields with her father. She and her sister, Annie each owned a field in Cherry Creek. She also loved the animals, and made sure that they had the best of care. Life on the ranch was very happy, esp
2 CONC ecially for the children who came there to visit. They loved Aunt Daisy and thought of her as a great companion and entertainer. She enjoyed singing, laughter and fun. She was cheerful, witty, kind, resourceful, and understanding and made the child
2 CONC ren feel happy and comfortable. She went hiking, fishing and horseback riding with them. Aunt Daisy was an expert driver and liked to fill the old Ford Touring car with children, drive to a deserted country road, set the gas control, and steer th
2 CONC e car with her feet! There were also buggy rides. They will never forget her. She was a good person with high moral standards; and in spite of having to part with so many dear ones, she enjoyed and appreciated life. Aunt Daisy also loved hats and w
2 CONC as really thrilled when anyone admired her hats. My mom used to kid her about the two of them going to the city where Aunt Daisy could study voice and mom could take dancing lessons so they could be famous. Aunt Daisy probably could have made it be
2 CONC cause she had an unusually beautiful, clear singing voice. But my mom---maybe practice makes perfect---but she always liked a bit of humor. 2 &amp;frac12; years ago we lost our Aunt Annie. On January 11, 1978, Aunt Daisy&amp;rsquo;s life very quiet
2 CONC ly, gradually, and peacefully ebbed away. I don&amp;rsquo;t believe she would like a lot of tears shed over her, but would like to be remembered; and I am sure she left a lot of her heart here with us. And now from those of us who love her, I woul
2 CONC d like to bid our Aunt Daisy a fond Good-bye! -- and ask our Heavenly Father to help us to perpetuate this great family unity and love. Retyped by Lynn Ellis Wood , July 2013&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
