1 _OID ae546c11-dc92-42ec-a041-831e5f3a9331
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line /><line>&lt;tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;td style="font-size: 13px;" valign="top"&gt;Birth:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;td style="font-size: 13px;" align="left" valign="top"&gt;Mar. 21, 1820&
2 CONC lt;br&gt;Farington&lt;br&gt;Lancashire, England&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;td style="font-size: 13px;" valign="top"&gt;Death:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;td style="font-size: 13px;" align="lef
2 CONC t" valign="top"&gt;Apr. 16, 1879&lt;br&gt;South Cottonwood&lt;br&gt;Salt Lake County&lt;br&gt;Utah, USA&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;td style="font-size: 13px;" colspan="2" valign="top"&gt;&lt;br&gt;Memo
2 CONC rial marker in the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a style="color: #552255;" href="http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;amp;GSln=bullock&amp;amp;GSfn=lucy&amp;amp;GSbyrel=in&amp;amp;GSdyrel=in&amp;amp;GSst=47&amp;amp;GScntry=4&amp;amp;GSob=n&amp;amp;G
2 CONC Rid=35037487&amp;amp;"&gt;Salt Lake City Cemetery&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Daughter of Thomas Clayton and Ann Critchely&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Married Thomas Bullock, 23 Jan 1843, Nauvoo, Hancock, Illinois&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;em&gt;Our Pionee
2 CONC r Heritage, Vol. 8, p. 287&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Lucy Clayton Bullock, born in Farrington, Lancashire, England: March 26, 1820, was the sister of William Clayton who wrote Come, Come Ye Saints, that beautiful song which inspired the pioneer
2 CONC s to push forward into the unknown land with greater faith and courage. She joined the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints in England and emigrated to America with the Saints.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;As a young girl Lucy was acquainted with th
2 CONC e Prophet Joseph Smith and his brother, Hyrum. At one time the Prophet was being hunted by a mob. In order to escape he sought refuge at the Clayton home where he quickly dressed, at the request of Mrs. Clayton, in a woman's nightdress and cap an
2 CONC d went to bed in Lucy's bed with his face toward the wall. When the mobsters came with their clothes wrong side out and their faces all blackened, they rushed into the house, searching it from top to bottom. As they entered the bedroom, Lucys' moth
2 CONC er told them the woman in the bed was very sick. They immediately left the house, and the Prophet once again escaped uninjured.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Brigham Young advised Lucy, with whom he was well acquainted, to marry his clerk and recorder, Thoma
2 CONC s Bullock. Later, Thomas himself asked her to become his wife, which she immediately agreed to do. The Family left Nauvoo and remained at Winter Quarters that first winter of 1846&amp;ndash;47. Mr. Bullock's first wife was in ill health, and Lucy h
2 CONC elped care for her and her three children that winter and during the trip across the plains in 1848. She drove a team the entire distance to Great Salt Lake. Thomas Bullock was more of a scholar than a handy man, and most of the heavy work fell o
2 CONC n the women's shoulders.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;When the Saints moved onto farms in the Valley of the Great Salt Lake, Lucy was given land in South Cottonwood, later called Murray, where she raised her family and worked her farm. She often walked to Sa
2 CONC lt Lake to buy supplies and then carried them home, as walking was her only form of transportation.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Lucy practiced midwifery for years, and many a new-born babe she laid in the mother's loving arms. This good woman who so ably pl
2 CONC ayed the roll of pioneer wife and mother, always inspired love and admiration among her associates and family. She died April 16, 1879, in South Cottonwood. She had been ill with dropsy and other complaints for some time.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Thoma
