1 _OID b1dd8bfe-303a-498d-9e61-ec849a61ed85
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 10px; margin-bottom: 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px; color: #333333; font-family: arial, 'helvetica neue', helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12px;"&gt;The start of the old Lancashire family o
2 CONC f Standish came into being shortly after the Norman Conquest, when the Bussel family acquired the two adjacent villages of Stanedis and Longetre, (now known as Standish and Langtree) as gifts from a grateful William the Conqueror. Later, an elder d
2 CONC aughter of the family, Juliana, married Radulphus de Stanedis, who took the name "de Standish". The family held the unbroken Lordship of the Manor of Standish over the following seven centuries (1220-1920). Later the name was simplified to Standish
2 CONC . The country seat of the family is at Standish Hall, which was first built on its present site in 1574 by Edward Standish. The family of Standish held extensive lands in Lancashire, including coal mining rights over their lands in Adlington, nea
2 CONC r Macclesfield. In 1840 Sir Thomas Standish of Duxbury is reported to have sold a coal mine in Duxbury for &amp;pound;8,000. Henry Noailles Widdrington Standish, the last Lord of the Manor, died without any heir at Contreville in France and the hou
2 CONC se of Standish came to an end.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
