1 _OID be548a42-c79c-4a21-90e7-fe38e0b749f8
1 DATE 16th Century
1 PLAC Lancashire
1 _DSCR Documentation for the Chaddock Coat of Arms can be found in Burke's General Armory. In heraldic language, the Arms or Shield is as follows: 
2 CONT "Gules, an escutcheon argent, charged with a plain cross of the field, within an orle of martlets of the second." 
2 CONT Translated, the Arms description is: 
2 CONT "Red, a silver shield charged with a red cross, within an inner border of silver birds without feet." 
2 CONT Mottos are believed to have originated as battle cries in medieval times. No Motto was recorded with the Chaddock Coat of Arms. 
2 CONT The surname Chaddock appears to be patronymically originated, and is believed to be associated with the English and Irish, meaning "descendant of Cath(war)." 
