1 _OID bf73899d-4f7b-42c2-8d33-1031c4403f2e
1 DATE 1649
1 PLAC Manchester, Lancashire, Kingdom of England
1 _DSCR An extract from The Moons of Amounderness.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Robert Moon of Woodplumpton, his uncle, John&amp;nbsp;&lt;/s
2 CONC pan&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Moon of Whinneyfield and Alexander Moon of Ashton&amp;shy;-on-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Ribble were amongst the jurors who gave evidence to the Commonwealth Commission on th
2 CONC e ecclesiastical&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;status of the hamlets in the parishes of Preston and St. Michaels-on-Wyre. On 8 June, 1649 an Act of Parliament was passed entitled &amp;lsquo;An Act for the Provid&amp;
2 CONC shy; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;ing and Maintenance of Preaching Ministers and other &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Pious Uses.&amp;rdquo; In Dec., 1649, it was ordered that the Lords Commissioners fo
2 CONC r the Great Seal of England do present to the House a Commission for the discovery of the value of the several livings in the prespective counties.&amp;rdquo; On 1 February, 1649/50, the members were ordered within seven days to send up the names o
2 CONC f commissioners. The Lancashire commission is dated 29 Mar, 1650 and the first inquisition was held at Man&amp;shy; chester 17 June of that year. The high sheriff was required to cause to appear before them &amp;lsquo;honest lawful &lt;/span&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;men by whom the truth may best be known and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;inquired of&amp;rsquo;. An inquisition was held at Preston 21&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: t
2 CONC imes;"&gt;June, 1650 before Richard Shuttleworth, John Starkie, Thomas Whittingham, George Toulieson, John Sawrey, Jeremiah Aspinwall, and George Pigot, esquires. Sixteen jurors gave evidence before the commissioners regarding the parishes of Prest
2 CONC on and St. Michaels-on&amp;shy; Wyre, of which body the three Moons were members, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;other local representatives were:&amp;mdash;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;From W
2 CONC oodplumpton, Thomas and John Wood; from &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Broughton, Henry Beesley, Thomas Goodshaw, senior and junior, and Roger Harrison: from Sowerby, John Swan. The jurors said that Woodplumpton chapelry conta
2 CONC ined 220 families and was three miles long and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;two and a half miles broad: the people desired to be a separate parish (Lancashire Rec. Soc. Vol. 1 pp. 145&amp;shy; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: times;"&gt;148).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Robert, son of Henry Moon, was the Catfort
2 CONC h representative on this panel, his cousin John Moon, junior, of Whinneyfield, was the representative of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Plumpton quarter of Woodplumpton, Alexander Moon &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family
2 CONC : times;"&gt;of Ashton was probably another cousin.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
