2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;On the arrival of the body at the entrance of the church-yard it was met by the following clergymen and pastors in their robes:&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;
2 CONC div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The Rev. G. T. Kingdon and Rev. J. C. W. Tasker, who had lately been officiating at All Saints Church during Mr. Prior's illness, the Rev. J. Last, Chaplain at Geneva, the Rev. P. M. Sankey, Chap
2 CONC lain at Montreux, the Rev'ds J. Ellerton, J. C. Thompson, J. Gale, J. H. McDougall, Haddon Parks, Pastor Buscarlet, Minister of the Scotch Presbyterian Church, Lausanne, Ceresole, Huser, Dulong, father and son Vodoz, Wurston.&lt;/div&gt;</line><lin
2 CONC e>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The Chaplains of Lausanne and Clarens, were unavoidably prevented from attending.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;After receiving the body a
2 CONC t the entrance, the clergy and pastors entered the church in procession, repeating the opening sentences of the funeral service, and the first Psalm appointed for the occasion.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</li
2 CONC ne><line>&lt;div&gt;The 90th Psalm was then read by the Rev. J. Last, and the 288th hymn from 'Hymns Ancient and Modern,' 'A Few More Years Shall Roll,' was sung by the united choirs of Vevey and Montreux English Churches, several members of the la
2 CONC tter choir having kindly assisted on the occasion.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The beautiful lesson from the XV Chapter, I Cor. was read by the Rev
2 CONC . G. T. Kingdon, and the ceremony within the church concluded by the appropriate 401st hymn, 'Now the Laborer's Task is O'er.'"&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;
2 CONC div&gt;The clergy and pastors then headed the procession on its way to the cemetery of St. Martin's, where the opening sentences were read by the Rev. J. Gale, who had often assisted the late Chaplain in his services at All Saints'.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/d
