2 TITL Life Sketch of Emily Louisa Street
1 _OID ce30fe4f-7117-4540-b52e-e1c6d5f4fa8f
1 DATE 1862
1 PLAC New, York, City, to, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah, USA
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;History of Emily Louisa Street Van Tassell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: '
2 CONC verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;History taken from one written by a granddaughter, Annie L. Van Tassell Millard, submitted and edited by Jane E. Millard Mitchell, from collection of Daughters of Utah Pioneers &lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;Emily Louisa Street was born 24 October 1819, in Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada. She was the daughter of George and Ce
2 CONC cilia Christana Von Hagen Street, who were of French descent. Eventually the Street family moved to New York City where Emily grew up. She was a good and beautiful young lady, it is no wonder that, when she met Henry Van Tassell, he fell in love wi
2 CONC th her. They were marred about 1835 or 1836. Henry was 3 years her senior, having been born 26 May 1816 in New York City. His parents were John Van Tassell and Sarah Ann Connelly. It is said that the Van Tassell&amp;rsquo;s were of the aristocrati
2 CONC c Dutch. Emily and Henry continued to live in New York City where they became the parents of 7 children: 1) Sarah Maria b. 9 Sept. 1837 d. 11 Dec 1840; 2) George Henry b. 27 Nov. 1839; 3) William Moncton b. 4 Nov. 1841; 4) James Dailey b. 12 May 18
2 CONC 45; 5) Francis Lousia b. 3 June 1849; 6) Ann Maria b. 18 April 1854; 7) Henrietta b. 29 Sept. 1857. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;They go
2 CONC t along pretty well as most young couples, but Henry&amp;rsquo;s health was not very good. Consequently, as the family increased, he was not able to earn enough to keep the family as they should be kept. Emily was a very good cook, so decided to op
2 CONC en a boarding house to help out with the family finances. Emily&amp;rsquo;s motto was &amp;ldquo;An idle brain is the devil&amp;rsquo;s workshop.&amp;rdquo; So, everybody worked at the Van Tassell household. Her boarders were of the better class o
2 CONC f people and some very influential. One of them was a railroad man of the New York Central staff. Emily was anxious that her children be kept busy, so she asked this man if he would take her son George into his office as an errand boy and to do od
2 CONC d jobs about the place. He consented, and George worked vacation time, after school and on Saturdays. He kept advancing and learned to be a foreman, then an engineer on the railroad. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font
2 CONC -family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;On 28th April 1860, George married Sarah Frances Beam, and died in 16 March 1902. At this death he was the oldest employee in the New York Central railroad on the fastest tra
2 CONC in in the world, called the Buffalo Express. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;When William was old enough to go out to work, she got employmen
2 CONC t for him in a store. He progressed steadily in the store business. On 12 September 1861, he married Amelia Bapp. He was a successful merchant and made a large fortune. He died in New York 7 Dec. 1888. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&
2 CONC lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;The mother found work for James in a printing office. As the girls grew older, she kept them busy helping her in the house. Perhaps, due to Henry being s
2 CONC o spoiled as child and in poor health in the married years, he did not control his violent temper as he should have done. He was very jealous of his wife and would abuse her terribly at time. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span st
2 CONC yle="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;Emily was a deeply religious, Christian woman. She taught her children to believe in God and live by the Golden Rule. It was no wonder then, that when she heard th
2 CONC e true gospel of Jesus Christ preached by humble Mormon Elders, she was much impressed by their message, as were her children, James and Frances. However, this religion had the opposite effect on her husband and her sons, George and William. They w
2 CONC ere very bitter against the Mormons and made life almost unbearable for Emily, James and the girls. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;Even so
2 CONC , Emily could not squelch that spark of truth that burned in her soul and was willing to take the consequences. She was baptized 27 January 1857. James followed suit on 26 March 1857 and Frances on 20 June 1857. It was their desire to join the Sain
2 CONC ts in Utah. It was decided that James, age 16, should immigrate to Utah in 1860 and prepare a place for his mothers and sisters, so he did.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4
2 CONC c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;It was in 1862 when Emily, age 42, with Fanny, nearly 13, Anna Maria, age 8, and Henry, age 4, were ready to leave New York City to make a new home in the V
2 CONC alley of the Mountains. They were anxious to be reunited with James and live among the Mormons. When the husband and father, Henry, saw his family ready to leave, he decided he did not want to be left behind, so he went with them.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/
2 CONC p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;They took the train to St. Louis, Missouri, then a steamboat up the Missouri River to Florence, Nebraska, which was the outfitt
2 CONC ing station for the Saints to make the final preparations to cross the plains. Although there were some 5, 036 Saints in 13 organized companies made the long trek that year of 1862, Blanche Sundberg, a granddaughter, says her folks crossed the plai
2 CONC ns in an independent company, with just a few wagons in the group.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;After arriving in Salt Lake City, they live
2 CONC d in Sugar House. On 14 December 1867, James married Miss Jane Elizabeth Swift. He died 15 Dec. 1921. Frances Louisa married George Hill on 2nd Dec. 1865 and died 5 Dec. 1869. Anna Maria married Lathrop A. Barnabus Adams on 20 Feb. 1871 and die
2 CONC d 8 Feb. 1875. Henrietta married David Dowding 8 Dec. 1872, and died 15 Mary 1926. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;Being the energetic natur
2 CONC e that she was, and need to help support the family as she had always done, Emily found work doing different things. Henry, her husband, bought himself an old horse and cart and did a little peddling or what he could get to do, but with living an i
2 CONC dle life and with poor health he made very little money to help with the family needs. He died of a stroke on 4 Dec. 1868 in Salt Lake City at the age of 52. After the death of her husband, Emily Street Van Tassell, married John Spencer of Bluffdal
2 CONC e, South Jordan area, as his plural wife. She took her daughters Ann Maria and Henrietta with her, until they were married. Jane Mitchell says that the Spencer relatives say that after John Spencer died, his wife Emily was given $50.00 a year fro
2 CONC m the Spencer estate, for the rest of her life. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;There is no known date of when Emily married Samuel Naylor, o
2 CONC r anything about him or this marriage. Blanche says that her grandmother came to live at the home of her parent&amp;rsquo;s (Henrietta and David Dowding) in Sandy, when she got old and needed care. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;s
2 CONC pan style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;Blanche remembers her grandmother as being a very small woman. Her dark hair was parted in the middle with a bob in the back. She had brown eyes. All the child
2 CONC ren loved grandma, but Blanche, the youngest of a large family, seemed to be the one grandma showered the most love and affection on. She would hold Blanche on her lap and rock her and tell her stories, such as Little Red Riding Hood, over and over
2 CONC . &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;Grandma was very tenderhearted, and when Blanche was naughty, and had to be punished, this was very hard o
2 CONC n Grandma. She could not stand to see this little child mistreated and declared she was the only one that loved her. Grandma lacked 7 months of being 77 years old when she died on 25th of March 1805, of old age. She was buried in the Sandy Cemetery
2 CONC . Only 2 of her 7 children, James and Henrietta, survived her. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'verdana',sans-serif; color: #4f4f4c; letter-spacing: -.1pt;"&gt;She died as she had lived, faithful and true t
2 CONC o the gospel of Jesus Christ, that she had embraced so long ago, and sacrificed so much for.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
1 _CREA 2021-04-20 09:28:24.000
