2 TITL Death of Joe Smith (Newspaper Article)
1 _OID d3e58b22-cf99-488f-8bd0-8b4dedd43a83
1 DATE 6 August 1844
1 _DSCR Dundee Courier - Tuesday 06 August 1844
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dundee Courier - Tuesday 06 August 1844&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;DEATH OF JOE SMI
2 CONC TH THE MORMON IMPOSTOR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;(From the New York Herald)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001p
2 CONC t;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;We have just received by the western mail the following particulars of the death of Joe Smith the Mormon Prophet, and his brothe
2 CONC r Hiram. They were both shot. There was a tremendous excitement at the west in consequence of their deaths. A dreadful civil war was expected. We learn from an extra of the St Louis Gazette, that Warsaw was all excitement. The women and children we
2 CONC re all removed, and an immediate attack was expected from the Mormons.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;It seems that the circumstances attending the killing of the Mo
2 CONC rmon Prophet and his brother Hiram are as follows : - Governor Ford had left Carthage with about one hundred and twenty soldiers, for the purpose of taking possession of the Nauvoo Legion and their arms. At Nauvoo they called for the assembling o
2 CONC f the Legion. About 2000 men with arms immediately responded to its call. These troops were put under command of Colonel Singleton, of Brown county, who accompanied Governor Ford to Nauvoo. The Governor finding all quiet left Nauvoo with a compan
2 CONC y of sixty men, for the purpose of encamping about seven miles from the city. At about the same time that Governor Ford left Nauvoo, the Prophet and his brother were killed at Carthage. It appears that they were both confined in the debtors&amp;rsq
2 CONC uo; room of the Carthage jail, awaiting their trial on a charge of treason. The jail was strongly guarded by soldiers and anti-Mormons, who had been placed there by the Governor. A Mormon attempted to rush by the guard for the purpose of forcing hi
2 CONC s way into the jail. He was opposed by the guard, and fired a pistol at one of them, giving him a slight wound. A general confusion ensued in the crowd around the jail. Joe and his Mormon fellow prisoners it seems had provided themselves with pisto
2 CONC ls, and commenced firing upon the guard within. He then attempted to escape from the window, when a hundred balls entered his body, and he fell a lifeless corpse. The man was a most egregious imposter, but this mode of ridding the world of him is b
2 CONC arbarous in the extreme. It would appear that the large colony he has created had excited the envy and cupidity of his murderers, and these were the motives for the commission of the deed. It was supposed at first that his followers would revenge h
2 CONC is death, but they seem at once to have turned it to a business account and the profit of their creed. It will be remembered that this same Joe Smith visited England a year or two since, and succeeded in cajoling some 200 or 300 substantial person
2 CONC s into a belief of his divine mission. These followed him out to Nauvoo, indeed, English Mormons have been increasing ever since. It is of course useless to talk to such wild fanatics of the folly of their conduct. His brother Hiram shared the sam
2 CONC e fate, and Richards, a leading Mormon, was badly wounded.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .
2 CONC 0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Death of the M
2 CONC ormon Prophet&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="newspaperTitle" style="box-sizing: border-box;"&gt;Devizes and Wiltsh
2 CONC ire Gazette&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;-&amp;nbsp;&lt;span id="newspaperDate" style="box-sizing: border-box; color: #999999;"&gt;Thursday 01 August 1844&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"
2 CONC &gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The Mormon prophet, Joe Smith and his brother, Hiram Smith are both dead. They were murdered in the debtor&amp;rsquo;s prison at Carthage, on or about the 25&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; June. In my lett
2 CONC er of the 30&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; ult., I gave you a statement of the Mormon difficulties, so far as an account of them had been received in this city. From that statement the most direful consequences were to be apprehended. Those apprehension
2 CONC s have been realized.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The circumstances attending the death of these men are very differently represen
2 CONC ted by the parties for and against them. It is said by their enemies, that the Smiths, while within the prison, fired with pistols upon the guard, and then attempted to escape, whereupon the guard returned the fire, and that they were then both sla
2 CONC in. I have taken some trouble to ascertain the facts, and have come to the conclusion that the following statement is as near the truth as anything that has yet appeared in print, but I may be mistaken.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bot
2 CONC tom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;On a solemn pledge given by the Governor Ford, of Illinois, that their persons should be protected from injury by a mob, Joe and Hiram Smith with o
2 CONC thers, suffered themselves to be arrested, and were removed to the gaol at Carthage, there to await a trial for any crime they might be charged with having committed. Everything appearing to be peaceful, and only a small guard was left to protect t
2 CONC he prisoners from being injured or from escaping.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;On or about the 25&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; of Jun
2 CONC e a mob, consisting of 60 or 70 armed men, all disguised, rushed past the guard, in strength numbering only six or eight into the gaol, and immediately commenced firing through the door of the room in which the prisoners were confined, wounding th
2 CONC e Prophet ; whereupon he and his brother fled to a window, and were in the act of jumping out, when Joe received three balls in his body and fell dead upon the ground. Hiram fell inside of the prison, having received 10 or 12 balls through his body
2 CONC . These were the only Mormons killed. One or two others were wounded. The dead bodies were removed to Nauvoo (&amp;ldquo;the holy city&amp;rdquo;) to be interred with great solemnity. The followers of Smith were greatly exasperated, but kept in sub
2 CONC jection by their leaders. Smith&amp;rsquo;s mother is said to possess a controlling influence, which was exerted in the preservation of order, and the suppression of violence. At the last accounts everything was tranquil.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;
2 CONC p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Smith was a most egregious impostor, but his mode of ridding the world of him is barbarous in the extreme. It would appear tha
2 CONC t the large colony he has created had excited the envy and cupidity of his murderers, and these were the motives for the commission of the deed. It was supposed at first that his followers would revenge his death, but they seem at once to have turn
2 CONC ed it to a business account and the profit of their creed. It will be remembered that this same Joe Smith visited England a year or two since, and succeeded in cajoling some 200 or 300 substantial persons into a belief of his divine mission. Thes
2 CONC e followed him out to Nauvoo. Indeed, English Mormons have been increasing ever since. It is of course useless to talk to such wild fanatics of the folly of their conduct.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-bottom: .0001pt;"&gt;&lt;span styl
2 CONC e="font-size: large;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
