1 _DSCR History for Ander Dudley Sr.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;font size="1"&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Andrew Dudley Sr. was born June 6, 1844 in Adams Co. Illinois, the son of Joseph Dudley and Sarah Dereck Stevens Dudley. When Andrew was eight years of age, he crossed t
2 CONC he plains in company with his parents and arrived in Salt Lake, November 3, 1852. While he was growing up, he lived with his parents first in Pleasant Grove, Utah, then in Rose Canyon west of utah lake. Two years later they moved to Fort Herriman
2 CONC , then to Alpine, utah. In 1855 they went to provo and resided there until 1863 when they moved to Spanish fork. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;In April 1863 , when Andrew was 19 years of age, he returned to the Missouri Rive
2 CONC r to assist a company of mormons in coming to Salt Lake. The group he met was under the leadership of Captain Roswell Hyde. He returned with this company driving four yoke of oxens. They arrived in Salt lake October 13 1863, having been two month
2 CONC s enroute from the Missouri river.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;In 1865 he married Mary Ellen Packette and had three children by her: Mary Ellen, Melissa Emeline and Lucy Emily, the later two died in infancy.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;A good portion of his time between 1865 to 1869 was not only spent in farming a piece of land in Spanish fork canyon, but he, along with others was set by President Young to track horses that had been stolen fro
2 CONC m the settlers by the Indians.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;In 1867, in company with Franklin Pace and two Corn Creek Indians (One by the name of Wandrodes) he came out to the Uintah Reservation as far as Lakes forks in searc
2 CONC h of the stolen horses. The Indians went as far as White River where they found the horses. (Wandrodes was the leader and friendly with the white man.)&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;This is the way Andrew first became acquaint
2 CONC ed with the Uintah Basin. In the Fall of 1885, he came out again to look over the country. He liked it, so the following Spring, 1886, He came out and this time filed for land and put crops on land not far from the Green River, in the locality no
2 CONC w known as Jensen. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Prior to this, however, he had been called to server in the Black Hawk War from April to July 23 1867 in the O.P. Miles Company.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4
2 CONC "&gt;Then on Oct 11, 1869, his wife Mary Ellen died having given birth to Lucy Emily five days before. The child lived until Oct. 16, then died, thus leaving only his oldest child Mary Ellen with him. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="
2 CONC 4"&gt;However, prior to the death of his wife, Mary Ellen, on July 19, 1869, he had married into polygamy. He married Mary Lucy Markham and from this union came the following children. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Andrew, Wi
2 CONC lliams (who died in infancy), Sarah Ann, Helen Aurelia, Stephen Markham, Joseph Stratton, Mary Atta, Guyletta Jane, Hosmer Marion, Sanford Hyrum, Arvilla Julina, Bertha, Bernice and lewis Manvil (the latter two died in infancy) and Parna Trioley. T
2 CONC he latter three children were born in Jensen, the other children in Spanish Fork Utah.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;When Andrew moved to Jensen, he brought with him his wife and children. His daughter Mary Mary Ellen by his f
2 CONC irst wife, had married a man by the name of Hollinshead, and had two daughters by him, Hettie and Emma. Then Mary Ellen later Married Albert Wm. Wilkins and they came to Jensen in 1869. Andrew, Mary Ellens&amp;#39;s half brother, helped move them
2 CONC . Mary Ellen and Alber Wm. Wilkins were the parents of eleven children and some of their grandchildren and great grandchildren are still living in Jensen. One daughter Eleanor married Douglas Chew and are still living on the Chew Ranch, east of th
2 CONC e Green River.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Of Andrew&amp;#39;s 15 children by his second wife Mary Lucy Markham, Dudley only two sons made their permanent homes in Jensen, Andrew Dudley Jr. and Joseph Stratton Dudley. Four d
2 CONC aughters married and lived in Uintah County until their deaths: Mary Atta, Guylette Jane, Helen Aurelia, Sarah Ann (Aunt Sadie).&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Mary Atta married Henry James Chatwin in 1900 and was well known i
2 CONC n the Jensen area by the &amp;quot;Hotel Atta&amp;quot; which she and Henry built soon after their marriage. &amp;quot;Aunt Atta&amp;quot; played hostess to many weary traveler at her hotel where she lived and which she operated until her death i
2 CONC n 1941. In 1945 it was sold to Curt Dudley and he made it into apartments. In 1947 it was sold to Mr. and Mrs. Richard Hoffman and later sold to Lester White. His daughter and husband are living in it at the present time. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Daughter Guyletta married Fritz Gerald Stewart and their posterity stayed in Jensen for years and have had an impact on the community. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Helen Aurelia married Lucius Hunt
2 CONC ington Woodward and they lived in Vernal and owned and operated the L.H. Woodward Furniture Store until Woodward&amp;#39;s death. They had several children and they married and lived in Vernal. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;D
2 CONC aughter Sarah Ann (Aunt Sadie) married Ephraim Rasmussen and they lived and raised their family in LaPoint and Vernal. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Son, Joseph S. Dudley married Jemima Moon, and they built their home on par
2 CONC t of the land Joseph&amp;#39;s father had homesteaded. Their posterity have played an important part in developing of the Jensen and Vernal area.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;When Joseph was just a young man in 1893, he carri
2 CONC ed mail from Rangely to Vernal on horseback. He inherited and farmed part of the 160 acres of land his father had filed on. Joseph was well known for the work he did with the Burns Bench Canal. For a number of years he had a small coal mine up Brus
2 CONC h Creek and supplied people of Jensen and Vernal with coal. He was the proud owner of the first threshing machine in Jensen and operated it for a number of years. He was owner of the I.D. Ranch south of the Jensen bridge on the east side of Green R
2 CONC iver. His son Orville farmed this ranch for years and then at his death his oldest son Mervin farmed and operated it until he sold out and moved away. Joseph died April 26, 1964. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Orville and hi
2 CONC s wife Iola Curfew Dudley were parents of Mervin and Devere, and three daughter, Connie, Nila, and Ilene. Devere and his wife and family lived in Jensen, Nila in Vernal, and Ilene lived with her husband Donn Fedellec in the home Orville and Tola bu
2 CONC ilt just east across the drive from Joseph and Jemima old home. Orville died May 7, 1967 and Iola re-married and moved away from Jensen. Don and Ilene are at present farming the land belonging to Orville. Ilene has and operates the Lady Bug Beaut
2 CONC y Parlor on the ground where the old Joseph Dudley home once stood.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Joseph&amp;#39;s older son Curtis was well-known in Jensen and in Uintah County. He was interested in various businesses in Jens
2 CONC en, as well as farming some of land of the old Dudley homestead. He was owner for a short time of the Atta Hotel. He operated &amp;quot;Curt&amp;#39;s Cash Store&amp;quot; across the highway 40, across from Aumiller&amp;#39;s Store, just west of th
2 CONC e Green River Bridge. Later he moved one -half mile west to the junction of Highway 40 Highway 149. There he owned and operated Curt&amp;#39;s Conoco Service Station, store and eating place. This service station is still operating, at present owne
2 CONC d and operated by Wm. &amp;quot;Billy&amp;quot; Ainge. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Curtis served as Unitah County Commissioner for a number of years and during his administration, the roads of the country were much improve
2 CONC d with asphalt covering, which made the country roads passable in wet and muddy weather conditions. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Also during his administration he took an active part in getting a TV transmitter installed o
2 CONC n Blue Mountain which made it possible for the people of the country to have TV in their homes. He served as Director of the Uintah County Water Conservancy District and was President of the Jensen Culinary Water System until ill health forced hi
2 CONC m to quit and his vice president A. Demarr Dudley took over the Presidency and the responsibility of getting the line laid and functioning. Curtis served as a Director of the Moon Lake Electric Association and was involved with many more activitie
2 CONC s in the Jensen and Vernal area until his death, May 17, 1973. His wife Jessie as well of some of his children and grandchildren are living in Vernal.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Joseph&amp;#39;s other son, Marion dies if dr
2 CONC owning in the Green River as he and his father were swimming. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Of Joseph&amp;#39;s four daughters: Kathryn Ross, Mary Robertson, Elta Alder, and Veda Hatch, only Veda lives in the county. She marr
2 CONC ied Earl Hatch and they have lived in Jensen, and are now living in Vernal. There children are prominent in the Vernal area. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Andrew Dudley left a large posterity and their influence has been fel
2 CONC t in Uintah County, but before going on, it would be well to point out that Andrew himself was a man of exceptional ability. He had great powers of leadership and was an unusually good social mixer. People liked him. He was strictly honest and hi
2 CONC s word was as good as his bond. His knowledge of farming and of the country in general, his nature good common sense made his counsel of value and people came to him for it. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;He undermined his nat
2 CONC ure ability and power by the use of alcohol. He drinking not only hurt him, but brought a great deal of suffering to his wife and family. He was not much to make over his children and hence his children missed the comradeship and intimacies of th
2 CONC e family circle. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;He was a tall, stalwart, muscular fellow and had unusual endurance. He wasn&amp;#39;t very religious and took very little part in church activities. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&
2 CONC gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;It was a custom for the entire family to meet together on his birthday in a sort of family reunion. This continued up until the time of his death. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;Andrew Dudley died of he
2 CONC art failure at the home of his daughter Atta Chatwin in Jensen, Utah on March 16, 1925. He lives yet in the memory of the older people here in this locality for since the writer came to this part of the state, she had heard people speak of him, hi
2 CONC s appearances in public celebrations in which he took a great delight for he was a great cut-up.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;His contribution to the building up of this country has more or less been swallowed up in the accom
2 CONC plishment of his posterity. But each early pioneer who came sacrificed, fought, and learned, and left a rich heritage to his children.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font size="4"&gt;-Dudley Family History&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</l
2 CONC ine></content></metadataxml>
