1 _OID d9adfe21-9ede-40f3-8b6c-8d7ef802d5eb
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;The marriage found for Edmund Hopwood &amp;amp; Dorothy Ashton was in February 1611, but both were very young. Dorothy the "young wife" was picked for Edmund by his grandfa
2 CONC ther, Edmund.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;The first child to this marriage appears to have been Dorothy in 1616. As they were only about 14 or 15 at the time of this arranged marriage it may b
2 CONC e that they still lived separately for sometime after the marriage. Dorothy, being the name of both Edmund's &amp;amp; Dorothy's mothers was the obvious choice for the first daughter.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font
2 CONC -size: medium;"&gt;(The James Hopwood born in 1614 is more likely the child of Edmund Hopwood born 1591, who is likely to have married Anne Wrigley in 1613, with that Edmund's father also being James. Richard, baptized 15 days after James in 1614
2 CONC , and Katherine, baptized in October 1611, are likely to be to an earlier Edmund Hopwood. Edmund's uncle, Edmund, probably married in 1585, and these could have been the last of his children.)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span st
2 CONC yle="font-size: medium;"&gt;Edmund and Dorothy's second child appears to be Alice, the name of Edmund's grandmother. Priscilla, the uncommonly named third child, was the same name as Edmund's aunt.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;sp
2 CONC an style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Their first son, and heir, John, born 1622, would have been named after Edmund's father.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Detailed transcript of the ma
2 CONC rriage&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Marriage: 2 Feb 1612 St Leonard, Middleton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Edmund Hopwoode, gent. - &lt;/span&gt;&
2 CONC lt;br&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Dorothie Assheton - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Notes: maried vppon Sundaye the seconde daye of Februarie in thaffore noone in the tym
2 CONC e of Divine service by mee John Walkden Clerke, Curate of Middleton wth the Consente of bothe their parents accordinge to Lawe and theffecte of A licence granted from Chester dated the first daye of the above named monethe of Feb. Ano 1612.&lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;The extended description of the marriage, and that the curate, not the rector, carryings out the service, suggest a level of importance and a connection to the rector bein
2 CONC g the father of the bride (i.e. to be able to give her away.) Many references confirm that the Edmund and Dorothy shown are the correct people. (Note: "1" and "2" written on the original look very similar, causing the date to be written as 1611/12
2 CONC . Looking back at the previous page confirms it was 1612.)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;From: Remains, historical &amp;amp; literary, connecte
2 CONC d with the palatine counties of Lancaster and Chester&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;"The young wife, chosen for him by his grandfather, was the daughter of Mr. Edwar
2 CONC d Assheton, B. D., the parson of Middleton.. by his wife he had issue 5 sons &amp;amp; 5 daughters. Her fortune was 6oo/." - The number of children likely to have been born to Edmund and Dorothy does not match with this description. (9 sons and 7 d
2 CONC aughters.) However, some may have died young, and not been included in the description above.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;It also describes Dorothy's father's will, an the reason she was not in
2 CONC cluded in it:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;"The rector's will is dated 8 September, 15 Jac. He being "ill at&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;ease in his body and refusing all othe
2 CONC r means of salvation besides Jesus&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;Christ and him crucified," desired his body might be buried in the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;chapel belonging to him i
2 CONC n the church of Middleton or else where it&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;should please God to appoint He divided his personalty into three&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;parts, of which h
2 CONC e gave one to his wife Dorothy, another to his younger&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;children, Isaac, Mary, Jane and Katherine, and any child hereafter to&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;b
2 CONC e born, and the third he reserved for his own disposal. He gave no&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;filial portion to his daughter Dorothy, wife of Edmund Hopwood of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;
2 CONC "&gt;Hopwood esq., because he had paid her husband a great sum of money&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;by agreement on his marriage."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: m
2 CONC edium;"&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;The grandfather, "Edmund Hopwood here named buried his wife at Middleton in the year 1596, and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;also his eldest son, John Hopwood, gen
2 CONC t., in the year 1600. The latter left a son&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;Edmund, baptized at Middleton 23 May 1597, and thus by a remarkable coincidence&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&g
2 CONC t;he was left to succeed his grandfather, as had been the case with the heir in the pre&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;ceding generation. He was betrothed at the same place the very day after his grand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span sty
2 CONC le="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;father's death, when of the immature age of 14 years, the form being doubtless a con&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;tract only made in facie ecclesi&amp;lt;z, and in conformity with a provision mad
2 CONC e by the old man&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;in a deed of settlement dated 18 January 1611, whereby he conveyed his manors and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;lands of Hopwood, Thorn
2 CONC ham, Siddal, and Middleton, to his friends and kinsmen&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px;"&gt;Theophilus Assheton of Clegg hall, esq ., LL. D. , councillor-at-law..."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="line-height: 16.9px; font-size: medium;"&gt;This last point, naming Theophilus Assheton as his kinsman matches Theophilus' mother Jane as daughter of the grandfather Edmumd Hopwood...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&lt;span style="line-height: 1.3;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
