2 TITL The Will of Anthony Moon
1 _OID da31a698-c576-4f24-ab6b-50eaa251887e
1 DATE 1702
1 PLAC Catforth, Woodplumpton, Lancashire, Kingdom of England
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;His will is dated 21 November, 1702.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;He is described as of Catforth i
2 CONC n Woodplumpton, shoemaker, &amp;ldquo;being indisposed in body but in good and perfect memory blessed be God for the same, and call&amp;shy; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;ing to mind that death is certaine but the manner, tim
2 CONC e and place uncertain doe make and ordain this my &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;last will and testament in manner and form following. And first and most principally I give and commend my &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-fami
2 CONC ly: times;"&gt;soule unto almighty God my maker trusting through &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;the merits of Jesus Christ his son and my redeemer to have full pardon and forgiveness of all my sins, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span s
2 CONC tyle="font-family: times;"&gt;my body to the earth to be buried in Christian manner &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;at the discretion of my friends and executors.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p
2 CONC &gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&g
2 CONC t;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;The will states: &amp;ldquo;Whereas I have the reversion and claim in my ancient estate at Swillbrooke in Bartle &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;quarter, amounting to the value or sum o
2 CONC f thirty pounds &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;over and above such debts as my son Henry hath un&amp;shy; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;dertaken the payment thereof&amp;rdquo;. He gives the &amp;pound;3
2 CONC 0 to his children begotten, or lawfully to be begotten, to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;be paid at the end of three years after his decease, equally. To his wife Alice all which was her own when she married him and all his p
2 CONC ersonal estate, goods and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;credits over and besides the &amp;pound;30, she to pay such other debts as his eldest son Henry was not under obligation &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-famil
2 CONC y: times;"&gt;to settle by former agreement. He appointed his wife &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;Alice, and son in law William Morriss of Ballam, shoe&amp;shy; maker, exors. The will is signed &amp;ldquo;Anthony Moone&amp;rdq
2 CONC uo;. The inventory was made 30 December, 1702 by ]ohn Craddell, John Willas, George Bayne and William Smith. The total was &amp;pound;19 155.0d.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><
2 CONC line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;</lin
2 CONC e><line>&lt;div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: large;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;The livestock: cow and a mare &amp;pound;3 10s. 0d.; one swine 4s. 0d.; the widdow&amp;rsquo;s cowe and 2 stirks &amp;pound;4; on
2 CONC e cart and pair of wheels &amp;pound;1; carts and wheels and cheese press 5s. 0d.; hay in the barn 4s. 0d. In the dwelling: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;a pair of bedstocks and furniture &amp;pound;2; one other pair of &lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: times;"&gt;bedstocks and furniture 10s. 0d.; one chest and two pairs of bedstocks 5s. 0d.; seven pairs of sheets 14s. 0d.; one other bed and furniture &amp;pound;1 10s. 0d.; four little chests, a counter and a c
2 CONC heese press &amp;pound;1; four little tables 45. 0d.; chairs and stools 2s. 6d.; a clothes press &amp;pound;1; one table and forms 55. 0d.; a tressel 1s. 6d.; buttery boards 6s. 6d.; chest 6d.; A spitt and a pair of racks 3s. 0d.; one gallon, one d
2 CONC ishboard and a chest 1s. Od.; one brass pan 3s. 0d.; brass pans and pot 1s. 6d.; another brass pot 105. 0d.; a brass mortar and pewter 25. 6d. one fire iron and 2 shelves 5s. 0d.; one fire iron, tongs and the rest within the chimney 65. 0d.; turve
2 CONC s 2s. 0d.; meal and malt 6s. 8d,; wooden vessels 10s. 0d.; apparell, saddle and bridle &amp;pound;1; shoemaker&amp;rsquo;s goods; wimbles and gouges, 1s. 0d.; deceased&amp;rsquo;s tools and leather, 5s. 0d.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p
2 CONC &gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
