2 TITL Henrietta Lucretia Patten Clayton (1841-1841): A Short Life
1 _OID e1058cd5-b628-4298-8cca-afced4f8f26f
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="text-decoration: underline; color: #0000ff;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Henrietta Lucretia Patten Clayton (1841-1841): A Short Life&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/stro
2 CONC ng&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;span style="color: #0000ff;"&gt;William and Ruth Moon Clayton arrived in Nauvoo, Illinois on November 24th, 1840, having immig
2 CONC rated from Penwortham, Lancashire County, England aboard the ship &lt;em&gt;North America&lt;/em&gt; (59, 62). William and Ruth were asked by the Prophet Joseph to permanentely settle in Zarahemla, Lee County, Iowa, just across the Mississippi Rive
2 CONC r from Nauvoo, Illinois, which they did starting 21st January, 1841 (64). William Clayton then became a "bookkeeper-turned-farmer" (but was never successful at farming) (65). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=
2 CONC "font-size: medium; color: #0000ff;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; It was in Zarahemla on the 6th of May, 1841, that Henrietta Lucretia Patten Clayton was born. The summer of 1841 saw a major outbreak of The Ague (Malaria) in the town
2 CONC s on or near the banks of the Mississippi, including Zarahemla and Nauvoo (67). Several people died, including Henrietta. Three-month-old Henrietta Lucretia Patten Clayton died on the 17th of August, 1841 (67). Her father, William Clayton, said o
2 CONC f her, "'When dead she was as pretty as I ever saw in my life.'" Later, he would write in his journal he would say, "'This [Henrietta's death] was a grief to us...but we afterwards saw the hand of God in it and saw it was best to be so during thi
2 CONC s time'" (67). &lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium; color: #0000ff;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; William also caught "The Ague" and was so sick that he couldn't finish harvestin
2 CONC g his crop of potatoes and corn, so Ruth and her sister Lydia finished the job (67). After going to Nauvoo to ask advice of Heber C. Kimball, who had baptized William and Ruth in England, William moved his family back to Nauvoo on 14th of December
2 CONC , 1841.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;I've written this synopsis to clear up a few clerica
2 CONC l errors about Henrietta. The book reference at the bottom of this page will help you to know which pages (included above in parantheses) to look up Henrietta's biographical information.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Henrietta was NOT born in England. It is well documented in church history that her parents and older siblings emigrated to the United States in the Fall of 1840. She was born and died during a difficult time for th
2 CONC e Clayton Clan. The author of the book, James B. Allen, a historian working for BYU, clearly states that Henrietta died on the 17th of August, 1841. Having used an online day-of-the-week calculator, the 17th was a Tuesday. I understand that the maj
2 CONC ority of church documents states that she died on Friday, the 20th. I don't know why. My &lt;span style="text-decoration: underline;"&gt;uneducated guess&lt;/span&gt; might be because Friday is when it was reported in the local newspaper.&lt;br&gt;
2 CONC &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;So far, &lt;span style="text-decoration: underline;"&gt;I haven't found a reference of where or when Henrietta was burried&lt;/span&gt;. I'm not sure if Zarahemla h
2 CONC ad a cemetery, the small town only being about three years old when the Zarahemla stake of Zion was disbandonded by church leaders in the spring of 1842. &lt;span style="text-decoration: underline;"&gt;My assumption&lt;/span&gt; is that Henrietta w
2 CONC as burried in the Old Nauvoo Cemetery on the day she died, or the morning after, because August is a very hot and humid month in that area of the Mississippi.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"
2 CONC &gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Thanks,&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: medium;"&gt;Tarnett84321&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</li
