2 CONC " style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px 0px 8px; border: none;" align="left"&gt;Andrew W. Pulley, a farmer living near American Fork, was born in Alton, Madison county, Illinois, July 10, 1856, a son of William and Sarah (Morris) Pulley. The father wa
2 CONC s born in England and came to America in 1848, after which he engaged in farming and wood chopping in Illinois. He was a lumber sawyer in England and after making his home for some years in Illinols he went to Florence, Nebraska, in 1857, intendin
2 CONC g to continue the trip to Utah; but his brother Joseph who came to the new world in 1844, was then living at Nebraska City, Nebraska, upon a farm, and William Pulley decided to remain in that district. There he continued until 1878, when he crosse
2 CONC d the country to Salt Lake City and thence made his way to American Fork. He was even then in ill health and was unable to work after reaching American Fork, where he passed away in 1886. He was a devoted member of the Church of Jesus Christ of Lat
2 CONC ter-day Saints and did everything in his power to promote its growth and extend its influence. He had joined the church prior to his emigration to the new world and was presiding eider of a branch conference in England. In the family of Mr. and Mrs
2 CONC . William Pulley were two sons and five daughters: Emma J., Andrew W., Alice E., Susanna, Mary Ann, George H. and Evelyn.&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;/table&gt;</line><line>&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&
2 CONC lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr id="sRes-1" class="tblrowalt record" style="border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-style: solid; border-bottom-color: #cdc7be;" valign="top"&gt;</line><line>&lt;td style="margin: 0px; padding-right: 8px; padding-left
2 CONC : 8px; vertical-align: middle; line-height: 1.1em; padding-top: 8px !important; padding-bottom: 8px !important; background-color: #f5f5f5;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;table style="border-collapse: collapse; border-spacing: 0px; font-family: inherit; font-
2 CONC size: inherit; font-style: inherit; font-variant: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; border-bottom-style: none;" width="100%"&gt;</line><line>&lt;tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr valign="top"&gt;</line><line>&lt;td class="body" style=
2 CONC "margin: 0px; padding: 0px 0px 8px; border: none;" align="left"&gt;Andrew W. Pulley had but limited educational advantages, for his labors were early needed upon the farm and this precluded the possibility of his attending school. After leaving hom
2 CONC e his first work in Utah was at cutting wood and later he went to Idaho, where he was connected with a railroad track gang as section foreman during the latter part of a two and a half years' period of employment with the railroad company. At the e
2 CONC nd of that time he came to American Fork, where he now lives, having his home two miles northeast of the town. He bought eighty-four acres of wild land covered with sagebrush and since then has transformed the undeveloped tract into a beautiful gar
2 CONC den spot. The place has indeed been made to bloom and blossom as the rose as the result of his close application, unremitting industry and persistency. He has a fine large brick residence, also extensive barns, a large silo and full farm equipment
2 CONC , and in addition to cultivating the fields he successfully raises cattle and sheep. He is likewise interested financially in the American Fork Cooperative Institution and in the Manilia Threshing Machine Company. Farming, however, is his principa
2 CONC l occupation and one can scarcely realize, as one looks abroad over his carefully cultivated fields and fine garden, that this was once a district of sagebrush. He raises fruit of all kinds upon his place and his work has been carried forward so en
2 CONC ergetically and successfully that his farm is one of the most attractive features in the landscape.&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;/table&gt;</line><line>&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line
2 CONC >&lt;tr id="sRes-2" class="tblrow record" style="border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-style: solid; border-bottom-color: #cdc7be;" valign="top"&gt;</line><line>&lt;td style="margin: 0px; padding-right: 8px; padding-left: 8px; vertical-align: mid
2 CONC dle; line-height: 1.1em; padding-top: 8px !important; padding-bottom: 8px !important;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;table style="border-collapse: collapse; border-spacing: 0px; font-family: inherit; font-size: inherit; font-style: inherit; font-variant: inh
2 CONC erit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; border-bottom-style: none;" width="100%"&gt;</line><line>&lt;tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr valign="top"&gt;</line><line>&lt;td class="body" style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px 0px 8px; border: none;" al
2 CONC ign="left"&gt;In 1889 Mr. Pulley was married to Miss Emma L. Smith, a daughter of Oliver Smith, a pioneer farmer and stockman of this section of the state. They have become parents of ten children: Florence Lorene, who is the wife of Bennett Peters
2 CONC on, of Bountiful; Charles, who died at the age of two years and one month; Ruth; Priscilla; Adolphus; John W.; Mary Jane; Laura; Francis G.; and Esther. Adolphus joined the American army as a member of an infantry regiment of the Ninety-first Divis
2 CONC ion and has recently returned from France, where he saw very active service on some of the hardest sectors of the war. He suffered greatly with his feet, having been many days in the trenches when he could not take off his shoes. He was on duty i
2 CONC n France and Belgium.&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;/table&gt;</line><line>&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr id="sRes-3" class="tblrowalt record" style="border-bottom-width: 1px
