2 CONC n style="-webkit-text-size-adjust: none; background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0);"&gt;&lt;br&gt;View:&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="http://images.archives.utah.gov/data/81448/2260366/2260366_0000253.jpg" target="_blank"&gt;Utah Death Certificate&lt;/a&gt;&
2 CONC lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Tribune Intermountain Service&lt;br&gt;PROVO -- Walter H. Robinson, 61, former Provo business man and well known in music circles of Utah, died Wednesday morning at the Sevier County hospital at Richfield, following a stroke suff
2 CONC ered last Friday. Mr. Robinson was stricken while on a business trip to the southern Utah city.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Mr. Robinson was born July 8, 1878, in Swinton, England, a son of Hugh and Margaret Stringfellow Robinson. He came to America when 1
2 CONC 3 years of age in company with his family, settling in Springville.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;As a young man he worked on the farm and later, in company with three brothers, Herbert, Eli and Fred C. Robinson, operated Robinson Brothers Music and Furnitur
2 CONC e store at Springville. He married Nellie Ross on December 9, 1903, in Manti Temple.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;In 1914, he and his brothers came to Provo and opened the Robinson Brothers Music Company in connection with the Barton Furniture company on Nor
2 CONC th University Avenue. Several years later, the Springville concern dissolved and Mr. Robinson purchased the interests of his brothers in the Provo concern and changed the name to the Walter Robinson Music Company. It closed in 1931 and Mr. Robinso
2 CONC n then became traveling representative of wholesale musical firms.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Surviving besides his widow are a son and three daughters, Rondo H. Robinson of Magrath, Alberta, Canada; Mrs. Florence R. Beck and Mrs. Phyllis R. Brown of Provo
2 CONC ; and Mrs. Iris R. Creer of Salt Lake City; and nine grandchildren. Four brothers and two sisters also survive as follows: Mark Robinson of Ogden; Herbert Robinson of Helper; Eli, Fred C. Robinson, and Mrs. William Matson of Salt Lake City; and Mrs
2 CONC . Thomas Livesy of Los Angeles, Cal.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Funeral services will be conducted Friday at 1:30 p.m. in Provo LDS First Ward Chapel.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;- Salt Lake Tribune | 12 Oct 1939, p. 6 | transcribed by Annie Duckett Hundley.&lt;br&
