2 TITL Janet Thane
1 _OID e545a5fa-649d-4ae2-afc1-9001d5be6391
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Her birth date is variously recorded as October 16, 1822, 1823 and 1832; her name is reported as Genet, Janet, Jannet (1881 Canadian Household census fo
2 CONC r Windham, Norfolk North, Ontario), Jennet, Jennett, and Janette. The spelling of her last name, too, is varied, sometimes Thain but usually Thane in family recollection from her Ontario descendants; she is rarely or never referred to as &amp;quot;
2 CONC Thayn&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;Thayne&amp;quot; in family or other official documents from Ontario. Her date of death has often been given as 1833, but in fact she lived into her late 80&amp;#39;s. Even the names and numbers of her siblings vary cons
2 CONC iderably from account to account, though there is no doubt whatsoever that she was the child of Ebenezer Thane and Janet Gevitt Lochhead Thane. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;But even the accounts of her early life and the early death of her father, Ebenezer, vary. One version, from Roxie, a daughter of her son, James Kitchen, reports, &amp;quot;Her fam
2 CONC ily came from Scotland. Her father was buried at sea while crossing. She had one sister and two brothers, one brother was a Mormon and lived in Salt Lake City.&amp;quot; The 1881 Canadian Household census for Windham, Norfolk North, Ontario gives h
2 CONC er ethnic origin as Scottish, but her birthplace as Ontario. However, the death certificate of Roxie&amp;#39;s father, James, filed with the City of Minneapolis on December 31, 1936, gives her name as &amp;quot;Janet Thane&amp;quot; and her birthpl
2 CONC ace as Scotland.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;My late grandfather, Charles Spurgeon Buck (1892-1976), a hi
2 CONC story teacher, who knew her well, gave this version of the arrival in Canada and her father&amp;#39;s death in an oral memoir, dictated late in his own life: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Janet Kitchen (nee Janet Thane) [emigrated with h]er father and her mother and their large family...to Canada about the year 1830. The family had been living in a vil
2 CONC lage near Glasgow. Hard times of the thirties in Scotland forced them to seek a new home in Canada. Grandma (really Great-Grandma) has told me that she remembers her and her family&amp;#39;s passage on a sailing ship to Quebec. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;From Quebec, Janet&amp;#39;s father travelled westwards, seeking land, or work, or some means t
2 CONC o support his large family. They had to remain in the Lower Town of the fort of Quebec, waiting for the father to return. By difficult stages, that father had got as far as the flat and plague-stricken lands (as they were then) bordering the uppe
2 CONC r end of the Detroit River. There he contracted a case of the ague, quite common in that region and in the early years of the 19th century: the ague struck down visitors in the marshy lands near Lake St. Clair. Janet&amp;#39;s father died of the di
2 CONC sease. In time, the mother anxiously waiting with her family of boys and one daughter, Janet, was told of his death. What was [Janet, Sr.] to do for her family of children? I do not know how she managed to find foster homes for them, but she did so
2 CONC . Her daughter, Janet, then nine or ten years of age, was brought into the family of William Kitchen [Janet] was sent to one of the pioneer schools at a village one or two miles east of the Kitchen&amp;#39;s farm, the Summit, so called because it w
2 CONC as located on a hill in that region of flat lands. At the home of William Kitchen she was a maid of all work along with his two daughters...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;My cousin, Marilyn Brown, now deceased, who had done very extensive research on the family, wrote me a letter commenting on and correcting my grandfather&amp;#39;s memoirs. In it she a
2 CONC dded information regarding Janet Thane she had garnered from Gordon Buck, her father, and Jessie Buck, her aunt. Both Gordon and Jessie were my grandfather&amp;#39;s younger siblings. Marilyn commented:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'tim
2 CONC es new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Jessie, Gordon and your grandpa all remembered Janet Thane, Grandma Kitchen, with affection. She must have had a strong an
2 CONC d pleasant personality because she seems to have stolen the show. However, Aunt Jessie and my father said Janet Thane had one brother and possibly, Aunt Jessie thought, a sister ...The father travelling west to the flat swamplands of Essex or Ken
2 CONC t Counties [Ontario], being stricken by ague and dying, yes, Gordon and Jessie told much the same, although with a bit of a twist. Aunt Jessie was not sure if the mother went with him and died then or if she died about a year later. The children we
2 CONC re said to have been left with a family who lived in some community west of Toronto. They could not recall the name of the place. I have always assumed the community was in Ancaster Township and have speculated that it was a Scots family they had k
2 CONC nown back in the Glasgow area. However I am quite willing to believe that Janet Thane somehow ended up as a servant in one of the Kitchen households. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;My grandfather said that originally Janet ended up with the William Kitchen family in a settlement to the east of Brantford, Ontario, the pioneer settlement of Summit, Lynden
2 CONC , and Dundas. This may be the community Jessie and Gordon were referring to. The memory of the mother having died soon after the father is clearly incorrect, but perhaps it is a memory of the family having been split up at that time for good; thi
2 CONC s would seem to parallel the &amp;quot;Western&amp;quot; branch of the family remembering Janet as having died in 1833, and perhaps this is a reasonable date for the separation of mother and daughter. Certainly my grandfather makes no mention of th
2 CONC e mother having died. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;My grandfather remembers the names of the two Kitche
2 CONC n daughters as Susan and Hattie but Marilyn Brown corrects this: &amp;quot;William Kitchen and Anna (Vansickle) Kitchen had 7 sons and 5 daughters.&amp;nbsp; Susan and Hattie (Harriet) Kitchen are not among them. These two daughters belong in the n
2 CONC ext generation and were daughters of Henry Kitchen, a brother of Joseph Kitchen. It appears your grandfather remembered the names but confused the generations.&amp;quot; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&
2 CONC gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;My grandfather continues:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-fami
2 CONC ly: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;One accomplishment that [Janet] learned on that farm, other than hand sewing, knitting, milking cows, cooking and gardening, was smoking a very strong brand of homegrown tobacco in a clay pipe. She had acq
2 CONC uired that ability, even as a small girl, from the duty she had of rolling birch-bark squibs like pencils, getting them alight in the fireplace, and next lighting the pipe of her foster father, William Kitchen. In the process she acquired a life-lo
2 CONC ng habit of smoking quite strong tobacco herself.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;An aside: During the last ten years or so of her life, she spent much of her time in my father&amp;#39;s and mother&amp;#39;s home in Ontario. Perhaps it was in the afternoon followin
2 CONC g the noon meal that she used to retire to her bedroom, fill, light her pipe, and enjoy a cloudy smoke. Many a time I have enjoyed watching her having this pleasure. Mother did not entirely approve of it but Dad had a different attitude. Regularly
2 CONC , he supplied her with quite ample quantities of different brands of tobacco. Somehow or other, Mamma had found in Baptist theology grounds for disapproval. To tell the truth, however, in a short time she dropped her objections and took up quite we
2 CONC ll-founded excuses for Grandma&amp;#39;s practice, only smiling grimly at the smell of the fragrant weed from above.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family
2 CONC : 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Every winter I was supplied with a new pair of black mitts, fastened together with a long braided woolen cord. Of course Grandma was the donor. It had to be an annual practice because in our rinks, our sleig
2 CONC hing hills or in snow-balling, we lost by January, February or March those gifts of the year before. Grandma Kitchen died when she was eighty-seven. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Since she died in 1908, this would put her birth year at about 1821 or 22, which again suggests, if she was indeed separated from her mother at age nine or ten, that 1832 or 3
2 CONC 3 would be the date at which the family broke up for good. My grandfather resumes:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times n
2 CONC ew roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;But I have got ahead of the story of Great-Grandma Kitchen. One of the sons of [William] Kitchen was [Joseph] Kitchen [b. about 1818] [I should note that in the original memoir, my grandfather throughout made the erro
2 CONC r of referring to Janet Thane&amp;#39;s husband as &amp;quot;William&amp;quot;, but all records have his name as Joseph. I have used square brackets around his name wherever it appears for this reason. He also, incorrectly, refers to Joseph&amp;#39
2 CONC ;s father as William Henry Kitchen, but this was a different Kitchen relative: William Kitchen was born in 1787 in New Jersey and died in Ancaster Township, Ontario, in 1845; his wife was Anna Vansickle , who was born 1791 and died after 1861. Jose
2 CONC ph Kitchen was born around 1817-19 and died around 1892.)] &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;My grandfather al
2 CONC so thought that the Kitchens were Pennsylvania German (or &amp;quot;Pennsylvania Dutch&amp;quot;), and Joseph may well have been told this by his father, William. In the 1881 Ontario census, Joseph cites his origins as German, but the Kitchens wer
2 CONC e almost certainly English. (See Don Anger&amp;#39;s book on the Kitchen Family, in which he argues that Henry Kitchen, c.1690-1745, who arrived in New Jersey c. 1710-17, probably came from somewhere in Yorkshire.) The Kitchens, along with the Vans
2 CONC ickles&amp;mdash;who really were Dutch but also often gave their ethnic origin as German&amp;mdash;migrated from New Jersey to Pennsylvania and then to Ontario at the end of the 18th century or beginning of the 19th. They bought land from the origi
2 CONC nal grantors of the Dundas Settlement. My Grandfather referred to it as &amp;quot;the settlement of Summit, Lynden and Dundas,&amp;quot; and says that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;[I]t was founded by two men..[who had] come up from&amp;hellip;Pennsylvania, about 1790 in search of free land for their Pennsylvania Dutch relative
2 CONC s. Having searched the area near Dundas, west of Hamilton, they found hundreds of acres of fine uncleared land and submitted a petition to the government of Upper Canada at Fort Niagara for a grant of thousands of acres. The two men were called Kit
2 CONC chen and Betzner, and their petition was favourably received. The grant of thousands of acres of fine prime forests was given to them to be shared by whatever settlers they could induce to immigrate to that area of Upper Canada.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Maril
2 CONC yn Brown corrects and amplifies this:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;The Kitchen family had come from east o
2 CONC f Brantford in Ancaster Township, Wentworth County. [The] Kitchen family lived in the Lynden, Copetown, Summit area. Ancaster Township was first settled by 22 men called James Wilson and Associates who began squatting four years before the lands we
2 CONC re surveyed. Some of them were Pennsylvania Germans, some were not. Their first petition was dated 1793, but they were there as early as 1789. None of them were Kitchens and our Kitchens were not Pennsylvania Germans. Our Kitchens had to buy thei
2 CONC r land when they arrived in 1801. Talbot [I do not know what she meant by this name: perhaps a mistake and she meant &amp;quot;Wilson&amp;quot; or this was perhaps one of the &amp;quot;Associates&amp;quot;] was allowed much this type of latitude bu
2 CONC t nearly everyone else had to petition separately. And it was to Montreal they had to travel to obtain a ticket of claim. Also, the petition submitted by Wilson&amp;#39;s group in 1793 was not the end of the matter.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;My grandfather continues: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-f
2 CONC amily: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;[Joseph] was living, of course, on the same farm as Janet Thane. He fell in love with her and married her whe
2 CONC n both of them were very young. [Joseph] and his bride went back to the middle portion of the peninsula of Western Ontario. There he took up some land not far from the pioneer cabin of the founder of the Buck clan in Upper Canada...&lt;/span&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;A little village grew up near their farm. It was called Windham Centre. Great-Grandma Kitche
2 CONC n has told me quite interesting stories about her early years on their farm. I remember tales of wolves howling in the forest beyond their cabin in the same woods. I recall that she has told me of the difficult struggle that my great-grandfather [J
2 CONC oseph] had in clearing his land of ...pine trees. Their stumps were enormous. Also she has spoken of his ability to acquire new skills. One of these was blacksmithing. After an apprenticeship in Windham Centre, he set up as a smithy. &lt;/span&gt;&
2 CONC lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Great-Grandfather Kitchen&amp;#39;s life had been a hard one. In the last years of that li
2 CONC fe he lived chiefly in an armchair, never going to bed. On Great-Grandmother Kitchen&amp;#39;s shoulders fell a great deal of the burden of rearing her young children...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&g
2 CONC t;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Marilyn Brown reports that &amp;quot;Joseph and Janet married at Alberton, Ancaster Township, circa 1840-42,&amp;quot; (other records definitively say 1840
2 CONC ) and adds that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;[They] removed from Ancaster Township before 1851 with the o
2 CONC lder children to Norfolk County in Windham Township. Then they moved to South Norwich Township, Oxford County, at Otterville where they lived for a time. This is how their oldest child, Anna or Ann, met Robert Buck: they were married May 25, 1865
2 CONC . In November, 1865, Joseph, Janet, and the by-then-larger family moved back to Windham Township, near Bookton.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'tim
2 CONC es new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Joseph and Janet had nine children: Ann or Anna, b. 1841 or 42, Marisa/Mariah/Maria b.1850, Sophia (age 24 on the 1881 Ontario census and still at home) and Arvilla (age 22 on the 1881 Ontario census; still at hom
2 CONC e) were the girls; the boys were William (b. 1843), David (c.1845) , James (1848-1936) , Armanus (c. 1858), and John (b. 1860). Some of these children ended up in the United States. Marisa/Mariah reportedly went to Iowa and Arvilla is said to hav
2 CONC e gone to Kansas. James and John emigrated from Ontario to Minneapolis, Minnesota, while Armanus went to South Dakota. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-fami
2 CONC ly: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;In another letter to me, Marilyn wrote that Aunt Jessie had told her that the name of the brother she remembered Grandma Kitchen talking about was John J. Thane. Jessie did not, as I have said, remember he
2 CONC aring mention of any other brother, and this recollection of the name of John J. Thane was probably a result of the fresher memories resulting from the 1878 visit to Canada on his return from Scotland, as recorded in the travel journal owned by hi
2 CONC s descendent, Cleora Peterson. Cleora reports: &amp;quot;He visited with Janet. John said how happy they were to see each other and sad when they parted. He hoped they would see each other again in the flesh, but I don&amp;#39;t think they ever did
2 CONC .&amp;quot; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;As I have noted, my grandfather recalled that Janet had &amp;ldq
2 CONC uo;brothers&amp;quot;, not merely &amp;quot;a brother&amp;quot;, but he gives no names in his memoir, and does not mention anything about them. He probably knew something of the visit, by hearsay, and might have added more upon reading the text its
2 CONC elf if he had not died before the memoir was transcribed, since he says no more about &amp;quot;Grandma Kitchen&amp;quot; after the above reference to her having had to raise her children practically by herself. He was probably side-tracked and di
2 CONC d not pick up the thread he had clearly dropped almost in mid-thought. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;So i
2 CONC t remains for his father, also a Charles Buck (c.1866-1913), son of Ann Kitchen and Robert Buck and grandson of Janet Thane to have the last word, in a letter he wrote to his cousin R[uth?] Kitchen, daughter of James, on March 22, 1908: &lt;/span&g
2 CONC t;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Dear Cousin:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12p
2 CONC t"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;I received your kind letter a few days ago I can assure you I were glad to hear from you. And was considerably surprised to hear from you, I have a di
2 CONC m recollection of your sister that died and also the one next to her...your mother and father lived near Grandpa&amp;#39;s then. Well do I remember when Father and Mother used to drive us down there and the grand times we used to have. Yes, poor Gr
2 CONC andma is gone. The past two years she has not had the best of health--I could see that she was failing. At times she would look quite well. Then she would get bad again. She was with me a good deal of the time.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Th
2 CONC is winter she was most of the time with mother [Ann Kitchen Buck, d. 1913]&amp;mdash;I was with her the last few days of her life. She took bad Sunday. I was there at the time. She was complaining a good deal of her head and stomach but talked an
2 CONC d ate some supper. As I went home I called in the Dr. and I sent him up. He was there two or three times a day afterwards. Everything was done that could be done. A good deal of the time she was a little wandering in her mind. The Sunday I was ther
2 CONC e she told me she would[n&amp;#39;t] be here [long]. She spoke of you all Especially Aunt Maria [Mary, first wife of James Kitchen: James and Mary had gone to Decorah, Iowa, by 1875, where Mary died in 1888]. Strange she spoke of her visit over the
2 CONC re with you. I might say it was the last talk I had with her. I was there night and day until she died. But afterwards she was too sick to talk. Once in awhile she would mention some of your names. I got the best Dr. in. He done all that could be d
2 CONC one. She suffered no pain and only was very weak. I was with her Wednesday night all night. In the morning she seemed better. I left early in the morning. Mother&amp;nbsp;and Uncle John [Janet&amp;#39;s youngest son] carried her in another room tho
2 CONC ught it would be more comfortable for her. In half an hour she died. Just went to sleep and never woke again. The best I can explain it to you. I can assure you Grandma met death like a hero. She was not afraid to die. She told me her life&amp;#39;
2 CONC s work was done. &amp;quot;I want now to go to the better land. I have lived more for others than myself. I have tried to bring a little sunshine in the world. I hope the world has been made a little better by me being in it...&amp;quot; This is th
2 CONC e way she would talk. Then she would get to wandering again. But most of the time would talk very sensible and nice. We got her a nice casket and a burial plot room to lay Grandpa by the side of her...we would like to move Grandpa and put a mar
2 CONC k I mean a [head]stone at their graves...I am glad to tell you Grandma never wanted for anything. We always seen she had some money, and plenty of good clothes....Well, I will close for this time. Will write you a letter again. Give you more news c
2 CONC oncerning other things. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;From your cousin &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-f
2 CONC amily: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Charlie Buck, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Norwich&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt; Ontario&lt;/span&g
2 CONC t;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt;Box&lt;/span&g
2 CONC t;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt; 44&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 12pt"&gt; Canada&lt;/span&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt; &lt;
2 CONC p style="margin: 0in 0in 0pt"&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;Janet Thane was buried at Norwich Village Cemetery, North Norwich Township, Oxford County, Ontario. As Charlie Buck had hoped, Joseph Kitchen&amp;rsquo;s coffin now rest
2 CONC s beside hers.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt; &lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0pt"&gt;&lt;font size="3"&gt;&lt;font face="Tim
2 CONC es New Roman"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;(See &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;http://www.islandnet.com/ocfa/&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt; for the Ontario Cemetery Findin
2 CONC g Aid.)&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font face="Times New Roman" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font face="Ti
2 CONC mes New Roman" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
