2 CONC ble Normal"; 	mso-tstyle-rowband-size:0; 	mso-tstyle-colband-size:0; 	mso-style-noshow:yes; 	mso-style-parent:""; 	mso-padding-alt:0in 5.4pt 0in 5.4pt; 	mso-para-margin:0in; 	mso-para-margin-bottom:.0001pt; 	mso-pagination:widow-orphan; 	font-size:
2 CONC 10.0pt; 	font-family:"Times New Roman"; 	mso-ansi-language:#0400; 	mso-fareast-language:#0400; 	mso-bidi-language:#0400;} &lt;/style&gt; &lt;![endif]--&gt;  &lt;p style="margin-: 51pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&lt;span&gt;THE&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt
2 CONC ;/span&gt;SHROPSHIRE&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;TURNERS&amp;rsquo;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;FRENCH&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;CONNECTION&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="line-height: 150%"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Family trees fan out the further back in time we care to trace.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Everyone ha
2 CONC s two parents, four grand parents, eight great grand parents, sixteen great great grand parents and so on.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;My mother was Dorothea Emily (nee Turner) b.1906 and the earliest Turner I knew was her father
2 CONC , my grandfather, Francis Richard Turner b.1877 in Newport, Salop, he died on the November 10&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1940&lt;sup&gt; &lt;/sup&gt;in Liverpool.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;His paternal grandparents were Edward Turner b. c181
2 CONC 3 in Salop and his wife Mary (nee Blower) b. c1815 also in Salop and his maternal grand parents were John Bullock b.1816 and his wife Hannah (nee Lewis) b. c1822 in Shrewsbury.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;It was Hannah&amp;rsquo;s eldest sis
2 CONC ter Mary Lewis b.1805 in Shrewsbury who initiated the &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;French Connection&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-
2 CONC height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;The following account is derived from a bundle of original documents, which I&a
2 CONC mp;rsquo;m going to refer to as a family historians &amp;ldquo;Pandora&amp;rsquo;s Box&amp;rsquo;, collected and preserved by my grandfather&amp;rsquo;s sister Louisa Emma Turner (1879-1976) and passed down to her niece Sophia Smith (nee Turner, 19
2 CONC 02-1996) thence to her daughter Pat Smith one of my many first cousins who has made them available to me.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font
2 CONC -size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;The earliest document in the box is a &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt
2 CONC ;Visa&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; authorising Mary Lewis to go to Paris in 1838.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;What attracted her to Paris, or whether or not she intended to settle there, is not known but sixteen years later she married Monsieu
2 CONC r Louis Philippe de Perpigna, the eldest son of Monsieur le Chevalier Jean Antoine de Perpigna.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The proof of this is contained in a seven-page &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Marriage Contract&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; do
2 CONC cument, also in the box, which details the entitlements and possessions of the parties.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Mary was henceforth known as Madame Marie de Perpigna and resided for the rest of her life at 17 Rue de Bruxelles, Paris.&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span styl
2 CONC e="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Marie de Perpigna&amp;rsquo;s sister Hannah Bullock (nee Lewis b.1822) had two daughters, Jane b.1846 and Emma b.1848.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Jane married Richard Turner and they had five childr
2 CONC en, Henry b.1871, Francis b.1877 (my grandfather), Louisa Emma b.1879, Ernest b.1883 and Alfred b.1886.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;In the box is a hand written note on the back of a hand written copy of Louisa Emma&amp;rsquo;s baptism detai
2 CONC ls certified by the Rector of Newport reads, &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;I Jane Turner leave my daughter Louisa Emma to the care of my aunt, Madame de Perpigna, July 8&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1891, signed Jane Turner&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="line-height: 150%"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Louisa Emma Turner, known affectionately as Auntie Lou&amp;rsquo; by later Turner generations, was 11 years old at that time and Marie de Perpigna (nee Lewis), her grand aunt was aged 86. &lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Two years later, on June 19&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1893 Marie de Perpigna died.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&
2 CONC gt;Louisa, still only 13, was not without a blood relative at 17 Rue de Bruxelles as her aunt, Emma Bullock had moved in from 40 Rue de Amsterdam two months earlier.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Emma had registered this change of address wit
2 CONC h the Prefecture of Police on 27&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; March 1893, as required by all foreigners in Paris at that time.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;This begs the question when did Emma Bullock leave Shrewsbury?&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;The 1851 census records Emma, aged 3, living with her mother Hannah, aged 29, father John aged 35, brother William aged 7 and sister Jane aged 5 in Shrewsbury.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The 1861 census records brother William (17
2 CONC ) and sister Jane (15) living at home with their parents but Emma was not.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The 1871 census records the parents living alone, William and Jane having married and living elsewhere, but no trace of Emma.&lt;span&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Could she also have been &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;lsquo;left to the care&amp;rsquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; of her aunt Marie whilst still a child?&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The absence of any record of her i
2 CONC n the British censuses after 1851 and known to be domiciled in Paris in 1893 suggest this is probable.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Returning to Marie&amp;rsquo;s death, in the box there are three copies of a black bordered printe
2 CONC d &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Notice of the&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;funeral arrangements of Madame Veurve (w
2 CONC idow) Marie de Perpigna,&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"
2 CONC &gt;listing the pecking order of&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;principal mourners thus; &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mrs Jane Reynolds and children, Mrs Hannah Bullock, Miss Emma Bullock, Mr and Mrs William Bullock and their child, M
2 CONC r and Mrs Richard Turner and their children, nephews and nieces and Monsieur le Comte (Count) Henri de Perpigna, his children and the whole family.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;The funeral servic
2 CONC e took place at the English Church, 5 Rue d&amp;rsquo;Aguessau and the body was interred in the de Perpigna family tomb at the Pere-Lachaise Cemetery, Paris.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The tomb had been restored on Marie&amp;rsquo;s instruc
2 CONC tion in 1887.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;(Receipt in the box).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &
2 CONC lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Some ten months after her aunt Marie&amp;rsquo;s death Emma Bullock married Richard Lewis in Paris.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&
2 CONC gt;I have not been able to establish whether Richard was related to Marie and Hannah Lewis or when he went to work in Paris.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The 1871 census records Richard, aged 8, living with his father, John Lewis and grandmot
2 CONC her, Elizabeth Lewis in the Mount, Shrewsbury.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;He was not living with his father at the time of the 1881 or 1891 censuses and although I traced two 18 years olds named Richard Lewis in the 1881 census, each a livi
2 CONC ng-in farm labourer born and working in Shropshire, I cannot match him with either.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Richard&amp;rsquo;s marriage to Emma Bullock was solemnized at the British Consul&amp;rsquo;s office in Paris on 19&lt;sup&gt;th&
2 CONC lt;/sup&gt; April 1894.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;He was 32, a bachelor and a telegraph clerk, she was 47, a spinster and of no profession. Their residence at the time of marriage is given as 9 Rue Demours, Les Ternes, Paris.&lt;span&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The Marriage Certificate&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="line-height: 150%"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&g
2 CONC t;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;is signed by George Falconer Atlee, Acting British Consul.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The witnesses present were R.Turn
2 CONC er, M.Lewis and four others.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;A Marriage Blessing at The English Church, 4 Rue Roquepine followed the civil ceremony and guests attended by invitation from Mrs Hannah Bullock and Mr and Mrs Lewis (no first names) o
2 CONC f Nottingham. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left
2 CONC "&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Seven years later, on the 27&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; May 1901 the Pastor of The English Congregational Church wrote a letter to Richard which is copied below: -&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &l
2 CONC t;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Dear Mr Lewis,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%
2 CONC " align="left"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;On the eve of your departure from Paris, I have the honour, as Minister of the English Congregational Church worshipping at 23 Rue Royale, to convey t
2 CONC o you, in the name of our church and congregation, our sincere thanks for all the kind services you have rendered us during all the time you have been acting as Deacon of our church.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="te
2 CONC xt-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;May the Lord&amp;rsquo;s blessings accompany you and Mrs Lewis and all dear to you wherever it will be His good pleasure t
2 CONC o direct your steps.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Believe me to remain, ever affection
2 CONC ately yours, in our Divine Redeemer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Samuel H Anderson, P
2 CONC astor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-
2 CONC align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;The next document of significance in the box is a receipt addressed to Richard Lewis at Newlyn, The Mount, Shrewsbury.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt;It is for the purchase of a grave in Shrewsbury General Cemetery in the sum of &amp;pound;1.5s and dated 29&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; November 1906.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Emma, his wife had died in Paris.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;According to an extract from the Bois-Colombes register of deaths (French death certificate) signed by Le Maire (Mayor), Emma died on 21&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; November 1906.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;If my Google translation i
2 CONC s correct, the extract states that Emma, aged 59 years, was residing in the conjugal home in Shropshire but died at 10.30pm at 92 Rue Raspail in Bois-Colombes, Paris, whilst visiting the residence of Lucien Charles Alexander Ghilbert, aged 34years.
2 CONC &lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Another gentleman was present, he was 31 years old Mathieu Fraisse.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;I cannot determine whether Richard had accompanied Emma, he may well have done, if so, he must have returne
2 CONC d to Shrewsbury promptly to make arrangements for the funeral.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Richard demonstrated his devotion to Emma by arranging the repatriation of her body home to Shrewsbury for burial.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC Receipts, in the box, from Parisian and Shrewsbury Undertakers as well as the London &amp;amp; North Western Railway of England give a morbid insight into the carriage of the coffin from Paris to Calais, the channel crossing to Dover, the rail tran
2 CONC sit to London and transfer to L&amp;amp;NW Railway for the last lap to Shrewsbury.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The burial took place on 29&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; November 1906 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height
2 CONC : 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;for which W.Felton, the local Undertaker, charged &amp;pound;6.18.0 which illustrates the effect of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%
2 CONC " align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;inflation over the past century. An item on his bill reads &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;men, &amp;frac12; gallon of beer 1s&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt
2 CONC ; &lt;/em&gt;(5p).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="line-height: 150%"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&g
2 CONC t;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;A letter from Maye
2 CONC r Freres &amp;amp; Cie, Paris dated 15&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; November 1910, indicates that Richard had moved from Shrewsbury to 24 Deepdale Villas, Chetwynd, Newport.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Later a bank statement from the Banque de A
2 CONC rgentine &amp;amp; Francaise dated 24 November 1928 gave his address as 52 Church Aston, Newport.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Some 39 years after his wife Emma, he died and was buried in their grave at Shrewsbury  Cemetery (Sec.29-5E) on 19&
2 CONC lt;sup&gt;th &lt;/sup&gt;July 1945, aged 83 years.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;The Lewis&amp;rsquo; departure from Paris in 1901 had left Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; without an Englis
2 CONC h relative there.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;It is not known when she returned to England, but the 1911 Census records her living at the home of Richard Lewis, 24 Chester Road, Newport.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Pat Smith recalls t
2 CONC hat Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; informed her that she had also lived at the Mount in Shrewsbury, where Richard lived until he moved to Newport sometime before 1910.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Some group photographs in the box show her with relative
2 CONC s and Richard at his house in Church Aston.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Several other group photographs feature a French gentleman who was always referred to as &amp;lsquo;Nunc&amp;rsquo;.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Apparently he vis
2 CONC ited Newport between the wars to visit both Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; and Richard Lewis.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo;s father, Richard died in 1922 and her mother, Jane in 1929.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nb
2 CONC sp; &lt;/span&gt;They are buried together in Shrewsbury   General Cemetery (sec.29-5A).&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;At sometime Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; moved into 25 Water Lane to live with her brother Ernest.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC Neither of them married.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Ernest had a successful career with Audley Engineering Co.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;A newspaper cutting with photograph shows Ernest with the granddaughter clock he had receive
2 CONC d at a presentation for thirty years service with the Company.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;At that time he held the appointment of Cashier, later in his obituary he was credited with the appointment of Company Secretary.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ; &lt;/span&gt;He died, aged 62 and was buried on 19&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; April 1948 in Shrewsbury General Cemetery (Ext.12-7).&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Also buried in this grave on 28&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; May 1952 is Louisa Alic
2 CONC e Ann Turner (little Lou&amp;rsquo;) who was the third child of Henry Turner, Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo;s eldest brother, and his wife Alice (nee Atkins).&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Little Lou&amp;rsquo; was born in Liverpool on 16.10.1892 but rea
2 CONC red by her grandparents at 25 Water Lane, Newport, she moved to the house next door as an adult and also remained a spinster.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 15
2 CONC 0%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;There were now thre
2 CONC e related graves in Shrewsbury Cemetery and several Invoices with specifications for headstones, curbing, engraving and the like, all addressed to Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; at 25 Water Lane, indicate that she had assumed responsibility for their maintena
2 CONC nce.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Pat describes Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; as a strong independent personality and not the best of patients.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;As she had never been employed she did not receive an OAP but was grante
2 CONC d one when she was about eighty, following a successful appeal.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="line-height: 150%"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/s
2 CONC trong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; remained in the family home after Ernest&amp;rsquo;s death and sold it to her other next d
2 CONC oor neighbour when she was about eighty, conditional upon her being able to continue living there, rent-free, for the rest of her life.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The neighbour was a builder and had intentions of combining the properties bu
2 CONC t had to wait 17 years to realise his objective.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Later Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; sold most of her valuables to supplement her pension.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;In the end, she lost the will to live and died i
2 CONC n Newport Cottage Hospital, her trusted Doctor holding her hand.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo; was 97 when she died in October 1976 and according to the obituary published in th
2 CONC e Newport Advertiser the chief mourners were her niece Sophia Smith (nee Turner), her grand niece Pat Smith and husband Don Smith.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Aunt Lou&amp;rsquo;, being aware the family graves in Shrewsbury were full, had el
2 CONC ected to be cremated.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;
2 CONC p style="text-align: ; line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 150%"&gt;W.E.Orr,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;(Revised 1.3.2009).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  </line></content></metadataxml>
