2 TITL Richard Turner and Eleanor Cowell
1 _OID eb8143e1-f7db-49ea-8e3c-0f7d3cfd4f62
1 DATE 1700s
1 PLAC Garstag, Lancashire, England
1 _DSCR Richard Turner, Eleanor Cowell, Edward Turner, James Cowell (1588)
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;The origins of the Turner family are clouded.&amp;nbsp; It is possible the family was forced to move from the Scottish Lowlands to Ireland&amp;nbsp;in the 1600s by order of the Crown, thus explaining why the Tur
2 CONC ners--not an Irish name--seem to have come from Ireland. Border records, according to Verne Turner, a family researcher, shows a Turner name listed as one of the minor clans in the Border Reiver histories(1). If the Turners were ordered to Ireland
2 CONC , it would explain the confusion between different family researchers who variously associate the line with the Scots and the Irish.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Richard Turner was born in Ireland and at some point moved to England, where he was employed by J
2 CONC ames Cowell, oftimes called the &amp;quot;Earl of Cowell of Blackmoor.&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;The title was one of perhaps respect or, possibly, awe of wealth, but it is not a recognized title of nobility (2). At some point, Richard convince
2 CONC d Eleanor he was the one.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;According to family researcher Michael Kaehn, Richard&amp;#39;s family owned the &amp;quot;Turner Farm, Barnacre-with-Bonds&amp;quot; near Garstang&amp;nbsp;and indicates the farm remained in the Turner f
2 CONC amily until recently. It is of interest to note, however,&amp;nbsp;that parish records reference an Edward Turner, who leased property from the Church in 1688 (3). This latter finding seems to provide support those who question why Turner would wor
2 CONC k 10 to 15 miles from his family&amp;#39;s farm for another man.&amp;nbsp; Furthermore, there is data indicating Cowell was furious that his daughter&amp;nbsp;married a workman on Cowell&amp;#39;s property (1). If the families had been of equal sta
2 CONC tus, it is unlikely the marriage would have been an issue. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Richard and Eleanor were married near Garstang, England&amp;nbsp;on November 11, 1737, according to the Preston Archives, and they posted bonds weeks ahead of the date(1
2 CONC , 2).&amp;nbsp; This allowed those who wanted to dispute their marriage opportunity to do so: one might wonder why Cowell did not take advantage of the bond process to stop the wedding. With the wedding behind them, the&amp;nbsp; newlyweds moved t
2 CONC o the Border area, evidently as a result of Cowell&amp;#39;s attitude. This detail further ties the Turner clan to Scottish lineage rather than Irish.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The family did not stay long. The Border was wild, and Cowell evidently forgav
2 CONC e his daughter for marrying beneath her station, after he reputedly suffered through the deaths of his sons*. The young family moved back to Lancastershire (1), and then immigrated&amp;nbsp;to the colonies in 1774 (2).&amp;nbsp; From Maine, the lin
2 CONC e moved to Canada, and they appear in lists of&amp;nbsp;English loyalists.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;* This detail has not yet been verified by records.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;SOURCES&lt;br&gt;__________
2 CONC _________&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. Turner, Verne. Email; sources include hand-written family records, relatives, and public resources.&amp;nbsp;30 Mar 2009.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. Kaehn, Michael. Posting on RootsWeb. http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com
2 CONC /th/read/LANCSGEN/1999-02/0918419668. Accessed 08 Feb 2009.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. University of London &amp;amp; History of Parliament. &amp;quot;The Parish of Garstang.&amp;quot; Citation for &lt;em&gt;L. and P. Hen. VIII&lt;/em&gt;, xiv (1), 609
2 CONC ; a grant by the Court of Augmentations in 1539. A lease for twenty-one years was made to Edward Turner in 1588; Pat. 30 Eliz. pt. xv. http://www.british-history.ac.uk/report.aspx?compid=53240. Accessed 08 Feb 2009.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></meta
2 CONC dataxml>
