2 TITL July Ann Moon Life Story
1 _OID ec20bed8-f735-4c42-8868-32e795ba98a4
1 DATE 12 December 1953
1 _DSCR Life story of July Ann Moon, the last surviving daughter of Elizabeth Kemmish and Hugh Moon
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #555555; font-family: proximanova, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 22px; white-space: pre-wrap; background-color: #eeeeee;"&gt;Written December 12, 1953 Today is my 85th birthday
2 CONC , I am the last survivor of my father&amp;rsquo;s family (3 wives, 27 children). I have been asked by my family and relatives to relate a few events of my life. I was born December 12, 1868, in a log house on what was called the Nelson farm at Farm
2 CONC ington, Davis County, Utah, the eighth child of Hugh and Elizabeth Kemmish Moon. Father was born August 5, 1815, at Eccleston, Lanshire, England, the youngest son and eighth arrival in a family of nine children of Mathias and Alice Plumb Moon. He w
2 CONC as twenty one years old when King William died and Victoria became Queen of England (July 17, 1837). Three days after this great event the ship "Garrick" docked at Liverpool, England. Among the passengers were seven missionaries from America. On Ju
2 CONC ly 23, at Vauxhall Chapel, Elder Heber C. Kimball spoke the first sermon of the restored gospel to the people of the British Isles. From the missionary work of Elder Kimball the Moon family were converted and baptized by him, Grandfather, Grandmoth
2 CONC er, their four daughters were baptized December 6. Father and his four brothers and other relatives on December 10, 1937 in England. The following in a quote from the Times and Seasons: Times and Seasons, Vol.2, p.494 (Also found in &amp;ldquo;Lif
2 CONC e of Heber C. Kimball&amp;rdquo; by Orson F. Whitney&amp;rdquo; Having an appointment to preach in the village of Wrightington, while on the way I stopped at the house of Brother Amos Fielding; when I arrived he informed me that a certain family o
2 CONC f the name of Moon, had sent a request by him, for me to visit them, that they might have the privilege of conversing with me on the subject of the gospel. Accordingly, Br. Fielding and I paid them a visit that evening. We were very kindly receive
2 CONC d by the family, and had considerable conversation on the object of my mission to that country, and the great work of the last days; they listened with attention to my statements, but at the same time they appeared to be prejudiced against them, ra
2 CONC ther than otherwise. We remained in conversation until a late hour, and then returned. On our way home, Brother Fielding observed, that he thought our visit had been in vain, as the family seemed to have considerable prejudice. I answered, and said
2 CONC , Brother Fielding, be not faithless, but believing, we shall yet see great effects from this visit, for I know there are some of the family that have received the testimony and will shortly manifest the same. At this remark he appeared surprised
2 CONC . The next morning I continued my journey to Wrightington, and after spending two or three days in that vicinity, preaching the gospel, I returned by the way of Brother Fielding's with whom I again tarried for the night. Times and Seasons, Vol.2, p
2 CONC .495 The next morning I commenced my journey; intending to go direct to Preston, but when I got opposite the road leading to Mr. Moons, I was forcibly pressed upon by the spirit of the Lord, to call and see them again. The impression being so stron
2 CONC g, I could not resist, I therefore directed my steps to the house, not knowing what it meant. On my arrival at the house, I knocked at the door, and Mrs. Moon from within exclaimed; "Come in! come in! you are welcome here! I and the lasses, (meanin
2 CONC g her daughters) have just been calling on the Lord, and praying that he would send you this way." She then informed me of her state of mind since I was there before, and said, she at first rejected my testimony, and endeavored to think lightly o
2 CONC n the things that I had advanced, but on trying to pray, she said "that the heavens seemed to be like brass over her head, and it was like iron under her feet," she did not know what was the matter, "certainly the man has not bewitched me," and upo
2 CONC n inquiry she "found it was the same with the lasses." They then began to reflect on the things I had told them, and thinking it possible that I had told them the truth, they resolved to lay the case before the Lord, and beseech him to give the
2 CONC m a testimony concerning the things I had testified of. She then observed, that as soon as they did so, light broke in upon their minds, they were convinced that I was a messenger of salvation, and that it was the work of the Lord, and they had res
2 CONC olved to obey the gospel, which they did, and that evening I baptized father and mother and four of their daughters. Shortly after I visited them again and baptized the remainder of the family, consisting of thirteen souls, the youngest of whom wa
2 CONC s above twenty years of age. They received the gospel as little children, and rejoiced exceedingly in its blessings. The sons were very good musicians, and the daughters excellent singers, and when they united their instruments and their voices i
2 CONC n the songs of Zion, the effect was truly transporting. Before I left England, there were about thirty of that family and connexions, baptized, six of whom were ordained to be fellow laborers with us in the vineyard, and I left them rejoicing in th
2 CONC e truths, they had embraced." End of Quote May 30, 1840, father, (Hugh Moon) his Mother, four sisters, four brothers and other relatives bade farewell to their relatives, friends, and native England, (Grandfather died November 30, 1839 was buried a
2 CONC t Eccleston) June 6, at Liverpool, England. They embarked on the Ship "Britannia" for America, being the first Company of 41 Saints to sail from a foreign County, they arrived at New York, July 20, for eight months they worked at several differen
2 CONC t places in the State of Pennsylvania. April 3, 1841, by boat the "William Penn" they went to St. Louis, Missouri, and on to Nauvoo, Illinois. There they were greeted by fathers Aunt Lydia, Uncle Thomas Moon, and Uncle William Clayton. I have hear
2 CONC d Mother relate many times of Father telling her the Prophet Joseph Smith was the greatest man living at that time. When at Monterouse, Iowa, he predicted the Saints would be driven from their homes by their enemies, and forced to find refuge in th
2 CONC e Rocky Mountains, where they would become a mighty people. Hugh lived at Nauvoo during the trying times when the Prophet Joseph and his brother Hyrum were killed. He was a body guard for the Prophet (although we haven&amp;rsquo;t documented it). A
2 CONC t that time he moved to Montrose, Lee County Iowa. He was at the meeting when President Brigham Young spoke to the people and said he spoke with great power and authority the congregation was astonished as he appeared to be the Prophet Joseph speak
2 CONC ing to them, they were convinced he was the proper authority to lead the church. Father married Maria Emeline Mott August 6, 1846 at Nauvoo, Illinois, (married at home of William Moses by Elder Thomas Cottam). He and Aunt Maria arrived in Salt Lak
2 CONC e Valley October 19, 1848, their son Hugh Moon Jr. one year and seventeen days old, died November 7, shortly after they arrived in the Valley. They were 158 days making the trip across the rugged and desolate plains, in a wagon he had constructed h
2 CONC imself, drawn by a lame cow and an oxen. Father and Aunt Maria had seven children their eldest son Hugh Jr. was born at Nauvoo, Illinois. They had four little girls: Dorothy Adealee, Maria Emeline, and twins Evaline and Angeline. When mother marrie
2 CONC d father, Aunt Maria had two sons after Hugh, Mathias, and Lehi, they were all born at Salt Lake City. After father&amp;rsquo;s death, Aunt Maria went to California with her daughter Angeline who married Alma Hall, when she returned, with her son H
2 CONC ugh, she went to Albion, Idaho. Later she lived with her son Hugh on his ranch 12 miles south of Burley, Idaho, where she died February 13, 1899, was buried at Albion, Idaho. Hugh was the only one of Aunt Maria's family I saw after they moved fro
2 CONC m Henderson Creek, Idaho. (When we went to the George Welton Reunion at Burley, June 11, 1931, Uncle Hugh and Aunt Martha (Martha McBride) invited Pa, LaVerne, Eliza and I out to their ranch for dinner. Pa, Uncle Bill, Aunt Ten, and I went to uncl
2 CONC e Hugh 's funeral services December 2, 1933.) Dorothy married Henry Robins, I think they moved to Morris Basin. Henry and Dorothy had 3 children born at Hendersen Creek, when they moved to Albion, Idaho. Dorothy, Lillie and Hailey. My mother, Eliza
2 CONC beth Kemmish Moon was born at South Sea (near Portsmouth) Hampshire, England, April 17, 1837, (just three months before Victoria became Queen of England.) Her parents Charles and Elizabeth Wilkey Kemmish were baptized members of the Church of Jesu
2 CONC s Christ of Latter-day Saints, her father September 7, her mother September 14, 1844. Mother was baptized in the year 1847 in England, with her parents, brothers, sisters, Jane, Peter Charles, Mary, Daniel. James Ephraim, (as listed on the ships ro
2 CONC ster) sailed from Liverpool, England, on the ship "Golconda" January 23, 1853, the 62nd Company of Saints to sail to America, there were 321 Saints in the Company under the direction of Elder Jacob Gates, during they voyage a storm despoiled the ga
2 CONC llant ship of her three top masts which caused deep concern among the Saints, and many prayers were offered or their safety. On February 17, a sister was born on the ship and christened Eliza Golconda Kemmish. They were nine weeks making the 5000 m
2 CONC ile voyage arriving at New Orleans, Louisiana, March 26th, traveling on to St. Lois, Missouri and Keokuk, Iowa, where several weeks were spent in preparation for the trek to Salt Lake Valley. Around the early part of June the Company was ready 42 w
2 CONC agons, 10 in the "Emigration Fund" and 32 in "The Ten Pound Company". The Kemmish family crossed the plains with the "The Ten Pound Company Joseph Young was Captain of the Company. To Mother who was sixteen years of age crossing the plains had man
2 CONC y pleasant memories, she walked most of the way, did the cooking for the members of their company. When wood was not available she gathered buffalo chips which they used as wood. (After we moved to Malad Mother and Mrs. Amelia Bush met at the D.L
2 CONC . Evans Store, they were so happy. They told of crossing the Ocean, traveling over the plains to Utah, later both moving to Oneida County. I wish I could relate some of the experiences they told.) After Mother arrived in Utah she worked for a Mrs
2 CONC . Remmington. While working there she met father at church, under the law of Plural marriage she became his second wife. They were married June 11, 1854 by President Brigham Young at the Council House. Mother has told many times of the three famili
2 CONC es Aunt Maria's, Aunt Jennet's and her family living in a adobe house with three bedrooms, a large living room where all three families ate at a long table. Their food was all cooked on a open fire place, a large cradle rocked the babies of the thr
2 CONC ee wives. Father had been living in Salt Lake for 13 years. At the October Conference in 1861, 309 families were called to help settle Utah Dixie. Father&amp;rsquo;s family being one of them. After one month of getting ready, buying oxens, wagons
2 CONC , grinding wheat, sacking flour, etc. They left November 7th. Mother was carried to the covered wagon, her four day old son Helaman Joseph, and four other children, Mosiah, Alice Elizabeth, Sarah Helen, and Helorum; Aunt Jennet, the third wife, an
2 CONC d her five children Lydia, Ephraim, Manasseh, Benjamin, Jennet, a girl named Ester Barnes, five men as teamsters, five wagons, twelve yoke of oxen, four cows, two horses, one pony, and a molasses mill which father had constructed. Mother said, "Th
2 CONC e journey they took and the time they spent at St. George, Utah was one of the hardiest experiences of her life." On the way at one of the stopping places, which father called Buttermilk Fort, (near Holden, Millard County, Utah) Aunt Jennet gave bi
2 CONC rth to a son on November 17, (Levi). At Round Valley they pitched their tents, got the stove set up. The women and children moved in and it started to snow. The wind almost blew the tents away. The children were all crying, after some difficultie
2 CONC s father got the women and children moved back into the covered wagon, and tried to comfort them with the promise in a few more days they would be in Utah's Dixie where the weather would be warm. They arrived at Washington Company, December 4th hav
2 CONC ing been on the road 28 days. When sister Alice came to visit us in 1912 she said: "They lived in a dugout and tent, father was sick most of the time with chills, fever." The older children picked cotton, striped and topped the cane for the molasse
2 CONC s mill, we hauled wood in a small cart the older boys holding the tongue up, the younger children pushing the cart. Father planted some corn three miles from where they were living, when the corn was ready to eat, some Indians had been watching an
2 CONC d waiting also they stripped every ear of corn off the stocks. We children were several days hauling the stocks home to feed the animals. When brother Si stayed with us from August 10 to September 9th in 1943, he said: "I would like to get some mol
2 CONC asses like father made, he was very particular in making process, the cane was put in the chopper which was operated like the early pioneer choppers, working in a circler motion pushed or pulled by a horse. Father planted some peach trees and alfal
2 CONC fa seed, he got the seed from California, we lived on lucrene greens, corn bread, and molasses." June 11, 1866 father left St. George, Utah returning to Salt Lake City. December 17, father moved mother to Farmington, Davis County, Utah, where she l
2 CONC ived in a log house on what was called the Nelson Farm. I was four months old (April 12, 1869), when Father bought a squatter right at Henderson Creek, Oneida County, Idaho from Josia D. Price paying $1100 for the right. Father and Aunt Jennet an
2 CONC d family moved to the farm May 5th, living in a log house (where the C.W. Bell house now stands 1952.) Sister Hortensia was born January 14, 1870, (in a log house where I was born) Hortensia was two weeks old, Uncle Henry Moon took Mother to Hender
2 CONC son Creek, father was very sick. Sister Alice took care of we children, we all had mumps. With the help of a good neighbor Polly Leonard, we all got along very well. During the summer we moved the family, at Henderson Creek, where we lived in a lo
2 CONC g house with a dirt floor, west of the road, Aunt Maria's house was north of our house where the large trees are. (I have been told they were the first three log houses built at Henderson Creek, later David Bowen built a house where the Brick Schoo
2 CONC l House is now standing (1952) Bill Allreid lived about where Lorum lives, William Bradberry lived where the Glen Price's live (1952). Si was fourteen years old September 22, father died the next day September 23, 1870 on sister Ruwaney's fifth bir
2 CONC thday, at Aunt Jennet's house. I was one year nine months, eleven days old. I have heard other and the older children tell of David Bowen and William Bradberry dressing father's body for burial. He was buried in a lonely Pioneer Cemetery at the Uta
2 CONC h-Idaho line, at East Portage, Box Elder County Utah. (John S. Wells homesteaded the land where the graves are, he told me, there were sixteen graves there and named the following: Father&amp;rsquo;s, baby Hall, Jimmy Howell's Mother and a baby, th
2 CONC ree men Walker, Slatters, and Kesler; Aunt Maggie Knudsen said Luman Wells and his wife buried a baby there, there was a man traveling through the country, his son died of consumption, he was buried a few days before father died). May 30, 1954 a Mo
2 CONC nument was placed at Father's grave with the following inscription, Hugh Moon born August 5, 1815, Eccleston, Lancs, England, joined the L.D.S. Church in 1837, came to America with the first Company of 41 Saints, arrived in Utah 1848, came to Hende
2 CONC rson Creek, Oneida County, Idaho 1869, died September 23, 1870, at Henderson Creek, Idaho. His Wives: Maria Emeline Mott, Elizabeth Kemmish and Jennet Nicol. We were left poor financial circumstances, as a family we suffered the many hardships of p
2 CONC ioneer life. Brother Si started to freight at the age of sixteen, making his first trip to Bozeman, Montana with Henry Bradberry and brother Hugh, he hauled salt from Corrine, Utah, to Butte, Montana with Benjamin Williams, Eph Jones and his brothe
2 CONC r Ephraim, and flour from Eagle Rock, (Idaho Falls, Idaho) to Fort Lemhi from the Indians. I well remember when Si was away freighting of the soldiers marching up the road, four abreast, dressed in blue suits, trimmed in shiny gold buttons. The Ind
2 CONC ians were on the war path, some of the Indians had been killed by the white people, the Militia was called to settle the trouble, when the soldiers returned they were riding horses and stopped at the Benjamin and Ages Williams farm where food was p
2 CONC repared for them. Mother was glad when Si returned home, the first pair of new shoes I had Si bought at Butte, Montana. I was so proud of them, they were black high topped, buttoned on the side with black buttons, trimmed in green and red. We wen
2 CONC t barefooted summer and winter. When we went to Aunt Jennet's house in the winter we waded up the Creek as the water was warm, the Creek was just a few feet from her door. She put a board in front of the fire place for us to warm our feet on. Siste
2 CONC r Ten and I spent many days, helping brother Lorum herd the sheep on the east hills, in the spring we gathered, what we called pig weeds and thistles. These we cooked as greens. Each morning we gathered all we could find, next morning there was jus
2 CONC t as many to take home again. Mother made dumpling dough which she cooked over the greens, we put milk and sugar on them and ate them as a dessert. (These greens we better than the spinach we buy today.) We had watercress from the Henderson Creek S
2 CONC pring. We picked wild currents and Mother made the best pies and preserves, we picked choke cherry's and made into jelly. Brother Lorum went hunting in the fall, we had wild ducks and venison. We cut the venison in long stripes, soaked it in a sal
2 CONC t brine, threaded the strips on a wire the meat was hung back of the stove to dry. We cooled this in the summer. We gleaned wheat along the fence an ditch banks, put it on a canvas cover, the what was shelled out by beating with a flat board, the c
2 CONC haff was sifted out and the wheat was stored for future use. Mother would put some of the wheat in a brass kettle, cover with water, put on the stove to cook, while she did the sewing. I kept the wheat cooking in the morning we precooked it and at
2 CONC e it as much (cereal). I can remember of several times when we didn't have flour nor sugar in the house. Joe Cortley, who stayed at the stage station sent a sack of flour home with the boys. Another time when the roads were very muddy, a freighte
2 CONC r who was hauling flour, got stalled in the mud, he gave us 100 pounds of four, I think the boys helped him get his outfit back on the road. We had one cow I remember adding water to milk making a cup full for we children to drink. June 16, 1872, M
2 CONC other married William T. Bell, he was a good looking man, with dark curly hair and brown eyes, he and Mother got along very well. When he wanted to sell the only team of horses the boys owned, they objected. He left to find work, he returned and wa
2 CONC nted to take Mother and Bill to Logan, Utah, to live. We children made quite a fuss. We took Bill and ran to Aunt Jennet's house, Mother decided she would stay with her family. Bill, (Charles William Bell) was born April 7, 1873 in the log house o
2 CONC n the west side of the road. We children were always interested in the daily arrival and departure of the Stage Coach, with its creaking wheels and trotting horses. The horses were always trotting when they arrived and they left trotting. The stati
2 CONC on at Henderson Creek was where the Clifford Jones home is now (1952). When the station got on fire, they put sacks over the horses heads to bring them through the front of the building which was on fire. After the station burned down, Garret Wolve
2 CONC rton carried the mail for some time also Benjamin Williams. When Albert E. Bowen was born (born October 31, 1875) and later became one of the twelve apostles, all of we children went over to the Bowen home to see the baby. Sister Bowen let each on
2 CONC e of we girls hold the baby. When Daniel Ward was drowned, (April 20, 1914) John Bowen a older brother of Albert who came to the funeral services, told me his father and mother worked for my father when they arrived in Utah, and were married at fat
2 CONC her's house. The old adobe house was still standing, used as a work shop, he said "you should go and see it," I never did. We always looked forward for the day W.H. Richards (who later was bishop, Stake President, and Patriarch of the Malad Stake
2 CONC ) came down our way in a horse drawn covered wagon selling merchandise, as calico, thread, pins, ribbon, etc. The first dime I earned gleaning wheat, (I thought that was a lot of money) Net, Ten and I bought some pink, blue and green ribbon from Pr
2 CONC esident Richards. The first calico dress mother made for me I was so proud of that. The summer of 1880 we girls had diphtheria, Asenath who was working out for other people came home sick. Mother had they boys moved their beds in the granary, whe
2 CONC n they came in to eat, we girls sat outside. We were kept away from every one all summer. Dr. W.H. Andersen who lived at East Portage, Utah gave Mother some medicine. He told us not to get it on our teeth. Most of the early settlers had some encoun
2 CONC ters with the Indians. Early one morning Aunt Jennet put the cream in the churn and left Heber to do the churning, she came to Mother&amp;rsquo;s house to have Mother help her with her sewing, she sent me to rince the butter take it out of the chur
2 CONC n. We were frightened when several Indians came riding up to the door, their bodies all painted wearing feather head gear. Heber gave them all the bread he could find. They were all talking, pale face, were the only words we could understand. Whe
2 CONC n they went galloping down the road, followed by several squaws riding on ponies with poles tied at the ponies side with one end dragging on the ground leaving a cloud of dust, the dust hadn&amp;rsquo;t settled when we were in Mother's house. Mothe
2 CONC r had taught us to give them something to eat. The first school, I remember was held at Aunt Jennet's house, just before Thanksgiving, Joe and Alice and their two children came to visit Mother, Joe (Joseph H. Pulley) taught for one month after Chri
2 CONC stmas they went home. Moroni Reese taught for a short time after the first of the year. (I think this was the winter of 1877- 1878 it was after the Bowen family had moved to Samaria, Idaho. Moroni Reese was living at the Bowen home.) The first scho
2 CONC ol I attended was held in a log house which my brother Hugh built west of the Benjamin and Agnes Williams home. (Leo Williams owns the property now 1952) (Henry Robins built a house south of Hugh's house he married Aunt Maria daughter Dorothy). M
2 CONC y first teacher was William Jardine at the end of the school term he gave us a apple, stick of candy, a Valentine cut from white paper put over colored paper, which showed a silhouette of a girl swinging. This I kept for several years. The winter J
2 CONC ames (Jim) Bush taught school, (1880-1881) we all had measles. Our friend Eddie Williams was sick, we and the Bradberry children went to see him not knowing he had measles. We and all the neighbors children had the measles, there was a January tha
2 CONC w and rain followed by a chinook wind. The roof of the house leaked. Mother put tubs, pans and what she could find to keep the beds dry. Finally the roof leaked in so many places Uncle Charley Clark (he and Ellen lived in a lean to he had built o
2 CONC n the north side of Mothers house) got a wagon cover, he and Mother couldn't get the cover nailed to rafters, Si got out of bed to help and fainted. I have heard Mother tell how her prayers were answered. That was a hard winter for all of us. I nev
2 CONC er spoke above a whisper all summer and that was the end of school for that year. Hannah Jardine taught we girls to sew, crochet, and knit, with the teaching Mother had taught us, I did my own sewing, Mother&amp;rsquo;s and helped my sisters sew fo
2 CONC r their families. I made the wedding dresses for sister Ten, Aunt Ann Harris Moon, Minnie Harris Chivers. (Royce&amp;rsquo;s Grandmother) Aunt Dora Prothero Moon, and helped to make Aunt Ten Bell&amp;rsquo;s and Jennet Stockflead Robertson's, and m
2 CONC y own which was cream cashmere trimmed with lace. Mother taught us to work and be good homemakers. I have did house work for other families washing, ironing, cooked the meals and took care of the children. Receiving fifty cents a week, when I wen
2 CONC t to work for Mrs. John Kelly who was living at Mound Springs, Utah, I received two dollars and fifty cents a week. I have helped Mother wash wool, card it in batts for quilts, spin the wool on a spinning wheel in little round balls. These we sen
2 CONC t to Brigham City, Utah where they were woven into cloth. I have made candles from beef and mutton tallow. Molding them in a candle mold father had brought from England. These furnished our light until we got a kerosene lamp and Mother warned us no
2 CONC t to touch the lamp as it was dangerous we may set the house on fire. After we moved on the east side of the road Christopher Gardner taught a Sunday School Class at Mother&amp;rsquo;s house teaching the Articles of Faith and scripture from the Bib
2 CONC le. He was a shoe cobbler, his wives had a carpet loom and we took our carpet rugs to them and they made it into carpet. He and his three wives are buried in the Cherry Creek Cemetery. In March of 1888 a pond was made in the T.A. Davis field west o
2 CONC f the road. I was baptized by Thomas A. Davis and confirmed by Davy Jones, sister Hortensia, Hanah Jones Peterson, Dinah Davis Jones and several others were baptized at that time. (The Davis family lived where his grandson Glen Price now lives 1952
2 CONC ). (Davy and Bessie Jones lived in a log house by the Spring they built the rock house where they were living when Si and Joe bought their place. The old rock house is still standing east of the new Hi Way 191.) Bill and Ten lived at the rock hous
2 CONC e for a few years Verna (Verna Bel Evans Earl) was born there. We have spent many afternoon visiting with Besse she told us Indian Stories, she would say "come down tomorrow and I will tell more Injun stories". In April 1893, Si took Mother and I t
2 CONC o Collingston, Utah, traveling in a small horse drawn buggy, from Collinston we went by train to Salt Lake City, Utah, where we went to the dedicatory ceremonies of the Salt Lake Temple, which was one of the most spiritual experiences of my life. P
2 CONC resident Wilford Woodruff dressed in a white suit. His hair and beard was white as snow dedicated the temple. Mother and I stayed at the home of Uncle Sam and Aunt Ann Wilkey, Aunt Ann Gregory Wilkey from Nephi, Utah, (Uncles John Wilkey's wife) st
2 CONC ayed at their home also. We stayed at Salt Lake a week, went to American Fork, where we visited with sister Alice and family, went to Provo, Utah and stayed with Janet K. Fowler for one week. Mother and Aunt Jane were the only members of the Kemmis
2 CONC h family who stayed in Utah and Idaho, the rest of the family returned to Harrison County, Iowa, (1858 or 1860). Mother never saw Grandfather or Grandmother after they left Utah, Uncle James, wife Leona (Leona Sellers) (Lyons) and son Harry came t
2 CONC o see Mother in 1912, or 1913. (Sarah Clark says about May 1914) I think it was the year Joe bought his Reo Automobile. He brought them and Mother to our house where they had dinner and spent the day visiting. They gave mother a wicker rocker. At M
2 CONC other's death the chair was given to Ellen. Grandfather Charles Kemmish born 11 May 1812, Portsmouth, Hampshire, England, died 1896, Persia, Iowa. His second wife name was Carrie. Grandmother Elizabeth Wilkey Kemmish born 21 November 1812, Portsmou
2 CONC th, Hampshire England, died January 18, 1881 at Harrison County Iowa, buried Valley View Cemetery, Iowa. They were the parents of 11 children, Sarrah Jane who lived 3 years, John who lived 12 years, they were buried in the Kingston(or Kingstown) Ch
2 CONC urch Yard, England. Mother born April 17, 1837; Jane born January 27, 1842; married Thomas Fowler; Peter Charles born February 8,1844, married Susan Lidgett; Mary born 1846 married a Allen; Daniel born July 15, 1848 married Nettie Shanks; James Eph
2 CONC riam born 1850, he died September 21, 1918, married Leona or Lyons Sellers; Eliza Golconda born February 17, 1853 she was born on the ship "Golconda" crossing the Atlantic Ocean, died August 21, 1900, at her home in Persia, Iowa, married C.H. Alle
2 CONC n they had one child Pearl who married L.G. Clark. (I have a obituary notice of Eliza death which was sent to Mother) There was two children born after they came to America, Ruth, and Rachel died 1860 and Ruth married Wallas Dinsmore. This Informat
2 CONC ion is from letters written to Mother from Uncle James, Dan and Aunt Eliza. On Wednesday, January 1, 1896, I was married to Jesse Nicholas Ward, Grandfather Ward performed the wedding ceremony. Which took place in the front room of Mother&amp;rsquo
2 CONC ;s two room house. (This old house the logs of the front room were moved from the west side of the road to the east side, they numbered each log as they rebuilt it, I think this was about 1880. Later a room was built on the back, the building is us
2 CONC ed as a work shop by my nephews Dale and Reed Moon and the present time 1952) That morning at 2 a.m. Joseph Harris died at Woodruff. We were married at 2 p.m. and Sarah Elizabeth Green (Sadie) was born at 2 a.m. the next morning. Mother had a dinne
2 CONC r and reception inviting close relatives and our closet neighbors. The food we served was cooked on a stove that was one of the first brought to the Salt Lake Valley, we had roast chicken with dressing, noodles, boiled sliced ham, mashed potatoes
2 CONC , cheese, pickles, jelly, apple, and gooseberry pies, current cake, white cake spread with currant jelly. (The receptions have changed in 56 years). Aunt Thoriza N. Nicholas made our wedding cake. During the afternoon Benjamin and Agnes Williams sa
2 CONC ng several songs, I can remember only one "Look at the Moon Above", Ralph Harding Sr. sang "Wounded and Cripple for Life," Tommie Davis sang "Give My Love to Nellie". They were all very good singers. The next day Grandmother Ward sent word for us t
2 CONC o come down to her house and wear my wedding dress as the children wanted to see it, when we arrived Uncle Moroni Ward opened the front door and said "come in this way" this was a surprise to me as the room was crowded with relatives and friends fo
2 CONC r another reception. Shortly after our marriage traveling in a horse drawn wagon to Brigham City, Utah, where we purchased at the Coop Store our furniture: a stove, 4 chairs, cupboard, flour bin, with the stove we got a large bread tin, a griddle
2 CONC , large and small frying pan and as a luxury a cruet set. We moved to the log house, which had been the pioneer home of Grandfather and Grandmother Ward from 1874 to 1893 at Woodruff, Oneida County, Idaho. March 25, Pa and I, Uncle Bill, Aunt Ten w
2 CONC ent to the Logan Temple where we received our endowments. The last of March we moved to the homestead which Pa had entered in May 1893 one year after Bannock Valley was open to settlers. Pa drove the cattle and horses, Little Tommie (Jesse&amp;rsqu
2 CONC o;s little brother who was nine years old) drove one wagon with the pigs, chickens, and some seed grain. I drove the wagon with our household goods. On the way we stopped at the Benjamin Waldron Store at Samaria, Idaho. We purchased food supplies t
2 CONC o last until fall. We camped at the Ireland Springs, arriving at the homestead the next night. Our first home at the homestead was a small log room with a dirt floor. In a few days we had our straw tick on the built to the wall bed, which was fiv
2 CONC e feet high. Under the bed another bed could be made on the ground, also used to store supplies. Grandfather Ward made our table and a wash stand, a wooden box nailed to the wall with a calico curtain hung at the front was our cupboard. Grandmothe
2 CONC r Ward gave Pa a stove they had bought in 1884. The summer and fall of 1893 they cut and hauled logs from Lake Canyon built the little log room, a barn for the horses, a cellar and a pole correl. There was snow on the ground every morning until th
2 CONC e first of May on May 29th Pa planted some corn, peas and potatoes. Aunt Eunice came out to the homestead the last of May, we milked 15 cows twice a day and made 100 cheese, Grandfather Ward came out July 1, sold the cows and calves to Lorenzo Evan
2 CONC s, a cow and her calf for $14.00. We were up in the morning when the morning stars was still shining and worked late doing the chores after dark. Pa cleared the land of sage brush and plowed most of the 320 acres with what he called an Oliver plo
2 CONC w or foot burner plow, drawn by two span of horses, guiding the plow with his hands. Taking around three hours to plow one acre of cleared sage brush land, five acres of stubble land could be plowed in a day. Pa has freighted wheat from the homeste
2 CONC ad at Arbon, Idaho, to Collinston, Utah, receiving 40 cents a bushel. The first wagon we had we borrowed $100.00 in $20.00 gold pieces from brother Si and Joe. It had a high spring seat, when we went visiting neighbors we traveled in this wagon fo
2 CONC r many years. Our nearest neighbors were the Anderson boys: John, Otto, Maggs, who lived about one mile northeast, George Arbon his son Joe, Ed and Ettie Davis and their two children Eddie and Sarah lived two miles north, John Bowen and family, Jam
2 CONC es Bailey and family had farms farther north. The Anderson boys came down and wanted me to make cheese and churn butter in a up and down churn. Joe and Ed came a few times during the summer. I had very few women visitors during the summers I spen
2 CONC t at the homestead. The fall of 1896 and summer of 1897 Pa cut and hauled logs from Knox Canyon and built a large log room, the land wasn't surveyed at that time the house was built on the school section. John Payne (Jim and Chance's father) sawe
2 CONC d out two doors and two windows. After the land was surveyed the house was moved one mile west. Dan Roberts helped Pa move the house more logs were added, making a two story house with a large bed room upstairs. The children have often called it "T
2 CONC he Battered Mansion", but it has survived the hard winds and deep snow that fell in Arbon Valley and four generations of our family have lived in the house, we lived there during the summers to 1920, when Elmer M. and Susan were married they live
2 CONC d there until he bought the Jerry Baker farm, when Elmer Vance (our eldest grandchild) and Edith were married they lived there. On May 28th 1949 Elmer M. and Elmer V. moved the old pioneer house down north of the Baker home, just about one fourth m
2 CONC ile north from where Pa built it in 1897. They have added more rooms making a modern farm home and I think it will be good for another 55 years. The spring of 1898 we arrived at the homestead late at night and found some sheep shearers were stayin
2 CONC g at the house. They had their beds made down on the floor, they were shearing sheep in the barn and corral, the man who owned the sheep gave us seven little goats. On May 23 Aunt Ida W. John died and Uncle Al Green came to Arbon to tell us of he
2 CONC r death. That night when we went to bed I felt I should leave the light burning in the window, but didn't. Not knowing the Valley to well he passed our place and went on to Ed Davis, he and Ed arrive about midnight telling us the sad news. When w
2 CONC e returned from Woodruff after the funeral services, Ella Clark (now Mrs. D.M. Green) came with us and stayed until we moved back to Woodruff that fall. Pa says "Ella was one of the best girls I knew, she could ride a horse as good as any man, sh
2 CONC e would go bring the horses into the corral while I did the milking." We always milked several cows. I made all the butter we used and made cheese for our own use and some to give away. When I was married my brother gave me a cow we called Snarater
2 CONC , a heifer Daisey and a yearling steer, pa had three cows Mell, Silk, and Red. By 1905 we had a small herd of cattle. We had many ups and downs in our life--yet we came through all right. In February 1901, Uncle Joe and Pa went to Ogden, Utah, to b
2 CONC uy a sheep camp. Pa had a severe pain in his side and could not eat. Uncle Joe put him in the camp took him to Aunt Thurza Nicholas at Willard. She called Dr. Taylor, he told them to put hot packs on his side. He continued to get no better. Dr. Tay
2 CONC lor took him to the General Hospital at Ogden, where Dr. R.S. Joyce operated for a ruptured appendix. Which had been ruptured for several days. He was in the hospital six weeks and very sick. After that sickness Pa was a firm believer in life insur
2 CONC ance. He had borrowed money from the Bank to pay the premium on his life insurance many times. He always said "a man should carry enough insurance to pay for his burial expenses and leave his family some money." We had some exciting times, when Mer
2 CONC l was two years old he went riding stick horse while I was preparing breakfast, when I couldn't find him my first thoughts we he had fallen in the well. Pa went down in the well three times when he came out the third he said he knew he wasn't in th
2 CONC e well. We started looking through he rye which was about 5 feet high, around noon we found him almost to the top of the field, he was lost, tired and crying. We were glad and thankful when we found him he didn't get to ride any more stick horses t
2 CONC hrough the rye. One spring after we moved out to the homestead a stranger called at breakfast time and ask if I would prepare him breakfast and a lunch of boiled eggs, we had bacon and eggs for our breakfast. He said he would pay me what I ask bu
2 CONC t didn't want any food that had salt on. He asked if he could sleep in the chair while I prepared his breakfast and lunch. He would sleep a few minutes get up go to the door and look around he seemed to be nervous. When Pa was ready to go to work h
2 CONC e asked the direction to Malad and started in that direction. Pa watched him until he went out of sight. The next morning before daylight I was awakened by the noise of someone drawing water at the squeaky well. I awoke Pa he got up dressed and wen
2 CONC t out to find the same stranger, he said the lighting and thunder was terrible in the hills and he had decided to go to Rockland. I prepared him more boiled eggs and he went north from our place. Pa didn't go to work. He decided the stranger was tr
2 CONC ying to locate a horse to ride. He brought the saddle and bridle to the house and turned the gentle horses in the field. He had two a colts he was breaking which he kept in the corral but was sure no one would ride away on them. We heard later tha
2 CONC t he called at the store. They told him the officers were looking for a man who had robbed the passenger and killed a man on a train. We never seen nor heard of the stranger again. The summer of 1905 Uncle Joe and Pa bought some meadow land from th
2 CONC e Rice Brothers at Sterling, Idaho. In the early fall Uncle Joe, Aunt Lizzie their children Joe Jr, Mary, Eunice, Pa, I, and our children LaVerne, Jesse, George M., Elmer M. and Ida, who was our baby two months old, made the trips in a wagon and sh
2 CONC eep camp, taking the Ward cattle there for the winter. I think we forded the Snake River a dozen times the water coming through the floor of the camp. The land is now a part of the American Falls Dam. (reservoir) 1906 Pa and Uncle Joe bought the ra
2 CONC nch at Birch Creek from Jedd Jones Sr. Uncle Joe and Aunt Lizzie and family moved from Woodruff to the ranch that summer. We moved to Arbon, Pa did the spring work and returned to Birch Creek to help with the hay, he has often remarked he would lik
2 CONC e to have a field of alfalfa like that now. I and the children stayed at Arbon milked the cows, made cheese and took care of the farm until he returned. November 1907 we bought a lot, one and one fourth acres from Thomas J. and Charlotte Adams Evan
2 CONC s at Malad, Idaho. Moving here in December, at a later date Pa purchased another lot of T.J. Evans lining on the west, on April 2, 1909 we bought a lot from Hugh J. and Sarah Palmer Evans north of the James J. Evans lot, later Pa bought the James J
