2 CONC e Westwood Moon--Uncle Henry's 2nd wife, Aunt Louise Mother) over we can have a nice visit." We stayed and had dinner at the log house where sister Hortense and I was born. We went to see father&amp;rsquo;s eldest brother William his 2nd wife Eliza
2 CONC beth and Father&amp;rsquo;s Sisters Aunt Hannah and Dorethy, when Aunt Hannah see brother Si she cried and said "you look just like my dear brother Hugh." They were living on a small farm and sold vegetables. (Uncle Williams 1st wife and her seve
2 CONC n sons went to California during the gold rush) Uncle Henry was born March 29, 1819. When Mother came to Henderson Creek, Oneida County, Idaho the spring of 1870 we lived in a little log house (second house) with a dirt roof and floor on the west s
2 CONC ide of the road. After father&amp;rsquo;s death on September 23, 1970, we moved to another log house (third house) by the Creek where the large trees are, on the west side of the road across the road from the Dale Moon home.(1956) Mother was livin
2 CONC g here when she was married to William T. Bell, June 16, 1872. Brother Bill was born here on April 7, 1873. I remember very well when an Indian came and stood in the only door and Sister Helen put sister Asenath out through the window, she ran to A
2 CONC unt Jennett's house on the east side of the road. Her husband Fred Stockflead came running back, with a black whip which the freighters used to drive their oxen. He popped the whip several times and ordered the Indian to leave and never come back
2 CONC , we never saw him again. I have never forgot how frightened Mother and we children were. I have been told those two log houses and the log house on the east side of the road where Aunt Jennett was living were the first buildings built on what wa
2 CONC s known as Henderson Creek, Oneida County, Idaho. About the year 1878 the boys moved this log house over on the east side of the road a short distance north east of Mother&amp;rsquo;s house that was built in 1905. We were living here when we girl
2 CONC s had diphtheria the year 1880 and in January 1881 when all the family had measles. The old dirt roof leaked like a sieve, there was a January thaw and Chinook wind. Mother has told many times of how sick we were and it was a hard winter for all o
2 CONC f us. Christopher Gardner held a Sunday School class her for a short time, teaching us the Articles of Faith and Verses from the Bible. All of these log houses were built by the early pioneer settles. My fourth house was about 1884, the boys Si, Lo
2 CONC rum and Joe built a large log room later adding a room at the back, the front of the house was covered with siding, it had a good roof and floor. I was married in the front room of this house. Mother and the boys lived here to the fall of 1905. I
2 CONC f you care to see it, it is still standing north of the home Si was living before his death, used as a work shop by my nephews Dale and Reed Moon. Aunt Nell Ward took this picture August 1954. Brother Bill and Aunt Ten lived here for some time, Wil
2 CONC l and Bessie Moon were living after their marriage. John G. Evans built this frame house for Mother the summer of 1905. Amos and Ted Jones laid the foundation and did the plastering. Harry Cottington did the painting and papering. There was two be
2 CONC d rooms, a large living room, small kitchen and a pantry where Mother kept a **** full of cookies and cinnamon cakes for her grandchildren. This was Mother&amp;rsquo;s home from the fall of 1905 to Saturday evening January 15, 1921 when she passe
2 CONC d away, Brother Si made his home here for about twenty five years until he went to Logan, Utah to live. Reed Moon and his family were living before they moved to Ashton, Idaho. (Log house number 5) This picture was taken in 1901 by a photographer w
2 CONC ho was traveling through the country in a covered wagon. Grandfather Ward bought this log house from Jimmy Howell at Mound Springs, Utah the year 1874, during the winter the building was moved to Woodruff where the Parry Ward garage is at the prese
2 CONC nt time (1954). Grandfather and Grandmother Ward lived here from 1875 to 1893, nine of their fourteen children were born here. They moved in their new two story house October 1893 where Delton Ward lives at the present time (1954). In February 189
2 CONC 6 Pa and I moved in the log house, LaVerne, Jesse L., George Merlin and Elmer Mosiah were born here. We lived here for 7 winters and a few months in the summers. Alf Atkinson the school teacher at Woodruff spent the winter of 1903-04 here. Uncle Th
2 CONC omas and Aunt Rose John and family spent several years here. The building is still in use as a feed granary at the Ellis Harris farm. We were living here when Pa and Uncle Joe went to Ogden, Utah to buy a sheep camp. Pa took sick, they took him t
2 CONC o Aunt Thurza Nicholas in Willard from there he was taken to the General Hospital in Ogden, Utah where Doctor R.S. Joyce and Doctor Taylor operated for a ruptured appendix. He was in the hospital for six weeks, this was in February 1901. One of th
2 CONC e nicest letters I ever received was from Pa's uncle Charles R. Ward of Almo, Idaho. When Pa was in the hospital, the faith, sympathy and kindness he expressed in that letter I will never forget. Another incident when LaVerne, Jesse and Merl had mu
2 CONC mps, I got up early went out to the house quietly so not to awake them and went to milk the cow. When I came hurrying back, LaVerne was sitting on the table she had eaten all the sour pickles from the bottle. Jesse was feeding Merl icicles, his nig
2 CONC ht gown soaking wet. I thought they would die of lockjaw and pneumonia. They suffered no ill effects. But I would not advise any one who has mumps to eat pickles. When Grandmother Ward and Mother were told they said: "your children never had mumps
2 CONC , they will get them later". They have been in school and around other members of the family and to this day they have not had mumps again. (The sixth house) The last of March 1896 we moved to the homestead which Pa had entered in 1893, one year af
2 CONC ter Bannock Valley was opened to settlers. This log house had a dirt floor and roof. They built it the summer of 1893 , also a cellar, pole corral and log stable for the horses. My memories of this small house was a colony of rats that moved in an
2 CONC d took over. When we moved out in the spring of 1897 we put every that was eatable on table under the tub, boiler and buckets with heavy weight on top. I never left LaVerne every time I stepped out of the house she was taken along. I was glad whe
2 CONC n fall came and we returned to Woodruff for the winter. (The seventh log house.) The fall of 1896 and summer of 1897 Pa cut and hauled the logs from Knox Canyon, built a large room, later when Dan Roberts came to Arbon he and Pa moved the house fro
2 CONC m the school section about a mile west, adding more logs making a room upstairs. Here we spent the summers for several years. Moving out in the early spring and staying until late fall. This pictures was taken July 27, 1947. (The eighth log house
2 CONC ) We made our home here from September 1903 during the winters to 1907 and the summer of 1905. Uncle Moroni Ward built this house in 1888-89 he and his family livied here one year and he was called back to work with Laminate people at Wasakkie, Uta
2 CONC h. Ida and Cless were born here. (The ninth log house) -- December 1907 we moved to Malad, in November Pa bought a lot and house from Thomas J. Evans and wife, this was built by his father William Evans there was 6 rooms 4 down and 2 upstairs. Char
2 CONC les Wilford was born here and Uncle Henry E. Thomas. (The tenth log house)-- 1908 and several other summers we spent at Birch Creek, living on the ranch Pa and Uncle Joe bought from Jedd Jones Sr. Uncle Joe and Aunt Lizze and family moved from Wood
2 CONC ruff to this ranch the summer of 1906. All of these 10 houses were built of logs. Uncle Moroni Ward and the William Evans homes were covered with siding which gave them a better look, After we moved in the Evans home we had new shingles put on th
2 CONC e roof, the outside painted white trimmed with green and the inside wool all pained, Will Stuart and Charles Peck Jr. did the painting. (Charles married Ida Holmes a sister to Dr. Turner&amp;rsquo;s wife Mae, they were school teachers from the east
2 CONC .) We lived in the Evans home for 12 years after we moved in the brick home. Hugh and Isabel Griffith moved in for the winter of 1920-21. Jesse and Delila moved here after they were married. Living here until they moved to the John O. Thomas ranch
2 CONC . Merl and Viola moved in June 1827 and lived until they moved into their new home. Neva stayed here while Cleland was overseas, Lucret and children stayed here when Tom was overseas, Bill and Dixie stayed here before they moved to Brigham City. Wh
2 CONC en I look back on the trial and troubles, the joy and sorrow that go in to 87 years of living. I have many blessing to be thankful for. I have at the present time 24 living grand children. and 44 living great grand children. Quin Ray Deshamps was b
2 CONC orn on the 84th birthday December 12, 1952. &amp;emsp;(The eleventh house) After 22 years of working hard and saving were financially able to build the brick house where I hope to spend the rest of my life. This house was started in August of 1918
2 CONC . The contractors were Dan K. Evans and Marshall McEntire, Ed Nelson did the painting, some of the painting of dinning, living, bed rooms are still in good condition. We moved in April 1919, using our old furniture. 1925 we bought a dining room set
2 CONC . Living room set. Each year we have added a few new things. December 23, 1940 we purchased as Zennith Radio from Lyle Thomas and Vance Vaughan, I have enjoyed this Radio. I could push a button and get the programs on the stations I wanted to hear
2 CONC , to date we have had few repair bill. We payed $60.00, receiving $20.00 credit on the radio we bought of D.P. Woodland April 1928.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
