1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;blockquote&gt;AFTER P.C. AND LYDIA WERE MARRIED, THEY LIVED IN KANESVILLE, NOW COUNCIL BLUFFS, WHERE HE SPENT TWO AND A HALF YEARSLEARNING THE WHEEL-RIGHT OR WAGON MAKERS TRADE AND CARPENTRY. HIS SALARY WAS $7.50 A W
2 CONC EEK. AFTER LEARNING HIS TRADE, HE STARTED OUT ON HIS OWN. HE MOVED HIS FAMILY TO TABOR, IOWA IN 1867 AND LIVED THERE UNTIL 1870, WHERE HE WORKED AT HIS TRADE. IN MARCH OF 1870 HE MOVED HIS FAMILY TO REEDERS MILL, NOW KNOWN AS HARD SCRATCH, HARRISO
2 CONC N CO., IOWA. THERE HE ALSO WORKED AT HIS TRADE FROM 1870 TO 1873. IN 1872 HE BOUGHT 80 ACRES OF LAND AT $2.00 PER ACRE NEAR THE CENTER OF UNION TWP., HARRISON CO., IOWA, AND MOVED TO THIS LOCATION IN 1873 AND STARTED FARMING.&lt;br&gt;HE BUILT A SM
2 CONC ALL HOUSE 14 FEET BY 16 FEET OF NATIVE BLACK WALNUT AND KEPT ADDING TO THE HOUSE AS THE FAMILY INCREASED AND CONTINUED TO PURCHASE MORE LAND UNTIL HE HAD 320 ACRES, ALL FENCED IN HOG TIGHT WIRE NETTING. HE OWNED THIS FARM UNTIL HIS DEATH ON APRIL 2
2 CONC 8, 1901. IN 1896 CHARLES RETIRED FROM FARMING AND MOVED TO WOODBINE, HARRISON CO., IOWA WHERE HE AND HIS SONS FRANK, NATHAN, AND CURTIS BUILT A BEAUTIFUL HOUSE ON NORMAL STREET, MOVING IN BEFORE THE HOUSE WAS COMPLETED. HE WORKED ON THE HOUSE FO
2 CONC R A YEAR OR TWO WHEN HE BECAME ILL. IN JANUARY 1901, IT WAS DISCOVERED THAT HE HAD DROPSY. IN MARCH HE WENT TO CALIFORNIA THINKING IT WOULD BE GOOD FOR HIM, BUT RETURNED AND DIED IN APRIL 28, 1901, AT HIS HOME IN WOODBINE, IOWA AT THE AGE OF 57. H
2 CONC E WAS BURIED AT VALLEY VIEW CEMETERY, PERSIA, HARRISON, CO., IOWA.&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;br&gt;http://awtc.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&amp;amp;db=:1602466&amp;amp;id=I0067</line></content></metadataxml>
