2 TITL Yates Coat of Arms
1 _OID f0b1bbd1-16ed-4192-b576-98be38da1d8e
1 _DSCR The surname of YATES was a locational name 'the dweller at the gates' one who had care of the road, or more simply one who lived on the road by the gate. Local names usually denoted that the man held land. Early records of the name mention Herewar
2 CONC d de Jette, 1198 Gloucestershire. Johannes atte Yate was listed in the Yorkshire Poll Tax of 1379. Thomas Wight married Ann Yeatman at St. George's, Hanover Square, London in 1758. John Yeates and Ann Davis werr married at St. George's, Hanover Squ
2 CONC are, London in the year 1753. The small villages of Europe, or royal and noble households, even large religious dwellings and monasteries gave rise to many family names, which reflected the occupation or profession of the original bearer of the nam
2 CONC e. Following the Crusades in Europe in the 11th 12th and 13th centuries a need was felt for an additional name. This was recognized by those of gentle birth, who realised that it added prestige and practical advantage to their status. At first th
2 CONC e coat of arms was a practical matter which served a function on the battlefield and in tournaments. With his helmet covering his face, and armour encasing the knight from head to foot, the only means of identification for his followers, was the in
2 CONC signia painted on his shield, and embroidered on his surcoat, the draped and flowing garment worn over the armour. The name is also spelt Yeates. The rise of surnames, according to the accepted theory, was due to the Norman Conquest of 1066 when Ol
2 CONC d English personal-names were rapidly superseded by the new christian names introduced by the Normans. Of these, only a few were really popular and in the 12th century this scarcity of christian names led to the increasing use of surnames to distin
2 CONC guish the numerous individuals of the same name. Some Normans had hereditary surnames before they came to England, but there is evidence that surnames would have developed in England even had there been no Norman Conquest. The development of the fe
2 CONC udal system made it essential that the king should know exactly what service each person owed. Payments to and by the exchequer required that debtors and creditors should be particularized, and it became official that each individual acquired exac
2 CONC t identification. The associated arms are recorded in Sir Bernard Burkes General Armory. Ulster King of Arms in 1884.
1 _META <metadataxml><cemetery /><transcription /></metadataxml>
