2 CONC tion and weakness.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The result is, that since some weeks, I have been obliged to get a friend to do my services; and if weather and my strength permit I shal
2 CONC l go over to England about the third or fourth of January, there to recruit and pass two or three months in tranquility, with my sister-in-law at Malvern.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;M
2 CONC y wife is still wonderfully active and energetic; my sons are growing up rapidly, and are all well looking, intelligent and honest young men.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt;The journey from this pla
2 CONC ce to England is very short, and would be pleasant were it not for the two hours crossing from Calais to Dover, and which some persons pronounce to be more fatiguing than all the voyage to America by the Atlantic.&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div&gt
