2 TITL Clarissa Ames Munjar Williams Pugsley Biography
1 _OID f17a34cd-3568-430c-b45e-e678e02500ad
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;The following was written by Clarissa&amp;#39;s daughter, Clarissa Pugsley Barlow:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', t
2 CONC imes" size="3"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Clarissa Ames Pugsley&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;b. 16 December 1827&amp;nbsp; Shoreham, Addison, Vermont&lt;br&gt;m (1): 1843&amp;nbsp; Nauvoo, Hancock, Illinois&lt;br&gt;m (2): 27 December 1850&amp;nbsp; Kanesville, I
2 CONC owa&lt;br&gt;m (3): 24 August 1855&amp;nbsp; Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah&lt;br&gt;d. 24 July 1910&amp;nbsp; Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah&lt;br&gt;b. 27 July 1910&amp;nbsp; Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah&lt;br&gt;Father: Ira Ames&lt;br&gt;Mo
2 CONC ther: Charity Carter&lt;br&gt;Husbands: (1) Thomas Munjar, (2) John D. Williams, (3) Philip Pugsley&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Clarissa Ames Pugsley, my mother, was born 16 December 1827 at Shoreham, Addison County, Vermont. Her father, Ira Ames
2 CONC , joined the Church in 1832, moving from place to place with the Saints. Her mother, Charity Carter, died from exposure caused through traveling when she was 12 years of age.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justif
2 CONC y"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;After the death of her mother, my mother wen
2 CONC t to live with Lydia K. Knight, who was the mother of Jesse Knight, taking care of the children while Mrs. Knight attended to her millinery shop.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times n
2 CONC ew roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;Mother&amp;rsquo;s people were neighbors to the Prophet Joseph Smith in Kir
2 CONC tland, Ohio, and she was baptized at the age of 8 years in the Mississippi River in the month of December, the ice having to be broken for her baptism.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'t
2 CONC imes new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;Her mother was a close companion to Emma Smith, the Prophet&amp;rsquo
2 CONC ;s wife, who gave her a bead necklace which she had made herself.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="
2 CONC color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;The Prophet Joseph was also a close friend of grandfather Ames and spent many evenings at their home, my mother then being a child but remembered having sa
2 CONC t on his knees many times.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="
2 CONC 'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;font color="#666666"&gt;Mother attended a school which was called the School of the Prophets, which convened at night, in which the Prophet Joseph was the teacher. At the age of 14 years she was taken ill
2 CONC , and having great faith in the gospel, went to her uncle&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font color="#808080"&gt;Jared Carter&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font color="#666666"&gt;&amp;nbsp;for a blessing, not knowing he had left the Church, not being able to stand t
2 CONC he persecutions.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"
2 CONC &gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;In 1843, at the age of 16, she married Thomas Munjar, having one child. After the death of her baby she left him as he failed to provide a living, and she returned to her father&amp;rsquo;s h
2 CONC ome in Ohio.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times" size="3"&gt;&lt;span style=
2 CONC "color: #666666; line-height: 19px"&gt;n December 1850, she married John D. Williams, and in the following spring came with him to Utah, locating in the 19th Ward. Mr. Williams died in February of 1852, just two months before the birth of their so
2 CONC n [Charles John Williams, 1852-1867]. In 1856, she married Philip Pugsley, who was my father. Like most pioneer mothers she could spin, card, weave, and knit. She belonged to the first Relief Society of the 19th Ward.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="li
2 CONC ne-height: 19px"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #666666; text-align: justify"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;span style="color: #666666; line-height: 19px"&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman'
2 CONC , times" size="3"&gt;Mother and Father had four children (Sarah, Mary, Clarissa and George). Father died August 7, 1903 at the age of 81 years. Mother died July 24, 1910 at the age of 83, they having been married 48 years.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&
2 CONC lt;div style="text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman', times"&gt;&lt;font size="3"&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 14px; text-align: justify"&gt;&lt;font face="'times new roman
2 CONC ', times"&gt;-Clarissa Pugsley Barlow, &amp;quot;Pioneer Mother and Father&amp;quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
