1 _OID fcb2ea25-c5a7-47d5-a867-8377abea57d9
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;After emigrating from England on the Golconda and surviving the arduous journey to Salt Lake City firstly by steamboat up the Missouri River to Keokuk, Iowa and then by Ox wagon Train, Charles then went to Goshe
2 CONC n, ninety miles south where he worked as a basket maker and brought wheat from the mormons to sell to the United States soldiers for $1 per bushel. This was frowned upon by the mormons. Charles wanted to leave the area and would have been in the te
2 CONC rrible Mountain Meadow Massacre had he been able to get flour for the trip.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;He&amp;nbsp;left Goshen&amp;nbsp;in 1859 after telling the Mormons that he was travelling to open up a farm locally. They stopped firstly in Pro
2 CONC vo Valley where they got ready for a trip across the plains that lasted 4 months. They went via St Joseph Mo to Fremont Co. Iowa where they stayed for 3 years then moved to Mills Co and then in autumn 1862 to Pottawatamie County, settling 5 miles e
2 CONC ast of Council Bluffs. A year later they moved into Council Bluffs and ran &amp;quot;Farmer&amp;#39;s Inn&amp;quot;. They stayed there for 3 months before leaving for Harrison Co in 1865. Here he brought a farm that was profitable and prosperous. H
2 CONC e was able to give his son James 40 acres of land.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
